const html: string = "<h2>Video Abstracts <span class=\"sp-count\">115</span></h2><p class=\"sp-note\">Submissions are grouped by presentation type. Each entry includes its abstract title, author names, institution, and abstract body.</p><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-1\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 1</p><h3>: Early Experience of Robot-Assisted Radical Prostatectomy in High-Risk Prostate Cancer After Neoadjuvant Therapy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Gautam VB, Agarwal MM, Bhaskar V, Gupta S, Shankar P, Agnihotri R, Rathore D</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Apollo Medics Super Speciality Hospitals</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction<br/>\nNeoadjuvant hormonal therapy before radical prostatectomy is increasingly being used in patients with high-risk disease. We present our early experience of robot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP) in these patients and report their early pathological, biochemical and functional outcomes.</p><p>Materials and Methods<br/>\nWe retrospectively reviewed 11 patients with high-risk/locally advanced prostate cancer who underwent RARP at our centre. Patient age, PSA, clinical stage, biopsy Grade Group, neoadjuvant treatment, final histopathology, postoperative PSA and urinary continence were assessed. Adverse pathological features including extraprostatic extension (EPE), seminal vesicle invasion (SVI), lymphovascular invasion (LVI) and lymph-node involvement were recorded.</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe median age was 65 years (range 61–76 years) and the median pre-treatment PSA was 29.55 ng/mL (range 5.3–64 ng/mL). Nine patients received neoadjuvant hormonal therapy (Relugolix, all 9, abiraterone 2). Surgical challenges after neoadjuvant therapy included fibrotic adhesions posterolaterally (n = 4), fusion of denonvillier fascia to perirectal fat (n = 1), apical fibrosis (n = 2), desmoplasia of perinodal tissue (n = 2). Final histopathology showed pT3 disease in 8 patients (72.7%) and lymph-node involvement in 1 patient (9.1%). EPE was seen in 8 patients (72.7%), SVI in 2 (18.2%), LVI in 2 (18.2%) and PNI in 9 (81.8%). Two patients had apical margin (+). Postoperative PSA at 1 month was 0.014 ng/ml (median). One patient had PSA persistence (PSA 0.45) and 2, PSA recurrence. PSMA PET CT was negative in all recurrences. At one month, all patients required two or fewer pads per day which was reduced to nil at 3 months in all but one patient who received adjuvant RT (1 pad/d).</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nOur initial experience suggests that RARP is feasible in select patients with high-risk/locally advanced prostate cancer, including those receiving neoadjuvant hormonal therapy. The neoadj therapy does pose few challenges during surgery which can be easily managed. Although early postoperative PSA results were encouraging, adverse pathological features were common. Longer follow-up and a larger number of patients are needed to assess the long-term oncological and functional outcomes of this approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-2\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 2</p><h3>Expanding the Boundaries of Robotic Reconstruction: Initial Experience with Complex Ureteric Reconstruction Using the CMR Versius Surgical System</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr B S Aulakh , Dr Amit Tuli , Dr Shikha Tiwari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Aykai Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Title: Expanding the Boundaries of Robotic Reconstruction: Initial Experience with Complex Ureteric Reconstruction Using the CMR Versius Surgical System</p><p>Introduction:<br/>\nRobotic surgery has transformed reconstructive urology by combining high-definition magnified visualization with enhanced instrument dexterity and precise intracorporeal suturing. However, its application to complex ureteric reconstruction remains technically demanding and relatively underreported, particularly with emerging robotic platforms. We present our initial experience of robotic reconstruction across a diverse spectrum of congenital and acquired ureteric pathologies using the CMR Versius Surgical System.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was performed of patients undergoing robotic ureteric reconstruction at our institution. The procedures included robotic ureteric reimplantation for vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) and primary megaureter, ureteroureterostomy/reimplantation for ureteric strictures, repair of ureterovaginal fistula, and complex ureteric reconstruction using an appendix interposition graft. Demographic characteristics, pathology, reconstructive technique, operative parameters, perioperative morbidity, hospital stay, stent-related outcomes and early postoperative functional results were assessed. Particular emphasis was placed on technical feasibility, tissue preservation, tension-free reconstruction and intracorporeal suturing.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nOur experience demonstrates the applicability of robotic surgery across increasingly complex reconstructive scenarios. Robotic reimplantation provided precise distal ureteric reconstruction in patients with VUR and megaureter, while strictures were managed according to their location and length using tailored ureteroureterostomy or ureteric reimplantation. Ureterovaginal fistula repair was accomplished with meticulous dissection, excision of the fistulous tract and tension-free ureteric reconstruction. In a complex reconstructive setting where native ureteric length was inadequate, an appendix interposition graft was successfully employed, highlighting the potential of a robotic approach even for bowel-based ureteric substitution. Across procedures, robotic magnification facilitated identification and preservation of periureteric vascularity, while articulated instrumentation enabled precise intracorporeal suturing in anatomically confined spaces. Early postoperative outcomes were favourable, with satisfactory drainage and preservation of the reconstructed urinary tract.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nThis series highlights the expanding role of robotic surgery in reconstructive urology, extending from conventional ureteric reimplantation to complex ureteric substitution. Our experience suggests that the CMR Versius platform can facilitate technically demanding reconstruction while adhering to established principles of open reconstructive surgery. Robotic reconstruction may represent an important evolution in the management of complex ureteric pathology, warranting further prospective evaluation and longer-term functional follow-up.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-3\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 3</p><h3>Laparoscopic bilateral ureteric reimplantation for bilateral vaginal ectopic ureters with a duplex left collecting system</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Prem Shankar, Dr Gyvi Gaurav</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Apollo medics superspeciality hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nTo present the laparoscopic management of bilateral vaginal ectopic ureters in an adolescent female with a duplex left collecting system, highlighting the intraoperative identification of complex ureteric anatomy and common-sheath reconstruction.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA 13-year-old female with bilateral ectopic ureters and a duplex left collecting system was evaluated with ultrasonography and CT urography. The left lower-moiety ureter drained into the bladder, while the upper-moiety ureter was ectopic. The right ureter was single and ectopic with vaginal insertion. Cystoscopy, vaginoscopy and fluoroscopy were followed by laparoscopic bilateral ureteric reimplantation.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nCystoscopy demonstrated a single left ureteric orifice. During vaginoscopy, an ectopic ureteric opening was encountered and cannulated with a guidewire; fluoroscopy confirmed it to be the right ureter. Laparoscopy demonstrated two left ureters sharing a common sheath. The uterus and ovaries were sequentially retracted using sutures passed through the anterior abdominal wall under laparoscopic vision, avoiding additional port placement. The two left ureters were spatulated along their common wall and were found to be naturally adherent, precluding the need for uretero-ureteric anastomosis. They were reimplanted into the bladder as a common-sheath unit. The right ectopic ureter was separately reimplanted. Three DJ stents were placed. The procedure was completed without complications. Foley catheter and DJ stents were removed at 14 days. At 3-month follow-up, there was no per-vaginal urinary leak, the vagina was adequate for age, and both kidneys were normal without hydroureteronephrosis.</p><p>Conclusions:<br/>\nBilateral vaginal ectopic ureters with a duplex collecting system present a complex reconstructive challenge. Cystoscopy, vaginoscopy, fluoroscopy and laparoscopic dissection facilitated anatomical delineation and reconstruction. Common-sheath laparoscopic reimplantation provided effective definitive treatment with satisfactory short-term functional and anatomical outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-4\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 4</p><h3>Robot assisted partial nephrectomy for a complex Hilar tumour</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Nilesh Taneja, Dr Mounika B, Prof Brusabhanu Nayak</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To demonstrate robot-assisted partial nephrectomy for a complex hilar tumour.</p><p>Methods: Presenting a case of 36 year gentleman with incidental detection of left renal mass. CECT showed 4.2 x 3.8 cm ball like enhancing solid lesion in left kidney arising from anterior part of mid pole cortex abutting the left renal vein and branch of renal artery. Patient underwent robot assisted partial nephrectomy using Da Vinci Xi robotic system with warm ischemia time of 30 minutes. Patient had an uneventful recovery and was discharged in stable condition on POD 3.</p><p>Conclusions: Robot assisted partial nephrectomy is a feasible procedure for complex hilar tumours in experienced hands. The guiding principles for surgery remain the same as with polar tumors, and achieving trifecta outcomes in challenging scenarios remains a priority.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-5\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 5</p><h3>Dorsal Urethral Diverticulum Masquerading as Recurrent Epispadias: A Diagnostic and Reconstructive Challenge — A Surgical Video</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Siddharth Sahu, Dr Naveen Bajiya, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS Medical College and Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Late urethral complications following epispadias repair are uncommon and may present with deceptive anatomy. We report an unusual case in which a chronic dorsal urethral diverticulum evolved into a urethrocutaneous fistula, clinically masquerading as recurrent epispadias.<br/>\nCase Description: An 18-year-old male presented with complete voiding through a 1-cm dorsal epispadiac meatus, 15 years after undergoing modified Mitchell repair for isolated epispadias at the age of three years. Preoperative examination revealed a dorsal epispadiac-appearing meatus along with a pin-point glanular/subcoronal ventral neo-meatus. The presence of dual meatal openings raised the suspicion of recurrent epispadias with a concomitant hypospadiac meatus. Intraoperatively, however, the ventral opening was identified as the original reconstructed neo-meatus, while the dorsal opening represented a urethrocutaneous fistula arising from a chronic dorsal urethral diverticulum along the previous urethral suture line. Progressive enlargement of the diverticulum had gradually diverted the urinary stream dorsally over several years.<br/>\nSurgical Technique and Outcome: The video demonstrates diverticulectomy, layered fistula closure with vascularised dartos flap interposition, and distal urethral reconstruction using Snodgrass (TIP) urethroplasty. Recovery was uneventful, with restoration of a single satisfactory urinary stream through the ventral neo-meatus and no fistula recurrence.<br/>\nConclusion: This rare delayed complication of epispadias repair can closely mimic recurrent epispadias. Careful clinical evaluation and recognition of the preserved ventral neo-meatus are crucial for accurate diagnosis and successful single-stage reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-6\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 6</p><h3>Sandwich Technique for Total Anatomical Reconstruction in Retzius-Sparing Robotic Prostatectomy: A SGPGI Modification for Early Continence</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shubham Singh, Dr Ashish Ranjan, Dr Arpan Yadav , Dr Sanjoy Sureka</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGIMS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Retzius-sparing robotic-assisted radical prostatectomy (RS-RARP) offers potential advantages in early urinary continence by preserving anterior supporting structures. However, disruption of the posterior urethral anatomy and the need for a stable vesicourethral anastomosis remain important considerations. We describe the SGPGI-modified “Sandwich technique,” incorporating total anatomical reconstruction to restore both anterior and posterior support of the bladder and urethra.</p><p>Technique: Following posterior urethral dissection, the technique reconstructs the anterior and posterior anatomical supports around the vesicourethral anastomosis. Perivesical and periurethral tissues are approximated to recreate the anterior hammock. Posterior reconstruction is performed using a reverse Rocco stitch, approximating the median fibrous raphe and Denonvilliers fascia. This provides circumferential dynamic support to the vesicourethral anastomosis, reduces anastomotic tension, and functionally increases the length of the striated urethral sphincteric complex.</p><p>Case: A 69-year-old man presented with voiding lower urinary tract symptoms. Evaluation revealed a 50-g prostate, bilateral palpable nodules on digital rectal examination, PSA of 8.70 ng/mL, and biopsy-proven adenocarcinoma with Gleason score 4+3=7. mpMRI demonstrated bilateral peripheral-zone lesions classified as PI-RADS IV. The patient underwent RS-RARP with the SGPGI Sandwich reconstruction.</p><p>Results: Operative time was 3 hours with an estimated blood loss of 150 mL. The drain was removed on postoperative day 2 and the urinary catheter on day 10. At follow-up at 15 days and 3 weeks, the patient achieved complete urinary continence with 0 wet pads/day. Histopathology demonstrated pT2c adenocarcinoma, Gleason 4+3=7 (Grade Group 3), with negative surgical margins, seminal vesicles, and bilateral pelvic lymph nodes.</p><p>Conclusion: The SGPGI Sandwich modification provides comprehensive anterior and posterior anatomical reconstruction during RS-RARP. By restoring periurethral support and enhancing anastomotic stability, this technique may facilitate early urinary continence while maintaining surgical feasibility and oncological adequacy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-7\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 7</p><h3>Point of Technique- Bladder Cuff excision in total Laparoscopic Radical Nephroureterectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kamal Sachdeva, Dr Swapan Sood, Dr Sanyam Nandwani</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PATEL HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim- We have demonstrated a point of Technique of Bladder Cuff excision in total Laparoscopic Radical Nephroureterectomy.</p><p>Method- We performed cystostomy after Laparoscopic Radical Nephroureterectomy and approx 2-3mm Bladder Cuff was excision under vision and then bladder was repair with absorbable sutures.</p><p>Conclusion- During total Laparoscopic Radical Nephroureterectomy, bladder cuff excision is critical step to achieve good oncological control of disease especially in distal ureter tumors.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-8\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 8</p><h3>From diagnosis to decision: Gender Assignment and Surgical intervention in Ovotesticular DSD (46 XY)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr A Sachan, Prof M.A Khan</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">UPUMS Saifai</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nOvotesticular disorder represents 10% of cases of disorder of sex development characterized by the presence of both ovarian and testicular tissue in the same individual, with karyotype 46 XY being a rare sex chromosomal abnormality.<br/>\nMaterials and methods:<br/>\nA 21-year-old person reared as male presented with complaint of left undescended testes with painful gynaecomastia and history of hypospadias repair at age of 2 year with no documents. On examination, right testes was small and atrophic with empty left scrotal sac and less developed secondary sexual characteristics. MRI was suggestive of left uterus and ovary with follicles. Karyotyping was suggestive of 46 XY .<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nUrethroscopy was performed which was suggestive of a normal tract going into bladder and another communicating tract at level of bulbar urethra going to vagina and uterus. Laparoscopic removal of uterus, ovaries and vagina was performed.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nOvotesticular disorder is diagnosed with a combination of tests including chromosome and genetic analysis, hormone testing, ultrasound or MRI. Considering the potential of fertility depending on the degree of gonadal differentiation and genital development for a preferred gender, the decision for particular gender rearing should be taken</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-9\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 9</p><h3>Robotic repair of Giant Arteriovenous Malformation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr A Kumar, Dr Alok, Dr Apoorva, Dr Arpan, Dr Sanjoy</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: To describe robotic-assisted repair of a giant renal arteriovenous malformation (AVM) in a young female, and evaluate feasibility and outcome of minimally invasive management.<br/>\nMethod: A 30-year-old female with 5 years of intermittent right flank pain and no prior surgical history underwent evaluation. Ultrasound showed a single cystic area (38 × 59 mm) in the right renal pelvis. CT renal angiography revealed a tortuous right renal artery with a 57 × 36 mm interpolar lesion showing arterial-phase filling and early venous opacification, and conventional angiography localized the sac to the anterior division of the right renal artery. The patient underwent robotic surgical excision of the AVM with ligation of the feeding vessels.<br/>\nResult: The excised specimen was sent for histopathology, which showed medial wall hypertrophy, myxoid degeneration, and fibrosis, with no evidence of thrombus. Follow-up imaging showed complete resolution with no residual aneurysmal dilatation.<br/>\nConclusion: Robotic minimally invasive repair is a safe and effective option for giant renal AVMs, offering reduced morbidity while achieving cure. Surgery is the treatment of choice for symptomatic AVMs, especially in women of childbearing age, while small AVMs (&lt;3 cm) may be managed conservatively with repeat non-operative treatments if needed. Bench surgery with autotransplantation remains a valuable adjunct when feeding-vessel dissection proves difficult.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-10\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 10</p><h3>Robot-Assisted Partial Nephrectomy for a Complex Renal Hilar Mass: Technical Insights and Institutional Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Varun H U, Dr Harshit Rewari, Dr Supradeep N, Dr Gagan Talwar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta, Gurgaon</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Robot-Assisted Partial Nephrectomy for a Complex Renal Hilar Mass: Technical Insights and Institutional Experience<br/>\nIntroduction<br/>\nRenal hilar tumors pose significant technical challenges for nephron-sparing surgery because of their proximity to major renal vessels and the pelvicalyceal system. Robotic-assisted partial nephrectomy (RAPN) offers enhanced visualization, dexterity, and precision for managing anatomically complex renal lesions. presenting video demonstration of technical approach to RAPN for a complex renal hilar mass.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods<br/>\nA 43-year-old male with recently diagnosed hypertension and diabetes was incidentally found to have a left renal mass. Contrast-enhanced CT and PET-CT demonstrated a 40 × 29 mm heterogeneously enhancing, lobulated mid-polar lesion indenting the underlying mid-polar calyces, with an accessory left renal artery supplying the lower pole. The tumor had a RENAL nephrometry score of [insert score], indicating increased anatomical complexity. Preoperative three-dimensional reconstruction was performed using Biocliq AI©.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nStandard robotic ports were placed and the da Vinci Xi Surgical System was docked. Following colonic mobilization, the renal artery and vein were individually dissected and controlled using laparoscopic bulldog clamps. Intraoperative ultrasonography was used to delineate tumor margins, and the planned resection margins were marked with diathermy. The tumor was excised using cold scissors until normal-appearing renal parenchyma was reached. The pelvicalyceal system was closed with 4-0 PDS sutures, followed by renorrhaphy using 3-0 V-Loc sutures and application of a Veriset hemostatic patch. Console time was 180 minutes, with an estimated blood loss of 50 mL. No significant intraoperative or postoperative complications occurred, The patient was discharged on postoperative day 2.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nRAPN is a feasible and effective nephron-sparing approach for complex renal hilar tumors, including those with accessory renal vasculature. Detailed preoperative imaging and three-dimensional reconstruction, meticulous hilar dissection, intraoperative ultrasonography, precise tumor excision, and structured renal reconstruction facilitate safe tumor removal while preserving renal parenchyma.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-11\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 11</p><h3>Robotic excision of large retrocaval paraganglioma: A case report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Shubham Patel,Dr Devendra Kumar sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">C.k.birla hospital (R.B.H), jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo describe the technique and outcome of robot-assisted excision of a large retrocaval paraganglioma, emphasizing intraoperative hemodynamic control in an anatomically vulnerable location.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 43-year-old woman presented with right flank pain and accelerated hypertension. CT angiography revealed a 52×34×46 mm infrahepatic retrocaval heterogeneous mass causing anterolateral displacement of the IVC, posteroinferior displacement of the left renal vein, and close abutment of the right aortic wall. Raised plasma normetanephrine confirmed extra-adrenal paraganglioma; 68Ga-DOTATOC PET-CT excluded other somatostatin receptor-expressing lesions. The patient was started on once-daily prazosin for preoperative alpha-blockade. Robot-assisted excision was performed using two 8 mm camera ports placed infraumbilically, two further 8 mm robotic ports placed laterally and cranially (8 cm apart), and a 5 mm assistant port, allowing enhanced visualization of feeding vessels and minimal tumor manipulation.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nOperative time was 78 minutes with estimated blood loss under 30 mL. No intraoperative hypertensive peak occurred. The drain was removed on postoperative day 1, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 2, normotensive and off antihypertensive medication. Histopathology confirmed paraganglioma with a classic Zellballen pattern, no capsular or vascular invasion, and no mitotic activity.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nRobot-assisted excision offers a safe and effective approach for paraganglioma in technically challenging retrocaval locations, enabling precise dissection, minimal blood loss, and hemodynamic stability without intraoperative catecholamine crisis.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-12\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 12</p><h3>Retroperitoneal Paragangliomas: Surgical Nuances – Tips and Tricks</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Deepak , Dr Aravind T.K, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Anup Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Vmmc and sjh</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Title: Retroperitoneal Paragangliomas: Surgical Nuances – Tips and Tricks<br/>\nAuthors: Dr Deepak , Dr Aravind T.K, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Anup Kumar<br/>\nPresenting Author: Dr Deepak Kharb, Senior Resident, Department of Urology and Renal Transplant<br/>\nInstitute: VMMC &amp; Safdarjung Hospital, Delhi<br/>\nSubmitting Category: Agra Urological Best Video Session 2 — Non-Robotic Urology Videos<br/>\nBackground: Retroperitoneal intra-abdominal paragangliomas are rare, hyper vascular neuroendocrine tumors originating from extra-adrenal chromaffin cells along the para-aortic sympathetic chain. Complete surgical resection represents the primary curative modality. However, the uncommon incidence, intimate proximity to major vascular structures and the intraoperative anaesthesia concerns makes it a formidable technical challenge. This video article demonstrates key surgical strategies for the safe, complete extirpation of these challenging retroperitoneal lesions<br/>\nMethods: We hereby describe the surgical nuances of operating retroperitoneal paragangliomas in the three most observed locations.<br/>\n1. Supra-hilar Para-aortic location<br/>\n2. Infra-hilar Para-aortic location<br/>\n3. Organ of Zuckerkandl Location (OOZ)<br/>\nWe aim to individual highlight the necessary preoperative evaluation and important intra-operative steps that deserve caution. Considering the rarity of presentation, we have included surgical clips that highlight the most essential steps such as, Concept of extensive abdominal quadrant mobilization, Tumor side first clipping of neovascularized vessels and Situations that demand extreme vigilance to identify and salvage essential structures such as Accessory renal vessels (Peri-Hilar Tumors) or Inferior Mesenteric artery, Duodeno-jejunal Flexure and Ureter (OOZ lesions). We have tried to encompass all steps that deserve attention to an operating urologist.<br/>\nConclusion: Successful extirpation of retroperitoneal paragangliomas relies on thorough preoperative medical optimization, meticulous anatomical imaging review, and strict adherence to certain surgical principles to achieve optimal outcomes via minimally invasive approaches. This video serves as an instructional guide detailing practical, site-specific operative strategies for the operating urologist navigating these complex retroperitoneal tumors.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-13\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 13</p><h3>Establishing a Robotic Focal ONE® HIFU Programme for Localised Prostate Cancer: Point of Technique, Early Hurdles and Initial North Indian Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Praanjal Gupta, Dr. Sachin Arakere Nataraj, Dr. Rajesh Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Sector-128, Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objective: Focal therapy ablates the MRI-visible index lesion while sparing continence and erectile structures. We describe our technique, case-selection protocol and operational learnings from launching a Focal ONE® robotic HIFU programme at Max Superspeciality Hospital, Noida.<br/>\nTechnique: A structured MDT protocol mandated a single PI-RADS 4–5 index lesion without extracapsular or seminal vesicle involvement, Grade Group 2 on MRI-targeted biopsy, prostate volume under 40 cc, and absence of dense calcification or rectal pathology on imaging. Under general anaesthesia in lithotomy after rectal preparation, mpMRI contours were elastically fused to real-time TRUS. A hemi-gland template with a 9 mm margin (7.6–9.0 cc) was planned, sparing urethra and external sphincter, with partial neurovascular bundle ablation only where the lesion abutted the capsule. Mean HIFU time was ~65 minutes. Initial cases were proctored, with telementored planning review. The technique is demonstrated in the video.<br/>\nResults: Five patients (age 61–79; PSA 5.4–17.6 ng/mL) were treated in June 2026 with 100% delivery of planned ablation, no Clavien-Dindo ≥II complications, day-one discharge and catheter removal on day third. Early hurdles included fusion registration drift with rectal gas or probe pressure, resolved by meticulous bowel preparation, gel degassing and repeat contouring before sonication; conservative catheter duration to pre-empt retention from apical oedema; and patient counselling on cost and follow-up burden. Operationally, a dedicated OT slot, standardised leaflets, referral pathway and a prospective eight-domain database proved essential. Follow-up comprises IPSS/uroflowmetry at four weeks, PSA at one three months and thereafter, IIEF-5 and mpMRI at six months, and protocol re-biopsy.<br/>\nConclusion: Strict selection, real-time fusion, telementoring and disciplined workflow allow safe, reproducible focal HIFU from the outset; oncological durability awaits longer follow-up.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-14\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 14</p><h3>Autologous Temporalis Fascia for Plaque Excision and Grafting in Peyronie’s Disease: Our Institutional Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Girraj P Sharma, Manish Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MGMCH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background<br/>\nPeyronie’s disease (PD) is characterized by fibrous tunical plaque formation causing penile curvature, deformity, and, in advanced cases, penile shortening. Plaque excision with grafting is an established option for men with stable disease, severe or complex curvature, and satisfactory erectile function. Autologous temporalis fascia is an attractive graft material owing to its pliability, tensile strength, biocompatibility, low donor-site morbidity, and cost-effectiveness.<br/>\nObjective<br/>\nTo demonstrate the surgical technique of plaque excision and autologous temporalis fascia grafting and evaluate perioperative safety, penile straightening, and early functional outcomes in patients with severe PD.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nThis video demonstrates the step-by-step technique in patients with stable PD, including penile degloving, neurovascular bundle mobilization, plaque excision, temporalis fascia harvesting through a limited scalp incision, graft tailoring and fixation, watertight closure, artificial erection testing, and wound closure. Perioperative outcomes, curvature correction, erectile function, complications, donor-site morbidity, and patient satisfaction were assessed during follow-up.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nFive patients with stable PD underwent plaque excision and autologous temporalis fascia grafting. Mean age was 55 years (range, 52–65), and mean preoperative curvature was 67.2° (range, 60–90°). At a mean follow-up of 6 months, complete correction or residual curvature &lt;15° was achieved in all patients. Penile length and erectile function were preserved, with no requirement for re-intervention. No major intraoperative or postoperative complications occurred. Donor-site morbidity was minimal, and all patients reported satisfactory cosmetic and functional outcomes.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nPlaque excision with autologous temporalis fascia grafting is a safe and effective reconstructive technique for severe PD, providing reliable correction of significant curvature while preserving penile length and erectile function. Its favorable handling characteristics, minimal donor-site morbidity, and encouraging early functional outcomes make temporalis fascia a valuable alternative graft material in appropriately selected patients. Larger studies with longer follow-up are warranted to confirm these findings.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-15\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 15</p><h3>''ONE KIDNEY , TWO SYSTEMS : A LAPAROSCOPIC SOLUTION TO A NON FUNCTIONAL MOIETY ''</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR. JANGVIR SINGH GREWAL</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MEDANTA GURUGRAM</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nDuplex collecting system is a relatively common congenital anomaly of the urinary tract. While many patients remain asymptomatic, a poorly functioning or non-functional moiety may present with obstruction, recurrent infection, ureteric dilatation or calculi. Heminephrectomy remains the definitive treatment in appropriately selected patients. Laparoscopic surgery, however, can be technically challenging because of anomalous vascular anatomy and the need to preserve the functioning renal moiety.</p><p>Case Presentation:<br/>\nA 30-year-old female was evaluated for a left-sided duplex collecting system. MRI of the whole abdomen and pelvis demonstrated a duplicated left renal collecting system with moderate dilatation of the upper moiety and its ureter, associated with significant parenchymal thinning of the upper pole, consistent with a non-functional upper moiety. A distal ureteric calculus measuring approximately 4 × 2 mm, with an adjacent tiny 1.3-mm calculus, was also noted approximately 3.1 cm distal to the crossing of the iliac vessels. The contralateral kidney showed a tiny 2-mm mid-pole calyceal calculus. In view of the non-functional upper moiety with associated ureteric dilatation and calculi, laparoscopic left upper-pole heminephrectomy was planned.</p><p>Surgical Technique:<br/>\nLaparoscopic left heminephrectomy was performed with meticulous identification of the duplicated renal anatomy. The vascular supply to the upper moiety was carefully delineated and controlled, followed by mobilization and excision of the non-functional moiety. The functioning lower moiety and its vascular supply were preserved. The duplicated ureter was dissected and managed appropriately. The procedure was completed laparoscopically without compromising the functioning renal unit.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic heminephrectomy is a safe and effective minimally invasive option for selected adults with a duplex kidney and a non-functional moiety. Precise preoperative anatomical assessment, careful hilar dissection and preservation of the functioning moiety are crucial for achieving optimal outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-16\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 16</p><h3>Robotic Tanagho Repair: Redefining Reconstruction for Complex Female Urethral Stricture</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kapil, Dr Niraj Kumar, Dr Aniket, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Pawan Vasudeva</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC and SJH, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIMS</p><p>To describe the feasibility and functional outcome of robot-assisted Tanagho repair in a patient with complex long-segment female urethral stricture associated with bladder neck stenosis, highlighting the application of robotic technology to challenging urethral reconstruction.</p><p>METHODS</p><p>A 50-year-old woman presented with progressive voiding lower urinary tract symptoms culminating in acute urinary retention, requiring suprapubic catheterization. Evaluation demonstrated a long-segment obliterative distal urethral stricture with bladder neck stenosis, associated with a trabeculated and diverticular bladder and bilateral hydroureteronephrosis. Robot-assisted laparoscopic Tanagho repair was performed. Following excision of the obliterated urethral segment, an anterior bladder wall flap was fashioned, tubularized over a Foley catheter and used for urethral reconstruction. Perioperative and early functional outcomes were assessed.</p><p>RESULTS</p><p>Intraoperatively, complete urethral obliteration with bladder neck stenosis was confirmed. Robotic assistance enabled precise dissection, flap creation and intracorporeal suturing during reconstruction. The postoperative course was uneventful. On follow-up, the patient demonstrated satisfactory spontaneous voiding. The robotic approach provided enhanced visualization and dexterity while allowing the principles of the established Tanagho bladder-flap reconstruction to be performed minimally invasively.</p><p>CONCLUSION</p><p>Complex female urethral stricture with bladder neck involvement represents a formidable reconstructive challenge. Robot-assisted Tanagho repair is a feasible minimally invasive adaptation of the conventional open technique, combining bladder-flap reconstruction with the precision and visualization of robotic surgery. In carefully selected patients, it offers a promising reconstructive option with satisfactory early functional outcomes. Further experience and long-term follow-up are required to establish its durability.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-17\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 17</p><h3>Reconstruction of a Complex Rectourethral Fistula with Obliterated Posterior Urethra After Anorectal Malformation Repair: Rectourethral Fistula Disconnection with Progressive Perineal Urethroplasty and Gracilis Muscle Interposition</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr MUK Sherwani, Dr A Kumar, Dr MS Jha, Dr KDP Guleria, Dr R Goel</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max superspecialty hospital, Saket, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objectives: Rectourethral fistula (RUF) following anorectal malformation repair is rare and challenging. Recurrent fistula associated with urethral obliteration and distorted pelvic anatomy presents significant reconstructive difficulties. We describe management of a complex recurrent RUF with obliterated bulbomembranous urethra in an adult with previous anorectal malformation repair.</p><p>Materials and Methods: An 18-year-old male with imperforate anus managed by neonatal colostomy and posterior sagittal anorectoplasty presented with urine passage per rectum and recurrent urinary tract infections. Previous interventions included bladder exploration, fistula repair, Mitrofanoff creation, and sigmoid colostomy. MCU and MR urography demonstrated a vesicorectal fistula, reduced bladder capacity, bulbomembranous urethral stricture, right vesicoureteral reflux, and bilateral hydroureteronephrosis. Endoscopy revealed a blind-ending bulbar urethra and large vesicorectal fistula. Repair was performed through a midline perineal approach. Following fistula dissection, rectal and bladder defects were closed separately, with gracilis muscle interposition. Urethral continuity was restored using progressive perineal urethroplasty.</p><p>Results: A 2 × 3 cm fistulous communication involving the posterior urethra, bladder neck, and rectum was identified, with complete obliteration of the proximal urethral lumen. Following fistula disconnection and layered closure, gracilis interposition and end-to-end urethral reconstruction were successfully performed. Urethral and Mitrofanoff catheters provided postoperative diversion. Erectile function was preserved, with SHIM scores of 23 preoperatively and 22 postoperatively. Pericatheter retrograde urethrogram demonstrated satisfactory urethral continuity without contrast extravasation. Subsequent uroflowmetry showed a satisfactory flow curve. The patient remained fully continent with satisfactory voiding during follow-up.</p><p>Conclusion: Complex recurrent RUF following anorectal malformation requires individualized reconstruction. Progressive perineal urethroplasty combined with gracilis muscle interposition can achieve successful fistula closure, restore urethral continuity, and preserve continence and erectile function.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-18\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 18</p><h3>Real-Time ExactVu™ Micro-Ultrasound-Guided Prostate Biopsy: Technical Workflow for a Comprehensive Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr HS talwar, Dr Sarvagya L, Dr Sudeep B, Dr Sachin AN, Dr Pankaj W, Dr RK Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital, Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo describe a standardized workflow for transperineal, transrectal, and MRI-fusion targeted prostate biopsy using the ExactVu™ micro-ultrasound platform.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nPatients undergoing prostate biopsy for elevated prostate-specific antigen, abnormal digital rectal examination, suspicious multiparametric MRI, or persistent clinical suspicion of prostate cancer were evaluated using the ExactVu™ EV29L 29-MHz transducer. Following informed consent and institutional anaesthesia and antibiotic protocols, the prostate was systematically examined from base to apex. The peripheral and transition zones, anterior prostate, apex, base, seminal vesicles, and prostatic capsule were assessed, with suspicious lesions assigned PRI-MUS scores.</p><p>For transrectal biopsy, the probe was positioned and stabilized, with the dedicated needle guide used for real-time targeted and systematic sampling. For transperineal biopsy, the patient was positioned in lithotomy and a sterile-covered probe with the dedicated needle guide was used. Suspicious PRI-MUS lesions were targeted under continuous micro-ultrasound visualization, followed by systematic sector-based sampling. For MRI-fusion biopsy, MRI targets were registered with the real-time micro-ultrasound image using FusionVu, followed by targeted sampling and systematic biopsy. Target location, PRI-MUS score, MRI correlation, and specimen identity were documented separately.</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe standardized workflow enabled systematic real-time assessment of prostate anatomy, identification and characterization of suspicious lesions, and continuous visualization of needle placement. Both transperineal and transrectal approaches allowed targeted and systematic sampling using the same micro-ultrasound platform. MRI-defined targets could be correlated with real-time micro-ultrasound using FusionVu, facilitating targeted biopsy while maintaining continuous needle visualization.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nExactVu provides a standardized and reproducible workflow for transperineal, transrectal, and MRI-fusion prostate biopsy. Real-time high-resolution imaging, structured PRI-MUS assessment, continuous needle visualization, targeted sampling, and systematic sector-based mapping allow a comprehensive approach to prostate biopsy.</p><p>Conflict of Interest: None</p><p>Keywords: ExactVu; micro-ultrasound; transperineal biopsy; transrectal biopsy; MRI fusion; PRI-MUS; prostate cancer</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-19\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 19</p><h3>Beyond Redo Pyeloplasty: Robotic Ureterocalicostomy for Recurrent PUJO with an Intrarenal Pelvis, Crossing Vessel and Multiple Stones</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rajat Upadhyay, Dr Rajesh Ahlawat, Dr Sachin Arakere Nataraj, Dr Harkirat Singh Talwar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital, Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo describe the technical nuances and perioperative outcomes of complex robotic ureterocalicostomy performed as a salvage procedure for recurrent ureteropelvic junction obstruction (UPJO) in a malrotated kidney with posterior hilar orientation following failed pyeloplasty..<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA young male with recurrent right-sided UPJO, secondary intrarenal calculi, and prior laparoscopic pyeloplasty (in Japan) presented with persistent flank pain and progressive hydronephrosis despite multiple stent exchanges. Preoperative imaging demonstrated a malrotated right kidney with a small, fibrotic pelvis, posterior hilum, and unfavourable anatomy for conventional redo pyeloplasty. Pre operatively evaluation with percutaneous nephrostomy and antergrade nephrostogram was done. A robotic approach with extensive adhesiolysis, identification and preservation of segmental vessels, and meticulous mobilisation of the ureter. The ureter was spatulated and anastomosed to a dependent lower-pole calyx under indocyanine green guidance, achieving a tension-free mucosa-to-mucosa ureterocalicostomy over an antegrade double-J stent.<br/>\nResult<br/>\nConsole time was 3 hours 25 minutes. Five calculi were retrieved from the inferior calyx. Postoperative recovery was uneventful apart from transient fever managed with culture-directed antibiotics (Pseudomonas-sensitive to colistin). Post operatively PCNL was performed and remaining calculi were removed. PCN and Foley catheters were removed sequentially, with satisfactory drain output and stable renal function (GFR 51 mL/min) at discharge. The patient was discharged in stable condition with a DJ stent in situ, scheduled for removal at 4–6 weeks and follow-up DTPA renal scan at 2 weeks.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nRobotic ureterocalicostomy is a feasible and effective salvage reconstructive option for recurrent PUJO with an unreconstructable pelvis, particularly in anatomically challenging kidneys with malrotation and posterior hilar orientation. Meticulous adhesiolysis, vascular assessment with ICG, and tension-free mucosa-to-mucosa anastomosis are key to achieving favorable outcomes in such complex redo cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-20\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 20</p><h3>Reclaiming Quality of Life: The Simultaneous IPP and AUS Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR GIRISH SHARMA, DR VIKAS AGARWAL, DR UDIT JAIN</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPERSPECIALITY HOSPITAL , SHALIMARBAGH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Severe stress urinary incontinence and erectile dysfunction are devastating clinical complications following robotic radical prostatectomy. While sequential implantation of the artificial urinary sphincter and inflatable penile prosthesis remains standard, simultaneous placement significantly reduces anesthetic exposure and accelerates total pelvic restoration. This video demonstrates a refined single incision synchronous approach, highlighting spatial management within compromised uro oncologic anatomy.<br/>\nMethods: We present a post prostatectomy patient undergoing simultaneous dual implantation. A single transverse upper scrotal incision provided excellent access to both the bulbar urethra and corpora cavernosa. Adhering to strict infection control, the procedure began with meticulous urethral dissection and placement of the AMS 800 cuff, ensuring urethral integrity prior to corporal dilation. The AMS 700 cylinders were subsequently introduced. To safely navigate dense pelvic scarring from prior robotic interventions, we utilized a high submuscular ectopic approach for both the reservoir and the pressure regulating balloon. We visually emphasize the strategic lateralized placement of both pumps within the dependent scrotum to prevent postoperative tubing crossover and guarantee optimal mechanical dexterity for the patient.<br/>\nResults: Total operative time was efficient, demonstrating minimal blood loss and no intraoperative complications such as urethrotomy or corporal crossover. The single incision surgical strategy expedited patient recovery, allowing hospital discharge within twenty four hours. At the six week postoperative clinical follow up, both prosthetic devices were successfully activated. The patient achieved complete continence, requiring zero absorbent pads daily, alongside reliable rigidity for sexual intercourse. The lateral scrotal pump arrangement proved intuitive for the patient, and no device infections, malfunctions, or erosions were observed.<br/>\nConclusion: Synchronous dual implantation provides a definitive, safe solution for comprehensive post prostatectomy urological reconstruction. Meticulous operative sequencing, ectopic reservoir positioning, and precise scrotal spatial management are absolutely critical to maximizing functional clinical outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-21\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 21</p><h3>Precision Beyond PCNL: Robotic Pyelolithotomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR GIRISH SHARMA, DR VIKAS AGARWAL, DR UDIT JAIN</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MAX SUPERSPECIALITY HOSPITAL, SHALIMARBAGH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Title of the Abstract: Precision Beyond PCNL: Robotic Pyelolithotomy</p><p>Introduction:<br/>\nStaghorn and large renal calculi present major therapeutic challenges due to high stone burden, complex calyceal anatomy, recurrent infection, and threatened renal function. Although percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL) is first-line for most large stones, Robotic Pyelolithotomy (RAPL) is a valuable complementary option when PCNL is limited by anatomy, prior failure, or concurrent pelvi ureteric junction (PUJ/UPJ) pathology. This video demonstrates RAPL’s role and technical pearls in managing a 5.2 cm renal stone.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nWe present a single transperitoneal, three arm robotic case performed by an experienced urologic robotic surgeon. Indications included very large stone burden, complex calyceal distribution, and suspected UPJ obstruction after prior endourologic attempts. Filmed steps: patient positioning and port placement, precise renal pelvis exposure, cold scissor pyelotomy, intact extraction of the principal stone, targeted flexible ureteroscopy and basket retrieval for residual calyceal fragments, on table inspection for residuals, and concurrent robotic pyeloplasty when indicated. The video annotates decision points, perioperative metrics (console time, estimated blood loss), and techniques to minimize parenchymal injury and septic complications.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe case demonstrates intact removal of a 5.2 cm stone with minimal blood loss, no major intraoperative complications, short hospitalization, and radiographic stone free status at 6 week non contrast CT. Key intraoperative advantages observed included direct visualization permitting complete extraction, avoidance of parenchymal transgression, and opportunity to perform simultaneous UPJ reconstruction—avoiding staged procedures.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRAPL offers several distinct benefits over PCNL in select scenarios: intact stone removal without fragmentation, higher single procedure stone free rates, true parenchyma sparing by entering through the renal pelvis, and capacity for simultaneous structural repair (e.g., pyeloplasty). For tertiary centers with robotic expertise, RAPL should be considered within a multidisciplinary algorithm for complex and staghorn calculi when PCNL is unsuitable or has failed.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-22\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 22</p><h3>Conquering the Aortocaval Space: Robot-Assisted Laparoscopic Excision of a Functionally Active Retroperitoneal Paraganglioma with Infrarenal Aortic Encasement and Simultaneous Bilateral Standard-Template Retroperitoneal Lymph Node Dissection</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aniket Anand, Dr Md Azfar, Dr Sanchit Rustagi, Dr Uday Pratap Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGI, LUCKNOW</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>INTRODUCTION AND AIMS:<br/>\nRetroperitoneal paragangliomas (RPPGs) are rare extra-adrenal neuroendocrine tumors with an annual incidence below 1 per 100,000 persons. Functionally active RPPGs with major-vessel encasement represent the most surgically demanding variant and are frequently considered a relative contraindication to minimally invasive resection. We describe a robot-assisted laparoscopic (RAL) excision of a large functionally active RPPG with infrarenal aortic encasement (&gt;180°), combined with concurrent bilateral standard-template retroperitoneal lymph node dissection (RPLND), in a 24-year-old female.</p><p>METHODS:<br/>\nA 24 year old female presented with episodic hypertension. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CECT) demonstrated an inter-aortocaval retroperitoneal mass (3.5×6.1×6.7 cm) encasing the infrarenal aorta (&gt;180°) and abutting the inferior vena cava (&lt;180°) with enlarged aortocaval and para-aortic lymph nodes. Biopsy confirmed paraganglioma (chromogranin-positive, synaptophysin-positive; Ki67 5%). Urine normetanephrine was markedly elevated at 2722.5 mcg/24h. Six weeks of preoperative alpha-adrenergic and calcium channel blockade was given. RAL paraganglioma excision with bilateral standard-template RPLND was performed under general anaesthesia.</p><p>RESULTS:<br/>\nIntraoperatively, a well-circumscribed mass extending from the inferior renal vessel border to the aortic bifurcation was identified with dense adhesions to the aortic wall but no gross vascular invasion. Complete resection with bilateral RPLND was achieved. Intra-operative spike in blood pressure was noted on handling of mass. Postoperative course was complicated by chylous ascites after drain removal on postoperative day 10, managed with percutaneous drainage and a fat-free diet with medium-chain triglyceride (MCT) supplementation. The patient was discharged in stable condition on postoperative day 21.</p><p>CONCLUSIONS:<br/>\nRAL excision of a functionally active retroperitoneal paraganglioma with aortic encasement, combined with simultaneous bilateral standard-template RPLND, is technically achievable at high-volume robotic centers with rigorous perioperative endocrine optimization. Meticulous dissection of tumor-vessel interfaces and adequate management of post-RPLND chylous ascites are pivotal to favorable outcomes. This case establishes an expanded frontier for robotic retroperitoneal oncological surgery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-23\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 23</p><h3>LAPAROSCOPIC ASSISTED PERCUTANEOUS NEPHROLITHOTOMY FOR LOWER CALYCEAL STONE IN ECTOPIC PELVIC KIDNEY</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Vikas Kumar Bansal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">YASHODA HOSPITAL &amp; RESEARCH CENTRE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>NO CONFLICT OF INTEREST TO DISCLOSE</p><p>Aims : The objective of this video was to demonstrate laparoscopic assisted percutaneous nephrolithotomy for a lower calyceal stone in an ectopic pelvic kidney.</p><p>Methods: A 63 year old gentleman presented at our hospital with complaints of high grade fever and chills and lower abdominal pain. He was found to have urinary tract infection with pyelonephritis and renal pelvic stone approx 18 mm in left ectopic pelvic kidney. He was initially managed with iv antibiotics and DJ stent placement and after 4 weeks he underwent laparoscopic assisted PCNL as RIRS scope was not negotiable due to severe angulation.</p><p>Result: The patient had an uneventful recovery and was discharged after two days. His stent was removed after 4 weeks and there was no immediate or delayed postoperative complication.</p><p>Conclusion: Laparoscopic assisted PCNL is an effective method to deal with ectopic kidney stones specially when RIRS is difficult or tricky. We have to be careful while dissceting the renal area due to the aberrant anatomy and vasculature. In a few cases even superior vesicle pedicle needs to be ligated and divided for appropriate bladder mobilistaion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-24\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 24</p><h3>Single-stage reconstruction of adult hypospadias with significant chordee using a tunica vaginalis flap</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Yogendra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nAdult hypospadias with a narrow urethral plate and significant chordee presents a complex reconstructive challenge. We demonstrate a single-stage reconstructive technique incorporating a tunica vaginalis flap as a vascularized intermediate layer for urethral protection.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nAn adult patient with hypospadias, a narrow urethral plate and significant chordee underwent single-stage reconstruction. Following penile degloving over Buck’s fascia, chordee correction was performed using dorsal midline plication and three ventral corporotomies. The urethral plate and urethra were dissected off the corporal bodies and reconstructed as the first urethral layer, followed by meatoplasty and spongioplasty. A right tunica vaginalis flap was harvested and used as a blanket wrap over the reconstructed urethra, providing an additional vascularized protective layer. Penile skin coverage was subsequently achieved using Byars’ flaps, with corrugated drain placement.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nAdequate penile straightening was achieved intraoperatively, permitting completion of a single-stage urethral reconstruction despite the narrow urethral plate and significant preoperative chordee. At 6-month follow-up, the meatus was located at the glanular tip, with satisfactory penile alignment and no residual chordee or torsion.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nSingle-stage reconstruction of complex adult hypospadias with significant chordee and a narrow urethral plate is feasible with meticulous tissue handling and layered urethral coverage. A tunica vaginalis flap provides a readily available, well-vascularized intermediate layer and may offer additional protection to the urethral repair in challenging adult hypospadias reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-25\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 25</p><h3>Male Epispadias: A Retrospective Analysis of 21 Cases – video presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ajay Gandhi, Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya, Dr. Gaurav Jain, Dr. Taranpreet Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nMale epispadias is a rare congenital anomaly of the urethra characterized by a dorsal urethral defect. It can occur in isolation or as part of the exstrophy-epispadias complex. Isolated male epispadias is exceedingly rare, with an estimated incidence of 1 in 117,000 males. Surgical correction is essential for anatomical restoration, urinary continence, and cosmetic outcomes.</p><p>Objectives:<br/>\nTo evaluate the outcomes and complications associated with the surgical management of male epispadias using the modified Cantwell-Ransley technique.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was conducted of 21 male patients with epispadias treated at our institution between September 2014 and December 2023. The cohort included 9 patients with post-exstrophy epispadias and 12 with isolated epispadias. Based on the anatomical location, 16 cases were penopubic, 4 penile, and 1 glanular. All patients underwent repair using the modified Cantwell-Ransley technique. Relevant postoperative complications and additional procedures during follow-up were recorded.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nAll 21 patients underwent successful epispadias repair. The most common complications were urethrocutaneous fistula in 3 cases (14.3%) and wound infection in 1 case (4.8%). Importantly, no patients developed urethral stricture, chordee, or glans necrosis. Five patients with post-exstrophy epispadias underwent subsequent bladder neck reconstruction and bilateral ureteric reimplantation to enhance continence.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nMale epispadias can be effectively corrected using the modified Cantwell-Ransley technique, with acceptable complication rates and good anatomical and functional outcomes. In post-exstrophy patients, epispadias repair contributes to increased bladder outlet resistance and improved bladder capacity, essential for successful bladder neck reconstruction. Familiarity with surgical nuances is crucial to minimize complications and preserve vital tissue structures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-26\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 26</p><h3>Endoscopic Management of an Iatrogenic Rectal Injury Encountered During Robot-Assisted Radical Prostatectomy: A Case Report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR. PRANAV RAJA YADAV , DR DEVENDRA SHARMA</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">RBH CK BIRLA HOSPITALS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Rectal injury is a rare yet formidable complication of robot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP), traditionally requiring open or laparoscopic re-exploration, primary multilayer repair, and frequently a protective fecal diversion. While surgical repair remains the standard of care, advanced therapeutic endoscopy offers a less invasive alternative by facilitating primary closure from the intraluminal side. We present a case detailing the successful entirely endoscopic management of an intraoperative rectal injury recognized during RARP.</p><p>Case Presentation: A patient undergoing RARP for localized prostate cancer experienced a small iatrogenic rectal wall defect during posterior dissection of the prostate apex. Recognizing the injury intraoperatively, the surgical team completed the robotic dissection and immediately transitioned to therapeutic flexible endoscopy. Inspection revealed a full-thickness rectal mucosal and muscular defect. To avoid secondary abdominal exploration or a diverting colostomy, primary endoscopic closure was elected<br/>\nResults: Postoperative recovery was uneventful. A contrast enema and follow-up sigmoidoscopy performed on postoperative day five demonstrated complete defect sealing without leakage, abscess formation, or fistula development. The patient resumed oral intake smoothly, was discharged in stable condition, and experienced no long-term bowel or urinary complications.</p><p>Conclusion: Endoscopic management is a safe, effective, and strictly minimally invasive strategy for select rectal injuries encountered during RARP. This approach successfully avoids the significant morbidity associated with secondary abdominal operations and fecal diversion while ensuring robust mucosal healing.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-27\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 27</p><h3>Surgical Management and Vascular Reconstruction in Primary Renal Vein Sarcoma: A Multidisciplinary Oncovascular Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Pranav Raja Yadav , Dr. Devendra Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">RBH CK BIRLA HOSPITALS</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Primary renal vein sarcoma is an exceptionally rare, aggressive malignancy that closely mimics advanced renal cell carcinoma with venous thrombus. Achieving a complete, negative-margin R0 resection remains the primary determinant of long-term survival.<br/>\nWe present our institutional experience involving a patient diagnosed with primary renal vein sarcoma. Preoperative contrast-enhanced CT angiography mapped the precise tumor boundaries, venous involvement, and collateral pathways. The surgical intervention involved a multidisciplinary oncovascular approach. Intraoperatively, after obtaining secure proximal and distal vascular control, we successfully excised the renal vein sarcoma en-bloc alongside radical nephrectomy. To restore venous return and prevent severe venous congestion or acute kidney injury, meticulous vascular reconstruction was performed. The defect was managed using tailored oncovascular techniques, successfully re-establishing unobstructed venous outflow<br/>\n.Results: The surgery was successfully completed without major intraoperative complications. Postoperative recovery was uneventful, with serial imaging confirming complete tumor excision R0 resection) and excellent patency of the reconstructed venous pathway. Follow-up renal function tests demonstrated stable glomerular filtration rates, validating the efficacy of vein preservation and reconstruction. Histopathological examination confirmed primary renal vein sarcoma with clear surgical margins.Conclusion: Primary renal vein sarcoma demands an aggressive surgical approach. Successful en-bloc tumor excision paired with precise vascular reconstruction is technically feasible, safe, and critical for optimizing oncological control while successfully preserving renal function and systemic venous drainage.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-28\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 28</p><h3>Redo laparoscopic no-touch excision of a biochemically active residual retrocaval pheochromocytoma in an adolescent with a duplicated suprarenal inferior vena cava</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ujjwal Das, Dr. Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr. Goutam Ram Choudhary</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo demonstrate a functional imaging-guided strategy for redo transperitoneal laparoscopic excision of a biochemically active residual retrocaval pheochromocytoma in an adolescent with a duplicated suprarenal inferior vena cava (IVC) showing how medial renal rotation and devascularisation-first, no-touch dissection convert a hostile re-operative retrocaval corridor into a working field.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 13-year-old boy with sustained hypertension, hypertensive retinopathy and catecholamine-induced left ventricular hypertrophy had grossly elevated 24-hour urinary normetanephrine (5454; reference 67–503). CT and I-131-MIBG showed an avid right suprarenal mass with retrocaval extension. Open right adrenalectomy (2023; laparoscopic-converted to open) removed a 5×7 cm tumour (PASS 5/20) and revealed a duplicated suprarenal IVC uniting at the renal hilum. Serial MIBG, PET (SUVmax 15.2) and MRI mapped a persistent 1.2×2.3×2.4 cm functional residuum (plasma normetanephrine 622 pg/mL) wedged between the IVC, right diaphragmatic crus and right renal artery. After six weeks of structured alpha–beta blockade, redo transperitoneal laparoscopic WLE was undertaken in the left lateral position under invasive monitoring.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nMedial rotation of the fully mobilised kidney opened the retrohilar corridor, delivering the 3×3 cm encapsulated lesion precisely at the MIBG-avid focus. Early clip-ligation of feeding vessels preceded en bloc excision; the right renal vessels and ureter was preserved. Operative time was 3.5 hours; blood loss 100 mL; no conversion or haemodynamic crisis. Oral intake resumed on day 0; the patient was discharged normotensive (130/88 mmHg) on day 2. Histopathology confirmed pheochromocytoma (5 mitoses/2 mm²) without necrosis, angioinvasion, lymphatic or capsular invasion; four nodes were negative.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nIn redo pheochromocytoma surgery the first operative step is pharmacological and the second is anatomical: structured alpha–beta blockade, functional imaging road-mapping, vigilance for caval anomalies, and rotation-based exposure with devascularisation-first, no-touch dissection render laparoscopic re-excision of retrocaval residua feasible, safe and oncologically sound — even in an adolescent.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-29\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 29</p><h3>Robotic Transvesical Excision of Functional Urinary Bladder Paraganglioma: An Ultra-Minimally Invasive Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Devanshu Sharma, Shubham Singh, Ashish Ranjan, Uday Pratap Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sanjay Gandhi Post Graduate Institute of Medical Sciences</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nUrinary bladder paragangliomas are exceptionally rare functional tumours arising from chromaffin tissue and may present with catecholamine-mediated symptoms, particularly during micturition. Surgical excision is challenging because tumour manipulation can precipitate severe haemodynamic instability, while its proximity to the ureter and pelvic structures increases the risk of conventional surgical approaches. We describe a novel robotic transvesical approach for excision of a bladder paraganglioma.</p><p>Case presentation:<br/>\nA 60-year-old male presented with recurrent episodes of palpitations, headache and syncope associated with marked elevation of blood pressure during micturition. Evaluation demonstrated a urinary bladder paraganglioma in close proximity to the right ureter. Following appropriate preoperative multidisciplinary optimisation and anaesthetic preparation, robotic excision was planned.</p><p>Surgical technique:<br/>\nA multi-port da Vinci Xi robotic system was utilised. A transvesical approach was adopted, providing direct intravesical access to the tumour. The lesion was precisely localised and dissected under magnified three-dimensional robotic vision while maintaining careful haemodynamic control during tumour manipulation. The tumour was completely excised while avoiding extensive extravesical dissection and potential injury to the adjacent ureter.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe procedure was successfully completed robotically without conversion. The transvesical route avoided abdominal entry, pneumoperitoneum and extensive dissection around the urinary bladder and ureter. Intraoperative blood pressure fluctuations were carefully managed by the multidisciplinary anaesthesia team. Histopathological examination confirmed paraganglioma. The patient had an uneventful postoperative recovery with satisfactory clinical outcome.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRobotic transvesical excision represents a novel ultra-minimally invasive approach for selected bladder paragangliomas. Direct intravesical access provides excellent visualisation and precise tumour dissection while avoiding extensive perivesical dissection and potentially reducing the risk of ureteric injury. This technique may expand the role of robotic surgery in the management of rare functional bladder tumours.</p><p>Keywords: Bladder paraganglioma; Robotic surgery; Transvesical approach; Functional tumour; Pheochromocytoma; Minimally invasive surgery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-30\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 30</p><h3>Robotic Ileovaginoplasty with Posterior Perineal Skin Flap Vaginoplasty in MRKH Syndrome with Solitary Ectopic Pelvic Kidney and Intestinal Malrotation-video</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR Alok kumar,DR Aniket,DR Anupam shukla,DR MS Ansari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SANJAY GANDHI POSTGRADUATE INSTITUTE OF MEDICAL SCIENCES</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo describe the surgical technique and early perioperative outcomes of robotic ileo-vaginoplasty with posterior perineal skin flap vaginoplasty in a patient with Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser (MRKH) syndrome associated with complex congenital urogenital anomalies, including unilateral renal agenesis and a solitary ectopic pelvic kidney.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods</p><p>A 31-year-old unmarried female with primary amenorrhea and MRKH syndrome (46,XX karyotype) underwent comprehensive clinical, radiological, and endoscopic evaluation. Imaging demonstrated left renal agenesis with a solitary ectopic right pelvic kidney of preserved function. Examination under anesthesia and cysto-panendoscopy confirmed complete vaginal agenesis, a normal urethra and bladder, and a single right ureteric orifice. Robotic exploration revealed malrotated bowel with left-sided ileocecal junction and appendix, absent uterus, bilateral cystic ovaries, rudimentary uterine horn-like structures, and an anterior ectopic pelvic kidney. Robotic ileovaginoplasty using an ileal segment with posterior perineal skin flap vaginoplasty was subsequently performed.</p><p>Results</p><p>The procedure was completed successfully without intraoperative complications despite complex pelvic anatomy. The postoperative recovery was uneventful. Vaginal packing and rectal tube were removed on postoperative day (POD) 3, oral feeding was initiated on POD 4, the urethral catheter was removed on POD 6, and the pelvic drain was removed on POD 7. The patient was discharged in stable condition with preserved renal function and advised regular vaginal dilatation using St. Mark’s dilators to maintain neovaginal patency.</p><p>Conclusion</p><p>Robotic ileovaginoplasty with posterior perineal skin flap vaginoplasty is a safe and effective reconstructive option for patients with MRKH syndrome, even in the presence of complex congenital renal and intestinal anomalies. Superior visualization and instrument dexterity offered by the robotic platform facilitate meticulous pelvic dissection, enabling successful neovaginal reconstruction with favorable early postoperative outcomes. Long-term follow-up is required to evaluate functional, anatomical, and sexual outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-31\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 31</p><h3>Right Partial Adrenalectomy for Functional Adrenal Mass</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR UMESH SHARMA</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ABVIMS &amp; DR RMLH, NEW DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nAdrenalectomy is the standard surgical treatment for functional adrenal masses. Laparoscopic surgery provides excellent visualization, precise dissection, and vascular control through a minimally invasive approach. We present the technique of laparoscopic partial adrenalectomy for a functional right adrenal mass.<br/>\nMaterial and Method<br/>\nA 35-year-old male presented with uncontrolled hypertension. Contrast-enhanced CT (CECT) of the abdomen revealed a well-defined right adrenal mass measuring 3.5 × 3 × 2.5 cm.<br/>\nFunctional evaluation demonstrated elevated serum cortisol levels with normal urinary catecholamines. The low-dose dexamethasone suppression test was positive, suggestive of a cortisol-secreting functional adrenal adenoma.<br/>\nLaparoscopic partial adrenalectomy with excision of the adrenal mass was performed. The procedure allowed clear identification of the adrenal gland and surrounding vascular structures, with precise dissection and preservation of the uninvolved adrenal tissue.<br/>\nHistopathological examination revealed an adrenal adenoma composed predominantly of cells resembling the zona fasciculata.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nLaparoscopic partial adrenalectomy is a feasible and minimally invasive approach for selected functional adrenal masses. It provides excellent magnified visualization and precise surgical control, facilitating complete excision of the lesion while preserving normal adrenal tissue.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-32\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 32</p><h3>Bilateral Robotic Videoendoscopic Inguinal Lymphadenectomy (R-VEIL) for Carcinoma Penis Following Partial Penectomy — A Technique Video</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Shubham Patel,Dr.Devendra Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">c.k.birla hospital (R.B.H)Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Inguinal lymphadenectomy remains a critical step in the management of carcinoma penis with intermediate to high-risk primary tumours, given its direct impact on regional control and survival. Conventional open ilioinguinal lymphadenectomy is associated with significant wound-related morbidity, including skin necrosis, lymphocele, and infection. Robotic videoendoscopic inguinal lymphadenectomy (R-VEIL) offers comparable oncological clearance with markedly reduced wound complications.<br/>\nObjective: To demonstrate the surgical technique and perioperative outcomes of bilateral R-VEIL in a case of HPV-associated carcinoma penis (pT3) following partial penectomy.<br/>\nCase/Technique: A 50-year-old male, previously treated with partial penectomy elsewhere, presented for further management. Metastatic workup (2D echo, MRI pelvis, viral markers) was unremarkable. He underwent bilateral robotic-assisted videoendoscopic inguinal lymphadenectomy under general anaesthesia. The video demonstrates key steps: frog-leg positioning, femoral triangle marking, balloon dissection to create the supra-fascial working space, robotic port placement in triangulation, systematic dissection of the fat-lymph node packet from lateral border of sartorius to medial border of adductor longus, saphenous vein preservation, and specimen retrieval via endobag. The procedure was performed sequentially on both sides with meticulous hemostasis and drain placement.<br/>\nResults: The procedure was completed successfully with no intraoperative complications. Postoperative course was uneventful; catheter was removed on day 2, and the patient was discharged in stable condition on regular follow-up with bilateral drains.<br/>\nConclusion: R-VEIL is a safe, reproducible, and minimally invasive alternative to open inguinal lymphadenectomy in carcinoma penis, offering excellent visualization, preserved oncological principles, and reduced wound morbidity.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-33\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 33</p><h3>Youssef's Syndrome Following Cesarean Section: A Case Report of Robotic Utero-Vesical Fistula Repair with Omental Interposition</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR Alok kumar,DR Apoorva saxena,DR Arpan yadav,,DR Sanjoy Sureka</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SANJAY GANDHI POSTGRADUATE INSTITUTE OF MEDICAL SCIENCES</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Utero-vesical fistula (UVF), known as Youssef's syndrome, is a rare urogenital fistula that most commonly arises as delayed complication of lower-segment cesarean section (LSCS). It classically presents with cyclical hematuria , amenorrhea, or urinary incontinence due to abnormal communication between the uterine cavity and the urinary bladder. Diagnosis and appropriate surgical management are essential to prevent long-term morbidity.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: We report the case of 34-year-old married woman, para 1, with a known history of hypothyroidism, who presented with cyclical hematuria and passage of amorphous clots following LSCS performed in May 2025. Diagnostic workup included ultrasonography and contrast-enhanced MRI (CEMRI) of the pelvis, followed by cystoscopy and hysteroscopy for confirmation. She was initially trialed on conservative hormonal management before proceeding to definitive surgical repair via a robotic approach with omental (sigmoid colon appendices epiploicae) interposition.<br/>\nResults: Ultrasonography revealed an ill-defined lesion at the LSCS scar site involving the adjacent bladder wall. CEMRI confirmed a small vesico-uterine fistula (~9 mm length, 6 mm thickness) communicating the posterior bladder wall with the endometrial cavity. Conservative management with Danazol provided temporary symptomatic relief for approximately nine months before recurrence prompted surgical intervention. Cystoscopy and hysteroscopy confirmed a 3×3 cm fistulous defect between the posterior bladder wall and anterior uterine wall. Robotic UVF repair with omental interposition was performed ; intraoperative findings included dense omental and perivesical adhesions. The postoperative course was uneventful. The patient was discharged in stable condition with a per-urethral catheter in situ for two weeks, along with appropriate medical advice .<br/>\nConclusions: This case highlights UVF as rare but clinically significant cause of cyclical hematuria following cesarean delivery. A combination of cross-sectional imaging and endoscopic evaluation enables accurate diagnosis, while minimally invasive robotic repair with tissue interposition offers a safe and effective treatment option with favorable short-term outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-34\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 34</p><h3>Robotic assisted recurrent retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma resection with reconstruction of left renal vein</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">dr.shubham H. Patel/dr.Devendra Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">c.k.birla hospital (R.B.H),Jaipur.</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Robotic assisted recurrent retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma resection with reconstruction of left renal vein<br/>\nIntroduction- This video is to report on the initial clinical experience of robot-assisted recurrent retroperitoneal leiomyosarcoma resection with reconstruction of left renal vein.<br/>\nMethods- The patient was a 44-year-old female with Left flank pain. She was referred to our hospital and found to be with a retroperitoneal mass (23 X 12 X 10 mm) anterior to left renal vein at its confluence with gonadal and lumbar vein. The distal part of the left renal vein was slightly compressed by the tumor. The operation was performed via five ports, robot assisted, transperitoneal laparoscopic approach. The tumor was completely resected with the wall of left renal vein and left renal vein primarily reconstructed.<br/>\nResults- Patient was discharged on day 3 with good flow in renal doppler study post operatively. Both kidneys had adequate perfusion with almost equal split function on follow up DTPA renal scan.<br/>\nConclusion- Use of robotic technique can make removal of retroperitoneal tumors amenable to minimally invasive resection in a safe and effective manner. Recurrence shall be managed with excision of adjoining venous wall.<br/>\nVideo link- https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KIe_c9hXIlNE9-sXNCJ6IcXL0XvhHYbo/view?t=7</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-35\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 35</p><h3>SGPGI Robotic-Assisted Supine Extraperitoneal RPLND in Post-Chemotherapy NSGCT (RASE-RPLND)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR MOHD AZFAR , DR ANIKET ANAND , DR ARPAN YADAV , DR SANJOY SUREKA</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Sanjay Gandhi Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and objectives:<br/>\nRobotic-assisted retroperitoneal lymph node dissection (RPLND) offers a minimally invasive option<br/>\nfor patients with residual retroperitoneal disease following chemotherapy for testicular cancer. We<br/>\ndescribe the technical details of robotic-assisted supine extraperitoneal RPLND in a patient with<br/>\nT2N2M0S0 non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT) following three cycles of BEP<br/>\nchemotherapy.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nA 30-year-old male with post-chemotherapy residual para-aortic lymph node mass (4x3 cm)<br/>\nunderwent robotic-assisted supine extraperitoneal RPLND. The patient was positioned supine with<br/>\n30 degrees flank elevation. A balloon-dissector was used to develop the extraperitoneal space,<br/>\nfollowed by strategic robotic port placement. The paracaval, interaortocaval lymph nodes were<br/>\nremoved based on right suprahilar template guidelines. Postganglionic sympathetic efferent fibers<br/>\nwere preserved.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nOperative time was 220 minutes, blood loss was 230 mL, and no major intraoperative complications.<br/>\nThe patient’s recovery was uneventful and was discharged on postoperative day 6 following oral<br/>\nintake and mobilization on postoperative day one.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nThe nerve-sparing Robotic assisted supine extra peritoneal RPLND approach in a post-chemotherapy<br/>\ncontext demonstrates superior outcomes due to minimal bowel handling, effective management of<br/>\nintra-operative challenges, and quick postoperative recovery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-36\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 36</p><h3>Integrating Prophylactic Lympho-Venous Anastomosis into Robotic VEIL: A Novel Approach to Prevent Lymphedema in Penile Cancer Management</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Aayush</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta- The Medicity, Gurugram, Haryana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction-<br/>\nInguinal lymph node dissection (ILND) remains cornerstone of regional management in penile cancer but associated with significant morbidity. Although robotic video endoscopic inguinal lymphadenectomy (VEIL) reduces wound-related complications, lymphoedema remains significant concern affecting quality of life. Borrowing from concept of immediate lymphatic reconstruction in breast cancer surgery, we demonstrate a novel single-stage approach combining robotic VEIL with prophylactic Lympho-Venous anastomosis (LVA).</p><p>Materials and Methods-<br/>\nA 70-year-old man with recurrent squamous cell carcinoma of penile stump and bilateral metabolically active inguinal lymph nodes underwent revision of penile stump with bilateral robotic VEIL and immediate LVA. Following completion of superficial and deep inguinal lymph node dissection, with preservation of great saphenous vein, 0.1 mL of indocyanine green (ICG) was injected into first and fourth web spaces of foot, with additional injection near medial malleolus when required. Infrared fluorescence imaging was used to identify functional lymphatic channels, and suitable subcutaneous venules were selected. End-to-end lymphovenous anastomosis was performed under 10–15× microscopic magnification using 11-0 nylon sutures</p><p>Results-<br/>\nBilateral robotic VEIL was completed in 4 hours, with bilateral LVA requiring additional 2.25 hours. Uneventful postoperative course, and patient was ambulatory on POD1. A single Grade I complication (fever) resolved with conservative management, and drains were removed on POD20. Histopathology confirmed squamous cell carcinoma of penile stump, with no metastatic involvement identified in any of the 15 excised lymph nodes. At 3 months, patient remained disease-free, with no clinical evidence of lymphoedema and no requirement for compression garments.</p><p>Conclusion-<br/>\nProphylactic LVA following robotic VEIL is technically feasible and can be safely integrated into a single-stage minimally invasive approach. Although this approach increases operative time and current evidence remains limited, it represents a promising strategy for prevention of lymphoedema following inguinal lymph node dissection and warrants further prospective evaluation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-37\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 37</p><h3>Bladder Neck Preserving Laparoscopic Bladder Paraganglioma Resection in a Patient with a Synchronous Retrocaval Paraganglioma: Surgical Nuances and Genetics Behind a Rare Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Abhay Garg , Dr.Madhur Anand , Dr.Sanjay P Dhangar , Dr.Ketan Vartak , Dr.Sachin Patil , Dr.Vasishta Martha</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">BHARATI VIDYAPEETH DEEMED UNIVERSITY MEDICAL COLLEGE , PUNE</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo describe a young woman with hereditary paraganglioma–pheochromocytoma (PGL/PCC) syndrome due to a germline SDHA mutation who presented with a synchronous non-secretory bladder paraganglioma and a functioning retrocaval paraganglioma. The case report also highlights the genotype-phenotype correlation that should prompt germline SDH testing whenever a bladder paraganglioma is diagnosed.<br/>\nCase Presentation<br/>\nA woman in her early 30s with a 10-year history of hypertension and a 2-month history of diabetes mellitus presented with a 1-month history of gross haematuria with clot passage and dysuria. Abdominal examination was unremarkable. Ga-68 DOTANOC PET-CT showed a non-somatostatin-receptor (SSTR) avid polypoidal lesion at the bladder base (25 × 30 × 38 mm) and a separate SSTR avid heterogeneous right suprarenal-region mass (62 × 33 × 53 mm). Biochemistry showed a markedly elevated plasma normetanephrine (299 pg/mL) with a normal metanephrine and a normal aldosterone-renin ratio and cortisol indicating a noradrenergic secretory profile localised to the retrocaval lesion. The retrocaval paraganglioma was excised first with intraoperative blood pressure surges corroborated its catecholamine secreting nature and histopathology/immunohistochemistry confirmed paraganglioma. The patient then underwent laparoscopic bladder neck–preserving partial cystectomy for the bladder lesion which arose from the anterior bladder wall just proximal to but sparing the bladder neck. Immunohistochemistry of the bladder lesion confirmed bladder paraganglioma, distinguishing it from a primary neuroendocrine (carcinoid-type) bladder tumour. Germline genetic testing identified an SDHA mutation consistent with autosomal-dominant hereditary PGL/PCC syndrome. No conflict of interest to disclose.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nSynchronous bladder and retrocaval paraganglioma is an extremely rare presentation of hereditary SDHA-related PGL/PCC syndrome and should be actively considered whenever a bladder tumour coexists with longstanding disproportionately severe hypertension in a young patient. Every confirmed case of bladder paraganglioma should undergo germline SDH testing, given the implications for lifelong surveillance, family screening and recognition of further metachronous or synchronous lesions.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-38\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 38</p><h3>Lateral-First Bladder Neck Dissection During Laparoscopic-Assisted Radical Prostatectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR UMESH SHARMA</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">ABVIMS &amp; DR RMLH, NEW DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nBladder neck dissection is a critical step during laparoscopic-assisted radical prostatectomy. A bladder neck-preserving technique may facilitate early recovery of urinary continence and minimise urinary leakage. We describe a lateral-first approach to bladder neck dissection aimed at precise identification and preservation of the bladder neck.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods:<br/>\nA 78-year-old man with diabetes mellitus presented with lower urinary tract symptoms and an elevated serum PSA of 13.5 ng/mL. Multiparametric MRI of the prostate demonstrated a PIRADS 5 lesion in the peripheral zone. Targeted biopsy along with a 12-core systematic biopsy was performed. Acinar adenocarcinoma was identified in 9 of 12 systematic cores and 2 of 4 targeted cores, with a Gleason score of 4+5.<br/>\nLaparoscopic-assisted radical prostatectomy was planned. During bladder neck dissection, the lateral planes were developed initially on both sides before opening the bladder neck. This lateral-first dissection helped define the bladder neck anatomy and enabled a controlled, limited bladder opening. The remaining steps of the radical prostatectomy were completed in the standard manner.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nThe lateral-first approach to bladder neck dissection allows better anatomical delineation, minimises unnecessary bladder opening, and facilitates preservation of the bladder neck. This technique may contribute to reduced urinary leakage and earlier recovery of urinary continence following laparoscopic-assisted radical prostatectomy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-39\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 39</p><h3>Navigating the Anomaly: Robot-Assisted Radical Nephrectomy and Level II IVC Thrombectomy for a Right Renal Mass in Crossed Fused Renal Ectopia</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Uday P Singh, Dr Snigdh Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGI Lucnow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction &amp; Objective:Crossed fused renal ectopia (CFRE) is a rare congenital anomaly, with an estimated incidence of 1 in 2,000 to 1 in 7,500 live births. Surgical intervention in these patients presents a formidable challenge due to highly unpredictable and complex vascular anatomy, the presence of a level II inferior vena cava (IVC) thrombus, and the need to transect the fused parenchymal isthmus.<br/>\nMethods:We present the case of a 70-year-old male with hematuria who was found to have a 9.8 x 7.6 x 11.4 cm heterogeneously enhancing mass in the right renal moiety. Preoperative computed tomography (CT) with 3D vascular reconstruction confirmed a Left-to-Right crossed fused renal ectopia with the mass localized to the right unit, further complicated by complex vascularity and a level II IVC thrombus. Steps<br/>\n1.Meticulous bowel mobilization and exposure of the retroperitoneum.<br/>\n2.Identification, dissection, and individual ligation of the anomalous, complex feeding arteries and veins supplying the tumor-bearing right moiety.<br/>\n3.Vascular control and robotic extraction of the level II IVC thrombus.<br/>\n4.Precise parenchymal transection at the fusion line using robotic shears.<br/>\nResults: The Da Vinci Xi robot-assisted radical nephrectomy of the right moiety and IVC thrombectomy were completed successfully without open conversion. The console was 274 minutes, and the estimated blood loss was 150 mL. The level II IVC thrombus was successfully extracted robotically with a warm ischemia time of 22 minutes. Final histopathological examination revealed a pT3c stage Clear Cell renal cell carcinoma with negative surgical margins.<br/>\nConclusion:Robot-assisted radical nephrectomy and thrombectomy for a tumor-bearing moiety with a level II IVC thrombus in crossed fused renal ectopia is safe and highly feasible. The 3D magnified vision and articulating EndoWrist technology of the Da Vinci Xi robotic platform provide a distinct advantage in navigating the complex vascularity, safely extracting the thrombus, and executing precise parenchymal transection.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-40\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 40</p><h3>Redefining \"En Bloc\": Contiguous Lymphadenectomy in Radical Nephroureterectomy for UTUC</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Uday P Singh, Dr Snigdh Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SGPGI Lucknow</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Radical nephroureterectomy (RNU) with bladder cuff excision is the gold standard for high-risk upper tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC). While template-based regional lymph node dissection (LND) is crucial for accurate staging and guiding adjuvant therapy, it is traditionally performed as a separate procedural step. This standard approach disrupts the intervening lymphatic channels. We present a novel robot-assisted laparoscopic technique achieving a true en bloc extraction of the entire nephroureterectomy specimen, bladder cuff, and regional lymph nodes in a single, contiguous tissue block to maximize strict oncological containment.<br/>\nMethods: We present a video demonstration of a robot-assisted laparoscopic RNU in a 60-year-old female patient with high-risk UTUC of the Left Renal Pelvis and Ureter. Da Vinci Xi Robot was used in maintaining anatomical continuity between the medial aspect of Gerota’s fascia and the adventitia of the great vessels. The distal ureter and bladder cuff were managed via a completely intracorporeal extravesical approach. The entire specimen encompassing the kidney, ureter, bladder cuff, and regional lymphatics was extracted intact.<br/>\nResults: The contiguous en bloc resection was completed successfully utilizing the robotic platform. The console time was 170 minutes with an estimated blood loss of 80 mL. Pathological evaluation confirmed an intact monobloc specimen, ensuring zero transection of the primary lymphatic drainage pathways. Final histopathology revealed a pT3N2 high-grade urothelial carcinoma with negative margins. The en bloc extraction yielded a total of 15 lymph nodes, allowing for precise and undisturbed pathological mapping.<br/>\nConclusion: Extracting the regional lymph nodes en bloc with the primary RNU and bladder cuff specimen is a technically demanding but highly feasible modification using a robot-assisted laparoscopic approach. This \"no-touch\" contiguous technique strictly adheres to the oncological principles by preventing the disruption of lymphatic channels, theoretically minimizing retroperitoneal spillage and providing an optimal specimen for pathological evaluation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-41\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 41</p><h3>Pushing the Boundaries of Nephron Sparing: Pure Off-Clamp Robotic Excision of a Parahilar Mass</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shubhankar Avasthi, Dr Sachin A Nataraj, Dr Harkirat S Talwar,Dr Rajesh Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super-speciality Hospital , Noida - 128</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nWhile pure off-clamp robot-assisted partial nephrectomy (RAPN) eliminates ischemic injury and optimizes renal functional preservation, its application in complex, parahilar tumors remains highly challenging due to the risk of significant hemorrhage. This video presentation aims to demonstrate the safety, feasibility, and technical nuances of a pure off-clamp RAPN for a complex cystic parahilar renal mass.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA patient presented with a 4.6 cm Bosniak IV cystic lesion in the right lower pole. Cross-sectional imaging demonstrated an exophytic, predominantly cystic mass with enhancing septations extending medially toward the renal sinus, causing mass effect on the renal pelvis. Split right renal function was 48.2% (GFR 28.7 mL/min). A right RAPN was planned. Following ureteric catheterization, the right colon was medialized and the kidney mobilized. The hilum (two arteries, single vein) was dissected and looped for safety without clamping. Following intraoperative ultrasound mapping, an off-clamp enucleation-resection was performed. The pelvicalyceal system was primarily repaired (4-0 PDS), and a Veriset hemostatic patch was utilized to achieve bed hemostasis.</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe procedure was completed successfully with a console time of approximately 120 minutes and an estimated blood loss of 100 mL. By utilizing a pure off-clamp technique, the warm ischemia time was zero. The postoperative course was uneventful, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 3. Final histopathology revealed a 4 cm Clear Cell Renal Cell Carcinoma (Grade 2, pT1aNx) with negative surgical margins and no sarcomatoid or rhabdoid differentiation.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nPure off-clamp RAPN for parahilar and centrally extending tumors pushes the boundaries of nephron-sparing surgery. Leveraging robotic precision for highly controlled enucleation and robust hemostatic agents allows surgeons to achieve excellent oncological outcomes and zero ischemia time, maximizing functional preservation even in anatomically complex scenarios.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-42\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 42</p><h3>Reduction clitoroplasty- dorsal and ventral approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Ujwal Kumar, Dr Sameer Trivedi, Dr Yashasvi Singh, Dr Lalit Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">IMS,BHU, Varanasi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>• Clinical Challenge: Managing clitoromegaly requires reducing erectile tissue without destroying sensory nerves.<br/>\n• Objective: To demonstrate the cosmetic outcome, nerve preservation of dorsal and ventral reduction clitoroplasty.<br/>\nSurgical Approaches<br/>\n• Dorsal Approach: Employs a dorsal approach, directly visualizes and isolates the dorsal neurovascular bundle; allows precise reduction of the clitoral shaft and glans but carries a risk of nerve handling trauma if dissection is extensive.<br/>\n• Ventral Approach: Initiates dissection from the ventral aspect; leaves the main neurovascular supply attached to the underlying dorsal fascia (Buck's fascia); minimizes direct manipulation of the high-density dorsal nerve fibers.<br/>\nComparison of Outcomes<br/>\n• Sensation: Both methods aim to be nerve-sparing, though ventral techniques theoretically reduce direct instrument contact with primary dorsal sensory trunks.<br/>\n• Aesthetics: Both achieve high cosmetic satisfaction, symmetrical reduction, and appropriate anatomical recession.<br/>\n• Safety: Ventral dissection limits lateral and dorsal nerve injury risks by operating away from the primary neural traffic concentrated on the dorsal surface.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\n• Both dorsal and ventral nerve-sparing clitoroplasty provide safe, effective cosmetic and functional correction of clitoromegaly. The ventral approach offers a distinct anatomical advantage in safeguarding the primary dorsal neurovascular bundle by avoiding direct dorsal dissection.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-43\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 43</p><h3>Navigating Robotic-Assisted Kidney Transplantation: Surgical Technique and Early Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shailesh C Sahay, Dr Pawan Kesarwani, Dr Prashant Shevach, Dr Shivam Chaudharyy</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Patparganj, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Navigating Robotic-Assisted Kidney Transplantation: Surgical Technique and Early Experience<br/>\nPresenter: Dr. Shailesh Chandra Sahay<br/>\nSenior Director, Urology, Max Super Speciality Hospital, Patparganj, Delhi<br/>\nAims and Objectives<br/>\nRobotic-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) is a well-established approach to kidney transplantation. We describe the surgical steps to make it easier for beginners to understand and initiate this procedure.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods<br/>\nThe index patient was a 65-year-old gentleman with a history of kidney failure who had been on hemodialysis for 3 years. He was diabetic, with a BMI of 29 kg/m² and a body weight of 72 kg. He had no previous history of surgery. He was planned for RAKT, and the procedure was completed successfully, with a total operative duration of 2 hours and 40 minutes. The total anastomotic time and cold ischemia time were 70 minutes. Urine output started immediately after anastomosis. Total blood loss was negligible.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nHis serum creatinine decreased from 4.6 mg/dL to 1.8 mg/dL within 48 hours and subsequently to 0.8 mg/dL on postoperative day 2. The abdominal drain was removed on postoperative day 3, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 5.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nRobotic-assisted kidney transplantation is a promising approach. It offers the advantages of reduced bleeding and fewer wound-related complications due to the use of a small incision.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-44\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 44</p><h3>Female Epispadias: A Series of 7 Cases – video presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Taranpreet Singh, Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Gaurav Jain</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nFemale epispadias is an extremely rare congenital anomaly, with an estimated incidence of 1 in 484,000 females. It is characterized by urinary incontinence and distinct anatomical abnormalities, including bifid clitoris, flattened mons, a glabrous skin area, poorly formed labia minora, and pubic diastasis. Associated urinary tract anomalies such as vesicoureteric reflux (VUR) and a small-capacity bladder are common.</p><p>Objectives:<br/>\nTo present the surgical approach, anatomical reconstruction, and continence outcomes in a series of female epispadias cases managed at our center.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis was conducted of 7 female epispadias patients treated between September 2014 and January 2023. Among them, 6 cases were sub-symphyseal and 1 was retro-symphyseal. The surgical procedure involved a diamond-shaped incision marking, followed by excision of the glabrous, hairless skin over the mons. The patulous urethra was excised and urethral reconstruction was performed to restore continence. Genitoplasty and layered closure were carried out to restore normal anatomy.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nOf the 7 patients, 5 achieved complete urinary continence, while 2 attained social continence (dry for several hours, especially during the day). None of the patients experienced significant postoperative complications. Continence was observed to gradually improve over time, with some patients achieving optimal outcomes up to two years post-surgery.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nSurgical repair in female epispadias aims to achieve urinary continence, preserve upper tract function, and reconstruct cosmetically acceptable and functional genitalia. A structured surgical approach provides favorable outcomes, with continence improving progressively postoperatively. In selected cases with persistent incontinence or associated VUR, adjunctive procedures such as ureteric reimplantation or bladder neck reconstruction may be required.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-45\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 45</p><h3>Post-hysterectomy Bilateral Long-Segment Mid and Lower Ureteric Strictures Managed with Ileal Ureter in Reverse-L Configuration: A Case Report (Video Presentation)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. I.D. Charan, Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya, Dr. Yogendra, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Iatrogenic ureteric injury is a recognized complication of gynecological surgery and may present late as complex, long-segment ureteric strictures. Bilateral involvement poses a major reconstructive challenge, particularly when the strictures are not amenable to endoscopic or standard ureteric reimplantation procedures.<br/>\nCase Presentation: A 43-year-old woman presented with bilateral hydroureteronephrosis and acute kidney injury (serum creatinine 5.18 mg/dL) eight months after abdominal hysterectomy for a benign pathology. Initial urinary diversion with bilateral DJ stenting followed by right percutaneous nephrostomy resulted in recovery of renal function (serum creatinine 0.8 mg/dL). CT urography demonstrated bilateral hydroureteronephrosis secondary to long mid-ureteric strictures. Bilateral retrograde pyelography confirmed approximately 10–11 cm strictures involving the mid and lower ureters.<br/>\nSurgical Management: As the strictures were unsuitable for endoscopic management or standard ureteric reimplantation, definitive reconstruction was performed using approximately 20 cm of isolated ileum. The ileal segment was configured in a reverse-L orientation, anastomosed proximally to the left ureter, then extended to the right ureter and bladder, providing bilateral ureteric substitution. Bilateral stents were retained for four weeks.<br/>\nResults: The postoperative course was uneventful, with satisfactory urinary drainage and stable renal function. Follow-up imaging demonstrated excellent drainage through the ileal segment without urinary stasis or obstruction. Long-term metabolic evaluation showed stable renal function and serum electrolytes.<br/>\nConclusion: Ileal ureter substitution is a dependable and effective reconstructive option for complex, long-segment bilateral ureteric strictures following hysterectomy when conventional reconstructive techniques are inadequate. Timely urinary diversion followed by definitive ileal replacement can restore urinary continuity, preserve renal function, and provide durable drainage.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-46\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 46</p><h3>Classical Bladder Exstrophy (CBE) repair: A series of 27 cases – video presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nThe exstrophy-epispadias complex (EEC) is a rare congenital malformation affecting the genitourinary tract and surrounding structures. Classical bladder exstrophy (CBE) demands complex, staged surgical management to achieve favorable long-term outcomes.</p><p>Objectives:<br/>\nTo present our institutional experience and outcome analysis of pediatric patients with classical bladder exstrophy managed using the modern staged reconstruction of exstrophy (MSRE) approach.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was conducted of 27 pediatric patients (20 males, 7 females) with CBE treated at our center between September 2014 and December 2023. Median age at primary bladder closure was 3 days (range: 2 days to 3 months). Patients were treated using MSRE principles. Cases of adult exstrophy and superior vesical fissure were excluded. Associated procedures performed during the same period included 28 epispadias repairs (including isolated and post-exstrophy cases) and 7 bladder neck reconstructions with bilateral ureteric reimplantation. All the steps of repair will be shown in a male neonate with CBE.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nAll 27 patients underwent successful primary bladder and abdominal wall closure. Four patients required additional interventions: two male patients underwent redo bladder closures for bladder prolapse (11.1% reclosure rate), one female required meatal dilatation for bladder outlet obstruction with hydronephrosis, and one male underwent suprapubic cystolithotomy for a vesical calculus formed around a non-absorbable suture, prompting a change in surgical material. All patients maintained stable renal function on follow-up.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nClassical bladder exstrophy requires precise, staged surgical management and long-term follow-up from infancy through adolescence. Early presentation allows for bladder closure without osteotomy due to pelvic malleability. Avoiding non-absorbable sutures in symphyseal approximation may reduce postoperative complications. Our results support MSRE as a reliable approach in managing this complex anomaly.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-47\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 47</p><h3>Minimal Access, Maximum Stone Burden: Laparoscopic Pyelolithotomy for a Giant Staghorn Calculus in a Dialysis-Dependent Patient</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Amandeep Singh, Manish Gupta, Girraj Prasad Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MGMCH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo demonstrate the feasibility of laparoscopic pyelolithotomy for a giant staghorn calculus in a dialysis-dependent patient with bilateral complex nephrolithiasis, emphasizing direct collecting-system access while minimizing renal parenchymal trauma.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA 68-year-old male with chronic kidney disease on maintenance hemodialysis presented with intermittent fever for 12 days and dysuria for one month. Investigations showed hemoglobin 7.4 g/dL, TLC 12.4 ×10³/µL, urea 81.2 mg/dL, and creatinine 5.3 mg/dL. Ultrasonography revealed bilateral renal calculi, gross hydronephrosis, and bilateral DJ stents. NCCT KUB demonstrated a 24 × 38 × 27-mm left renal pelvic calculus extending into all calyces and a 40 × 46-mm right staghorn calculus occupying the renal pelvis and all calyces. Bilateral DJ stenting was performed on 01 April 2025. Following clinical stabilization and preoperative optimization, laparoscopic right pyelolithotomy was performed on 16 May 2025. The surgical video demonstrates renal exposure and mobilization, pyelotomy, extraction of the staghorn calculus and accessible calyceal fragments, collecting-system inspection, and pyelotomy closure.</p><p>Results<br/>\nThe giant right staghorn calculus was extracted through the laparoscopic pyelotomy, along with accessible calyceal fragments under direct visualization. The approach provided direct access to the collecting system without creation of a percutaneous renal tract, thereby avoiding additional renal parenchymal trauma. The video highlights technical strategies for managing a large branching calculus in a patient with significant comorbidities.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nLaparoscopic pyelolithotomy is a feasible minimally invasive option for carefully selected patients with large, predominantly pelvic staghorn calculi. It may be particularly valuable when individualized surgical planning is required in patients with advanced CKD and significant comorbidities, offering direct collecting-system access while avoiding renal parenchymal tract creation.</p><p>Keywords: Laparoscopic pyelolithotomy; staghorn calculus; chronic kidney disease; hemodialysis; nephrolithiasis; surgical video.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-48\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 48</p><h3>Mastering robotic intra-corporeal urinary diversions: strategies to minimise complications</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Siddharth Yadav, TK Aravind, Biswajeet Sarmah, Dheerain Gupta, Sambit Kar, Deepak Kharab, Kapil Nathani, Anup Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC &amp; Safdarjung Hospital, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Intra-corporeal urinary diversions, both ileal conduit and neo-bladder, are becoming the SOC. In this video we aim to discuss subtle modifications that can significantly reduce both bowel related and diversion related complications.<br/>\nMethods: We focussed on the common problems encountered during intra-corporeal urinary diversions. These include 1) maintaining the orientation of the bowel to ensure that correct ends are joined together to maintain the bowel continuity and isolated bowel segment, from which the diversion is made, retains a retro-peritoneal position. 2) firing of stapler, if fired incorrectly, the vascularity of the edges may be compromised and bowel leak may occur in post-op period. 3) detubularising the bowel - too much cautery can cause delayed necrosis of bowel edges, leading to leakage at POD5-7 4) suturing the bowel - if inappropriate - leakage occurs on table during leakage test.</p><p>Results: The video describes important modifications to 1) promote safe bowel handling - handling bowel at mesenteric junction, 2) maintaining bowel orientation - orienting the bowel in a C - loop and maintaining this orientation until the desired segment is isolated, 3) principles of firing a stapler to isolate bowel segment - should be perpendicular to the bowel 4) principles of firing stapler to ensure proper side to side anastomosis - should be away from mesentery, at anti-mesenteric border, two 60mm staplers are fired, 5) detubularising bowel segment while maintaining vascularity - the bowel should be stretched, sero-muscular layer should be cut with cutting current and mucosa can be cut with point coagulation to avoid necrosis of edges of bowel, 6) suturing the bowel to ensure creation of leak proof pouch - sapproximating sero-muscular layer and inversion of mucosa.<br/>\n<br/>\nConclusion: Several subtle modifications and significantly reduce the chances of post-operative complications and ensure a safe and effective intra-corporeal urinary diversion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-49\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 49</p><h3>Initial experience with laparoscopic template based LND for renal masses: comparison with open template LND</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Siddharth Yadav, TK Aravind, Biswajeet Sarmah, Dheerain Gupta, Sambit Kar, Deepak Kharab, Kapil Nathani, Anup Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC &amp; Safdarjung Hospital, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Template based retroperitoneal LND (RPLND) is currently recommended for UTUC and for RCC with enlarged LN. However due to lack of survival benefit, a formal template LND is seldom performed. In the current era of immunotherapy, accurate staging of LN is gaining importance as adjuvant treatment is available. We aimed to evaluate the efficacy, defined as LN yield, of laparoscopic template RP-LND and compare with open RP-LND.</p><p>Methods: All patients who underwent template LND along with open or laparoscopic surgery for renal masses were included. The template, both for open and laparoscopic, included para-caval and inter-aortocaval for the right and para-aortic and inter-aortocaval for the left, extending from the hilum to aortic bifurcation. The mean number of LN removed were compared between laparoscopic and open.</p><p>Results: A total of 13 patients underwent template LND during surgery for renal mass, 8 in the open and 5 in the laparoscopic group. In the laparoscopic group - 3 had left side and 2 had right sided tumors. The mean number of LN yield was 17.7 in the open group and 19 in the laparoscopic group. None of the patients in either group developed major complications or local or infield recurrence.<br/>\nThis video discusses the surgical principles that allow for safe laparoscopic template based LND. Standard technique for split and roll is used for the right side whereas for the left side, slight modification is done and retro-aortic LN were spared. The video highlights one case for right and one for left, discussing the surgical technique and precautions required to perform safe template based LND.<br/>\n<br/>\nConclusion: Laparoscopic template based LND for renal masses can be safely performed and allows for adequate staging for the retro-peritoneum similar to open surgery, with low morbidity and similar complications.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-50\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 50</p><h3>Laparoscopic management of ipsilateral adrenal recurrences after Radical nephrectomy: Surgical principles and nuances</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Siddharth Yadav, TK Aravind, Biswajeet Sarmah, Dheerain Gupta, Sambit Kar, Deepak Kharab, Kapil Nathani, Anup Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC &amp; Safdarjung Hospital, New Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: Ipsilateral adrenal recurrence after radical nephrectomy(RN) is rare (2.2%) and adrenalectomy is the SOC and provides a chance of cure. However, performing ipsilateral adrenalectomy, after RN, is challenging. In this video, we share 3 lap adrenalectomies performed for recurrence after RN.<br/>\nMethods: A total of 3 patients underwent ipsilateral laparoscopic adrenalectomy after RN. 2 cases were on the right side, both had level 2 IVC thrombus and had underwent open RN. Both patients developed a small recurrence (1.3cm &amp; 2.9cm) in the ipsilateral adrenal gland, 1-1.5yrs after surgery. The third patient underwent left open RN via a subcostal incision 9 years ago and developed ipsilateral adrenal lesion of size 1.9cm. Metabolic workup was normal in all.<br/>\nResults: This video discusses the surgical principles and nuances that allow for safe adrenalectomy in 2 patients, one for each side.<br/>\nThe position was standard for lap adrenalectomy and 5mm port was placed in the ipsilateral iliac fossa and under visual guidance, other 3 ports were placed. The bowel adhesions were dissected. For the right side, the colon was separated from liver, the duodenum was medialised and IVC was identified along with prolene suture that was used to repair the IVC. The dissection was carried cephalad and adrenalectomy was performed. For the left side, the spleen and pancreas were reflected and aorta and left renal vein were identified. Left adrenal gland was removed in toto along with para-aortic nodal tissue. On both the sides, the adrenal fossa was made completely clear of the nodal and fatty tissue.<br/>\nThere were no complications and the patients were discharged on POD3. HPE showed ccRCC, pRCC and no malignancy. All patients are free of recurrences at 6mo.<br/>\nConclusion: Surgical excision is the SOC for ipsilateral adrenal recurrences after RN and can be safely managed laparoscopically.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-51\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 51</p><h3>Through the Cortical Window: Virtual 3D Planning and Intraoperative Ultrasound-Guided Robotic Partial Nephrectomy for a Completely Endophytic Hilar Tumor</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr HS Talwar, Dr R Upadhyay, Dr SA Nataraj, Dr RK Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital, Sector 128</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction</p><p>Completely endophytic renal tumors are technically challenging, particularly when located deep within the renal sinus, due to their proximity to hilar vessels and the collecting system. Advanced imaging and three-dimensional (3D) reconstruction can facilitate precise surgical planning and nephron preservation.</p><p>Methods</p><p>We present a robotic partial nephrectomy for a 3.5-cm completely endophytic upper-pole renal tumor in a 68-year-old man. CECT and MRI demonstrated a complex peripelvic lesion, ultimately classified as Bosniak 4, with close proximity to the upper segmental vessels and collecting system. Virtual 3D reconstruction demonstrated the tumor straddling the upper segmental vessels and closely related to the upper calyceal system. A trans-cortical approach was planned. Intraoperative ultrasound guided tumor localization and cortical window marking. Selective arterial clamping was followed by radial cortical incision and tumor enucleation. The collecting system was repaired with 4-0 PDS, while selective tumor-bed suturing with 3-0 V-Loc achieved hemostasis without formal two-layer renorrhaphy.</p><p>Results</p><p>Console time was 143 minutes, warm ischemia time 24 minutes, and estimated blood loss 150 mL. Complete tumor enucleation was achieved without capsular breach. An upper-pole calyceal infundibular opening was identified and repaired, with watertight closure confirmed by retrograde saline testing. Renal perfusion was confirmed using indocyanine green and Firefly fluorescence. There were no intraoperative or postoperative complications, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 3. Histopathology revealed hemangioendothelioma.</p><p>Conclusion</p><p>Completely endophytic renal tumors do not necessarily mandate radical nephrectomy. 3D anatomical reconstruction combined with intraoperative ultrasound can facilitate targeted trans-cortical access, complete tumor excision, and nephron preservation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-52\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 52</p><h3>Partial Urogenital Sinus Mobilization in Feminizing Genitoplasty: A Case Series of 7 Cases - Video Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nTo identify and classify the patients presenting with urogenital sinus and to demonstrate key surgical steps in its management.<br/>\nMaterial and methods:<br/>\nSeven children from an age range of 2-7 years presented to us with ambiguous genitalia (6 prader IV and 1 isolated clitoromegaly). All patients underwent a thorough history and clinical examination along with serum biochemistry including cortisol, ACTH, 17-OHP, Electrolytes (Na+, K+). Also, Karyotyping and Radiological investigations (MRI and Genitogram) were done to define the confluence and association with DSD. Preoperative endoscopy was done in every case to determine the exact level of confluence, length of the common channel and the proximal urethra. All these 6 children were amenable to correction by partial urogenital mobilisation with good cosmetic outcomes and urethral preservation. The one with isolated clitoromegaly underwent nerve sparing clitoral reduction. They are on hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone.<br/>\nResults and discussion:<br/>\nThese 7 children are in continuous follow-up and none of them has reached the adolescent age. Their parents have complete satisfaction with their cosmetic appearance.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nCommonest 46, XX DSD is 21-α hydroxylase deficiency. Prader IV genitalia requires multidisciplinary care. Hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone are cornerstone therapies. Surgery timing must balance ethics, consent &amp; parental wishes. Urologist plays a crucial role beyond reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-53\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 53</p><h3>Single-Stage Reconstruction of Classic Bladder Exstrophy in an Adolescent Female: A Viable Alternative to Diversion - Video Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Mukesh Chandra Arya, Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Taranpreet</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P. Medical College</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Untreated classic bladder exstrophy (CBE) presenting in adolescence is exceptionally rare and poses complex reconstructive, functional, and psychosocial challenges. Historically, delayed presentation has been managed predominantly with urinary diversion because of concerns regarding continence, malignant transformation, and technical feasibility. We present the successful single-stage anatomical reconstruction of an untreated 13-year-old female with preservation of the native bladder.<br/>\nMethods: Following multidisciplinary evaluation, multiple pre-operative mapping biopsies excluded malignancy and squamous metaplasia. After comprehensive counselling and shared decision-making, bladder preservation was selected over cystectomy. Single-stage reconstruction included bladder and urethral closure, bilateral ureteric reimplantation, Young–Dees bladder neck reconstruction, ileocystoplasty using a detubularized ileal segment, Mitrofanoff appendicovesicostomy, and reconstructive genitoplasty.<br/>\nResults: Postoperative recovery was uneventful with preserved renal function. The patient achieved volitional urethral continence with a compliant, high-capacity reservoir and remains dry while performing clean intermittent catheterization. Postoperative imaging demonstrated preserved upper urinary tracts without vesicoureteral reflux.<br/>\nConclusion: Delayed presentation of CBE should not be considered synonymous with mandatory urinary diversion. In carefully selected patients, meticulous single-stage bladder-preserving reconstruction is technically feasible and can achieve satisfactory continence while maintaining the native urinary tract. Pre-operative mapping biopsy, patient commitment to lifelong surveillance, and incorporation of a Mitrofanoff channel provide a safe and durable reconstructive strategy for these exceptionally rare cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-54\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 54</p><h3>Navigating the Anomaly: Robot-Assisted Radical Nephrectomy and Level II IVC Thrombectomy for a Right Renal Mass in Crossed Fused Renal Ectopia</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Aaryan Anand, Mohd. Azfar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction &amp; Objective: Crossed fused renal ectopia (CFRE) is a rare congenital anomaly, with an estimated incidence of 1 in 2,000 to 1 in 7,500 live births. Surgical intervention in these patients presents a formidable challenge due to highly unpredictable and complex vascular anatomy, the presence of a level II inferior vena cava(IVC) thrombus, and the need to transect the fused parenchymal isthmus.<br/>\nMethods: We present the case of a 70-year-old male with hematuria who was found to have a 9.8 x 7.6 x 11.4 cm heterogeneously enhancing mass in the right renal moiety. Preoperative computed tomography (CT) with 3D vascular reconstruction confirmed a Left-to-Right crossed fused renal ectopia with the mass localized to the right unit, further complicated by complex vascularity and a level II IVC thrombus. Steps<br/>\n1.Meticulous bowel mobilization and exposure of the retroperitoneum.<br/>\n2.Identification, dissection, and individual ligation of the anomalous, complex feeding arteries and veins supplying the tumor-bearing right moiety.<br/>\n3.Vascular control and robotic extraction of the level II IVC thrombus.<br/>\n4.Precise parenchymal transection at the fusion line using robotic shears.<br/>\nResults:The Da Vinci Xi robot-assisted radical nephrectomy of the right moiety and IVC thrombectomy were completed successfully without open conversion. The console was 274 minutes, and the estimated blood loss was 150 mL. The level II IVC thrombus was successfully extracted robotically with a warm ischemia time of 22 minutes. Final histopathological examination revealed a pT3c stage Clear Cell renal cell carcinoma with negative surgical margins.<br/>\nConclusion:Robot-assisted radical nephrectomy and thrombectomy for a tumor-bearing moiety with a level II IVC thrombus in crossed fused renal ectopia is safe and highly feasible. The 3D magnified vision and articulating EndoWrist technology of the Da Vinci Xi robotic platform provide a distinct advantage in navigating the complex vascularity, safely extracting the thrombus, and executing precise parenchymal transection.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-55\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 55</p><h3>Redefining \"En Bloc\": Contiguous Lymphadenectomy in Radical Nephroureterectomy for UTUC</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Aaryan Anand, Devanshu Sharma</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Radical nephroureterectomy (RNU) with bladder cuff excision is the gold standard for high-risk upper tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC). While template-based regional lymph node dissection (LND) is crucial for accurate staging and guiding adjuvant therapy, it is traditionally performed as a separate procedural step. This standard approach disrupts the intervening lymphatic channels. We present a novel robot-assisted laparoscopic technique achieving a true en bloc extraction of the entire nephroureterectomy specimen, bladder cuff, and regional lymph nodes in a single, contiguous tissue block to maximize strict oncological containment.<br/>\nMethods: We present a video demonstration of a robot-assisted laparoscopic RNU in a 60-year-old female patient with high-risk UTUC of the Left Renal Pelvis and Ureter. Da Vinci Xi Robot was used in maintaining anatomical continuity between the medial aspect of Gerota’s fascia and the adventitia of the great vessels. The distal ureter and bladder cuff were managed via a completely intracorporeal extravesical approach. The entire specimen encompassing the kidney, ureter, bladder cuff, and regional lymphatics was extracted intact.<br/>\nResults: The contiguous en bloc resection was completed successfully utilizing the robotic platform. The console time was 170 minutes with an estimated blood loss of 80 mL. Pathological evaluation confirmed an intact monobloc specimen, ensuring zero transection of the primary lymphatic drainage pathways. Final histopathology revealed a pT3N2 high-grade urothelial carcinoma with negative margins. The en bloc extraction yielded a total of 15 lymph nodes, allowing for precise and undisturbed pathological mapping.<br/>\nConclusion: Extracting the regional lymph nodes en bloc with the primary RNU and bladder cuff specimen is a technically demanding but highly feasible modification using a robot-assisted laparoscopic approach. This \"no-touch\" contiguous technique strictly adheres to the oncological principles by preventing the disruption of lymphatic channels, theoretically minimizing retroperitoneal spillage and providing an optimal specimen for pathological evaluation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-56\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 56</p><h3>The Kelly Repair : A step-by-step video atlas of RSTM without osteotomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Anuj Yadav, Dr Vinay Chandershekar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Kelly's Radical Soft Tissue Mobilisation (RSTM) offers a single-stage approach to bladder neck reconstruction, penile reconstruction, and abdominal wall closure without the requirement for pelvic osteotomy in patients with bladder exstrophy epispadias. We present a detailed step-by-step intraoperative video of Kelly's RSTM in a male patient with primary BEEC, performed without osteotomy.<br/>\nMATERIAL AND METHODS:<br/>\nA male infant with primary BEEC underwent Kelly's RSTM at our centre. The intraoperative video demonstrates the following sequential steps:<br/>\n1. Bladder plate mobilisation — circumferential incision around bladder plate with preservation of urothelium<br/>\n2. Pelvic floor dissection — radical mobilisation of the levator ani and pubococcygeus from the pelvic sidewall bilaterally<br/>\n3. Pudendal neurovascular bundle identification and preservation — meticulous dissection of the pudendal pedicle to protect erectile function<br/>\n4. Corporal separation — Complete separation of the corpora cavernosa from each other and from the urethra<br/>\n5. Bladder neck and urethral reconstruction — Tubularisation of the anterior detrusor muscle flap over a Fr 8 catheter<br/>\n6. Penile reconstruction — corporal realignment with correction of dorsal chordee<br/>\nRESULTS:<br/>\nOperative time was 180 minutes. Estimated blood loss was 50 mL. The urethral catheter was removed at 14 days postoperatively. At 3 months follow-up, the patient demonstrated satisfactory bladder cycling with an improving dry interval, voiding per urethra without the need for clean intermittent catheterisation.<br/>\nDISCUSSION:<br/>\nKelly's RSTM achieves continent bladder neck reconstruction through radical pelvic soft tissue mobilisation, a significant advantage in the Indian healthcare setting. The avoidance of osteotomy reduces operative time, eliminates the need for postoperative immobilisation, and extends applicability to older children and redo cases.<br/>\nCONCLUSION:<br/>\nThis intraoperative video demonstration provides a structured anatomical guide to the key steps of the procedure and aims to facilitate wider adoption of the technique among Indian urologists managing this rare but complex condition.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-57\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 57</p><h3>NEVER TOO LATE: modified Cantwell–Ransley repair with bladder neck reconstruction for adult primary penopubic epispadias: a step-by-step video</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Anuj Yadav, Dr Ganesh Gopalakrishnan</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nPrimary, unrepaired epispadias presenting in adulthood is rare, since the anomaly is almost always corrected in infancy. The penopubic variant is the most severe: the dorsal urethral defect extends to the bladder neck, disrupting the sphincteric mechanism, and most such patients are incontinent, with only about 40% achieving dryness. The modified Cantwell-Ransley repair is the procedure of choice for isolated epispadias, giving durable functional and cosmetic results with fewer major complications than earlier techniques.<br/>\nObjective:<br/>\nTo demonstrate, step by step, the modified Cantwell-Ransley repair with concurrent anatomical bladder neck reconstruction in an adult with primary penopubic incontinent epispadias.<br/>\nTechnique:<br/>\nThe video demonstrates positioning, stay sutures and marking of the incision; circumferential penile degloving; mobilisation of the dorsal urethral plate off the corpora, preserving its distal attachment at the glans to maintain vascularity; tubularisation of the plate over a catheter to create the neourethra; separation and medial rotation of the corporal bodies over the neourethra to transpose it ventrally and correct dorsal chordee; glansplasty; concurrent anatomical bladder neck reconstruction to reconstitute the continence mechanism; and skin cover with urinary diversion and dressing.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThe procedure was completed without intra-operative complication. At short-term follow-up the wound had healed, penile cosmesis was satisfactory with the neourethra lying ventrally, and the patient was voiding per urethra with insignificant postvoid residue and normal upper tracts.<br/>\nConclusions:<br/>\nAdult primary penopubic incontinent epispadias can be managed in a single sitting by modified Cantwell-Ransley repair with concurrent anatomical bladder neck reconstruction, with good early anatomical, cosmetic and voiding outcomes. Continence is the definitive endpoint and requires longer follow-up to track the upper tracts. The video is intended as a technical reference for a condition rarely encountered in adult practice</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-58\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 58</p><h3>Untouched Hilum: An Off-Clamp Robotic Partial Nephrectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Nishant Setya, Dr Sachin A Nataraj , Dr Rajesh Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital , Sector 128 , Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background: Nephron-sparing surgery aims to achieve oncological control while maximizing preservation of functioning renal parenchyma. An Off-clamp robot-assisted partial nephrectomy (RAPN) eliminates hilar occlusion and may be particularly attractive in elderly patients with metabolic comorbidities.<br/>\nCase :A 75-year-old man with hypertension, dyslipidemia and diabetes (HbA1c 7.0%) was evaluated for an incidentally detected right renal mass. Baseline serum creatinine was 1.06 mg/dL. CECT demonstrated a 2.5 × 2.3 cm heterogeneously enhancing interpolar cortical lesion in the right kidney, with multiple bilateral cortical cysts including large right renal cysts. PET-CT confirmed a 2.7 × 2.5 cm interpolar cortical lesion without evidence of metastatic disease. Considering the Peripheral location of tumor with minimal surrounding parenchyma , robotic partial nephrectomy using an off-clamp strategy was planned.<br/>\nThe interpolar tumor was resected without hilar clamping, permitting nephron-sparing excision while avoiding global renal ischemia. Hemostasis was achieved with topical hemostatic agent (Veriset) . Selected renal cysts were simultaneously deroofed.<br/>\nOutcome: Histopathology demonstrated papillary renal cell carcinoma, grade 2, confined to the kidney, with negative margins and no lymphovascular invasion (pT1a).<br/>\nDiscussion: The principal advantage of off-clamp RAPN is avoidance of ischemic insult while preserving maximal renal parenchyma. Its importance is highlighted in carefully selected tumors where meticulous excision and hemostasis can be achieved without hilar control. A 2023 meta-analysis of 10 studies reported less postoperative eGFR deterioration with off-clamp RAPN. Similarly an updated 2026 meta-analysis of 39 studies involving 10,154 patients found a smaller eGFR decline with off-clamp surgery, although blood loss was higher, emphasizing individualized selection.<br/>\nConclusion: This video demonstrates an off-clamp robotic partial nephrectomy for a small interpolar renal tumor with surrounding cortical cysts ,in an elderly, comorbid patient, highlighting ischemia avoidance without compromising oncological control or nephron-sparing principles.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-59\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 59</p><h3>Revascularizing the Solitary Kidney in Renovascular Hypertension: A Novel Robot-Assisted Splenorenal Bypass surgical approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Nipun Bansal, Karandeep Guleria, Rahul Agarwal, Samit Chaturvedi, Anant Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality Hospital, Vaishali, Ghaziabad, NCR Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Renovascular hypertension in a solitary functioning kidney poses a significant therapeutic challenge. Although endovascular stenting is generally preferred, ostial renal artery disease and unfavorable anatomy may limit its feasibility and durability. We report the first robot-assisted splenorenal bypass using da Vinci Xi platform with intraoperative indocyanine green (ICG) perfusion assessment.</p><p>Methods: A 67-year-old woman with a solitary left kidney presented with accelerated, medically refractory hypertension. She had undergone right simple nephrectomy for a non-functioning kidney secondary to recurrent infections. Her serum creatinine was 1.3 mg/dL. CT renal angiography demonstrated approximately 20% ostial and up to 30% short-segment post-ostial stenosis of the left renal artery, with mild aorto-mesenteric atherosclerosis. Given the ostial location and solitary renal status, endovascular stenting was considered unfavorable. DTPA renography demonstrated preserved renal perfusion and function. Robotic splenorenal bypass was performed transperitoneally in the right lateral decubitus position with a four-arm da Vinci Xi configuration. ICG Firefly imaging was used to assess graft patency and renal and splenic perfusion.</p><p>Results: Robotic dissection and vascular anastomosis were successfully completed. The bypass demonstrated satisfactory intraoperative flow, with homogeneous and synchronous ICG fluorescence of the kidney and spleen, confirming adequate perfusion. No perioperative vascular complication was encountered.<br/>\nPostoperatively, serum creatinine and ultrasound doppler were normal. At follow-up, the requirement for antihypertensive therapy was found to be reduced.</p><p>Conclusion: Robot-assisted splenorenal bypass is a feasible minimally invasive alternative for selected patients with renovascular hypertension and a solitary functioning kidney when endovascular therapy is anatomically unfavorable. Robotic precision combined with real-time ICG perfusion assessment may offer safe and minimally invasive surgical treatment for such patients.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-60\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 60</p><h3>Robotic-Assisted Pyelovesicostomy With Psoas Hitch For Partial Duplex Collecting System With Pelviureteric Junction Obstruction And Ectopic Ureter: A Reconstructive Challenge</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr.Shikhar Garg, Dr. Ankur Mittal, Dr.Vikas Kumar Panwar, Dr. DattaSai</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Rishikesh</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>INTRODUCTION<br/>\nPartial duplex collecting system with concurrent pelviureteric junction obstruction<br/>\n(PUJO) of the lower moiety and ectopic ureteric insertion into the prostatic urethra is an<br/>\nexceptionally rare congenital triad. When the entire ureter is rendered anatomically and<br/>\nfunctionally unusable, standard reconstructive options are unavailable. We report a<br/>\nrobotic-assisted pyelovesicostomy with psoas hitch for this combination.<br/>\nCASE<br/>\nA 25-year-old male presented with left flank pain and dysuria for 3 months. CT<br/>\nurography revealed a left partial duplex collecting system with lower moiety PUJO, gross<br/>\nhydronephrosis, cortical thinning, and the ureter inserting ectopically into the posterior<br/>\nprostatic urethra.. Percutaneous nephrostomy was placed with output~ 1 L/day.<br/>\nCystoscopy and retrograde pyelography confirmed the ectopic orifice at the prostatic<br/>\nurethra.<br/>\nOPERATIVE TECHNIQUE<br/>\nRobotic-assisted transperitoneal surgery was performed. The sigmoid colon was<br/>\nmobilised and the left ureter identified. The ectopic ureter was dissected to the<br/>\nprostatic level, divided, and the stump closed. The obstructed PUJO segment was<br/>\nexcised proximally. Retzius space was developed via median umbilical ligament<br/>\ndivision; a left psoas hitch was fashioned to bridge the distance tension-free. The renal<br/>\npelvis was spatulated and a pyelovesicostomy constructed using interrupted Vicryl 4-0<br/>\nsutures, creating a direct pelvis-to-bladder anastomosis bypassing the entire unusable<br/>\nureter.<br/>\nRESULTS<br/>\nThe post-operative course was uneventful. Drain was removed and PCN clamped on<br/>\npost-operative day 2. The patient was discharged on day 3 in haemodynamically stable<br/>\ncondition. Planned follow-up includes catheter removal at 2 weeks and nephrostogram<br/>\nat 4 weeks to confirm anastomotic patency.<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\nRobotic-assisted pyelovesicostomy with psoas hitch is a safe, feasible reconstructive<br/>\nsolution when the entire ureter is unusable. PCN output is a critical determinant of<br/>\nrenal salvageability. The robotic platform provides decisive advantage for this dual-field<br/>\ndissection requiring both deep pelvic and retroperitoneal access.<br/>\nKEYWORDS: pyelovesicostomy · psoas hitch · ectopic<br/>\nureter robotic reconstructive urology</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-61\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 61</p><h3>Robotic heminephrectomy for complex large renal masses: a case series</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sahil mahajan, Dr Sidhartha kalra, Dr Rudra Prasad Ghorai, Dr Sreerag KS, Dr Parthiban Palanivel, Dr Pulkit Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JIPMER, PUDUCHERRY</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Robotic Hemi Nephrectomy for Complex, Large Renal Masses: A Case Series<br/>\nIntroduction<br/>\nPartial/hemi nephrectomy remains the gold standard for nephron-sparing management of renal masses. Complex, large renal masses with adverse anatomical features have traditionally posed a challenge for nephron-sparing surgery and often prompted consideration of radical nephrectomy. The robotic platform, with its enhanced dexterity and precision, allows safe and effective nephron-sparing resection even in such complex tumors.<br/>\nObjective<br/>\nTo demonstrate, through a case series, that robotic hemi nephrectomy is a feasible nephron-sparing option even in complex, large renal masses.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nWe present a case series of four patients (age range 32–41 years) who underwent robotic hemi nephrectomy for renal masses ranging from 4.5 cm to 6.6 cm. Tumor complexity ranged from low to high nephrometry scores. Key surgical steps including selective vascular control, tumor excision with adequate margins, and parenchymal reconstruction are demonstrated.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nAll four procedures were completed successfully with negative margins and preservation of the remaining renal unit. No major intraoperative or postoperative complications occurred. The video highlights technical nuances in vascular control and hemostasis specific to large, complex renal tumors.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nThis case series demonstrates that robotic hemi nephrectomy is a safe and feasible option even in complex, large renal masses, achieving oncologic control while maximizing renal preservation.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-62\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 62</p><h3>Complex Redo Scrotal Hypospadias Repair in a Single Stage: Video Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Kushagra Tyagi, Dr. Mukesh Arya, Dr. Yogendra, Dr. Shubham Jain, Dr. Taranpreet</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">S.P Medical College, Bikaner</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Complex Redo Scrotal Hypospadias Repair in a Single Stage: Video Presentation<br/>\nINTRODUCTION<br/>\nHypospadias is one of the most common congenital anomalies of the male genitalia. In developing countries such as India, delayed presentation into adulthood remains not uncommon, owing to limited awareness, socioeconomic constraints, and restricted access to healthcare. Redo or secondary hypospadias repair is technically challenging because of dense scarring, an inadequate urethral plate, persistent ventral chordee, and deficiency of healthy local tissue. Achieving satisfactory functional and cosmetic outcomes in a single stage remains particularly demanding in such unfavorable settings.<br/>\nMATERIALS AND METHODS<br/>\nAn 18-year-old male presented with complete dehiscence following previous repair of scrotal hypospadias. Clinical examination revealed a severely scarred and poorly developed urethral plate with significant ventral chordee. Surgical reconstruction involved dorsal midline plication for chordee correction, followed by dorsal inlay augmentation of the urethral plate using buccal mucosal grafts harvested from both cheeks. The augmented plate was subsequently tubularized.<br/>\nRESULTS<br/>\nPostoperatively, the patient received intravenous ceftriaxone and amikacin for three days. The Romovac drain was removed on postoperative day three, and the urethral stent was removed at two weeks. The patient demonstrated satisfactory postoperative recovery and follow-up outcomes.<br/>\nInstitutional data from January 2010 to December 2023 included 95 patients undergoing secondary hypospadias repair, with an overall success rate of 78.9%. Twenty patients required revision surgery, predominantly for urethrocutaneous fistula, meatal stenosis, glanular dehiscence, and urethral stricture.<br/>\nCONCLUSION<br/>\nRedo scrotal hypospadias repair is a complex reconstructive challenge requiring individualized correction of associated abnormalities, including fistula, chordee, torsion, short urethra, penoscrotal transposition, and ventral skin deficiency. Even in the setting of previous failed repair and severely compromised local tissues, carefully planned single-stage reconstruction is feasible in appropriately selected patients. Minor residual chordee or torsion may be acceptable when balanced against achieving a successful single-stage functional reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-63\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 63</p><h3>Concomitant Robotic Redo anti-reflux surgery with uretero-ureterostomy in a previously missed retrocaval ureter: Technical caveats</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Sarvagya Loria, Dr. Pankaj Wadhwa, Dr. Harkirat Singh Talwar, Dr. Praanjal Gupta, Dr. Sudeep Bodduluri, Dr. Rajesh Ahlawat</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Super Speciality hospital, Noida</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo describe the technical challenges and clinical outcomes of robotic redo anti-reflux surgery combined with uretero-ureterostomy in a patient with a missed retrocaval ureter, persistent high-grade vesicoureteral reflux (VUR), and dysfunctional voiding.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA young woman presented with recurrent febrile urinary tract infections, poor stream, elevated post-void residual urine, and renal insufficiency. She had previously undergone robotic Hutch diverticulectomy and bilateral Lich–Gregoir ureteric reimplantation for VUR. Despite clean intermittent catheterisation, infections and right flank pain persisted.<br/>\nRepeat micturating cystourethrography showed persistent grade 4 reflux, obstructive right-sided drainage, and upper ureteric kinking. Urodynamics revealed dysfunctional voiding with extrinsic sphincter non-relaxation and staccato flow. Serum creatinine was 2.2 mg/dL with a positive urine culture. MRI confirmed a retrocaval ureter; cystoscopy showed absent bilateral intravesical tunnels. Right DJ stenting and Foley catheterisation reduced creatinine to 1.6 mg/dL.<br/>\nRobotic right uretero-ureterostomy with bilateral extravesical ureteral tunnelling and detrusorrhaphy was performed. A type I retrocaval ureter with marked hydronephrosis was mobilised, anteriorly transposed, and repaired with a wide end-to-end anastomosis. Owing to absent periureteral tissue and concern for vascular compromise, the right distal ureter was not mobilised beyond the aortic bifurcation. Extravesical seromuscular tunnels (4–5 cm) were created without distal ureteral transection. Indocyanine green confirmed satisfactory bilateral ureteral perfusion.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nAt one-year follow-up, the patient remained infection-free, voided well with insignificant post-void residual, and no longer required catheterisation. DTPA renography showed adequate drainage and compromised but preserved renal function, with total GFR 40 mL/min (right 60.3%; left 39.7%). Serum creatinine remained stable at 1.6 mg/dL.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nEvaluation for concomitant upper-tract obstruction is essential in persistent VUR. Non-transectional techniques, in-situ detrusorrhaphy, is preferable to treat reflux. Limited proximal ureteral mobilization, avoiding distal transection maintained ureteral vascularity during redo surgery. Management also requires correction of bladder–bowel dysfunction through pelvic-floor rehabilitation and functional therapy.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-64\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 64</p><h3>Concurrent VVF and RVF in a Previously-Operated Pelvis: Single-Stage Robotic Cure</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Beniwal S, Dr Kalra S, Dr Gupta P, Dr Kolte A, Dr Sreerag K, Dr Ghorai R P</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">JIPMER , Puducherry</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Concurrent VVF and RVF in a Previously-Operated Pelvis: Single-Stage Robotic Cure</p><p>Aims<br/>\nVesicovaginal fistula (VVF) complicates approximately 0.1–0.2% of hysterectomies, gynaecological surgery being its leading cause in the developed world; a concurrent VVF and rectovaginal fistula (RVF) is rare and technically demanding, particularly in a hostile, previously-operated pelvis [1]. We report a single-stage robot-assisted repair of both fistulae and highlight the principles of ureteric protection and separate vascularised interposition that underpin cure.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 60-year-old woman developed urine and stool leak per vagina after laparoscopic total hysterectomy for endometrial carcinoma. A diversion loop ileostomy transiently controlled symptoms, but leakage recurred within a month of reversal, leaving combined urinary and faecal incontinence for nearly two years. Comorbidities included hypertension, hypothyroidism and schizophrenia. Diagnostic evaluation per EAU principles — CECT with rectal contrast — confirmed concurrent RVF and VVF with no oncologic recurrence, alongside a large incisional/parastomal hernia (5.7×5.2 cm defect) and gross rectus divarication (17.3 cm) [3]. After multidisciplinary optimisation (urology, surgical gastroenterology, psychiatry) and a sterile pre-operative urine culture, she underwent single-sitting robot-assisted transabdominal repair — O'Connor technique for the VVF and layered closure for the RVF — using multilayer, non-overlapping suture lines with separate vascularised interpositions (omentum and sigmoid mesentery), and, per ERUS best-practice recommendations, bilateral protective ureteric catheters to safeguard orifices lying only ~2.5 cm from the tracts [1,2].<br/>\nResults<br/>\nRobotic supratrigonal VVF repair carries reported success exceeding 90% [1,2]. Recovery was uneventful, with early return of bowel and bladder function and no new stoma; she was discharged on day 15. Cystogram confirmed no leak, and at follow-up she remained continent with no fistula recurrence.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nConcurrent VVF and RVF in a hostile pelvis can be cured in a single stage using a robotic approach, meticulous ureteric protection and separate interposition flaps, avoiding a second diversion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-65\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 65</p><h3>Robotic “Open-Book” Trans-Parenchymal Nephrotomy for a Completely Endophytic Hilar Tumour</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Mohit</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta-the medicity gurugram</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Dr Mohit, Dr Supradeep, Dr Harshit, Dr Gagandeep Talwar, Dr Puneet Ahluwalia, Dr Gagan Gautam</p><p>AIMS<br/>\nCompletely endophytic hilar renal tumours are challenging for nephron-sparing surgery because cortical landmarks are absent and the tumour is closely related to hilar vessels and the collecting system. We demonstrate robotic “open-book” nephrotomy aided by three-dimensional (3D) reconstruction and intraoperative ultrasound for management of a completely endophytic hilar tumour with complex duplicated hilar vasculature.</p><p>MATERIALS &amp; METHODS<br/>\nPreoperative 3D reconstruction demonstrated a completely endophytic mid-polar hilar tumour surrounded by multiple segmental vessels arising from two renal arteries and two renal veins. Following transperitoneal robotic access, meticulous hilar dissection was performed with control of both renal arteries and veins. A robotic drop-in ultrasound probe localized the tumour and delineated its relationship to the hilar vessels and collecting system. An ultrasound-guided trans-parenchymal scoring line was marked. Following vascular clamping, controlled nephrotomy was performed along the planned trajectory, opening the kidney in an “open-book” fashion and directly exposing the concealed tumour. Tumour enucleation was completed along the pseudo-capsular plane. Renorrhaphy included selective vessel ligation, inner-layer reconstruction, and cortical closure. Indocyanine green fluorescence was used after unclamping to assess renal perfusion.</p><p>RESULTS<br/>\nThe open-book nephrotomy provided excellent exposure of the tumour and hilar anatomy, facilitating precise excision despite complex vascular relationships. Warm ischemia time was 19 minutes. Indocyanine green confirmed homogeneous renal reperfusion without segmental devascularization. Histopathology revealed clear cell renal cell carcinoma with negative surgical margins.</p><p>CONCLUSION<br/>\nRobotic open-book nephrotomy combined with 3D planning and intraoperative ultrasound is a feasible strategy for selected completely endophytic hilar tumours, facilitating anatomical orientation, precise tumour excision, nephron preservation, and sound oncological outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-66\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 66</p><h3>Hemostatic Versatility of Veriset™ in Partial Nephrectomy: A Transition Toward Suture-Sparing and Sutureless Techniques</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Ashish Kumar, Dr. Palash Sawhney, Dr. Swarnendu Mandal</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, BHUBANESWAR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background<br/>\nAchieving secure hemostasis during partial nephrectomy while minimizing warm ischemia time and parenchymal compression remains challenging. Conventional renorrhaphy is effective but may increase operative complexity and parenchymal strain. Veriset™, a topical hemostatic patch, may facilitate renal reconstruction while reducing the extent of suturing.</p><p>**Objective**<br/>\nTo demonstrate the versatility of Veriset™ during partial nephrectomy across reconstructive strategies ranging from adjunctive use with renorrhaphy to completely sutureless hemostasis.</p><p>Materials and Methods<br/>\nFour illustrative cases were reviewed. Case 1 involved a 38-year-old male with a 3.6-cm right renal mass and chronic kidney disease; internal renorrhaphy was followed by application of a 5 × 5-cm Veriset™ patch over the resection bed. Case 2 involved a 69-year-old male with a 4.2-cm left renal mass and pelvicalyceal system entry; after collecting-system closure, Veriset™ was used as a bolster with subsequent V-Loc defect closure. Case 3 involved a 42-year-old male with a 2.7-cm interpolar right renal mass undergoing robotic partial nephrectomy with renorrhaphy supplemented by Veriset™. Case 4 involved a 58-year-old male with a 2.0-cm upper-pole right renal mass managed without formal renorrhaphy, with Veriset™ applied over the resection bed.</p><p>Results<br/>\nWarm ischemia time decreased from 30 minutes in Case 1 to 22 minutes, 15 minutes 17 seconds, and approximately 11 minutes in Cases 2, 3, and 4, respectively. Drains were removed on postoperative day 2 or 3, with uneventful recovery where documented. Reduced ischemia time paralleled decreasing reliance on parenchymal suturing, although differences in tumor size, complexity, and operative approach prevent direct comparison.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nVeriset™ showed versatility as an adjunct, bolster, and primary surface hemostatic agent. Progressive reduction in parenchymal suturing may simplify reconstruction and shorten warm ischemia time. Completely sutureless partial nephrectomy appears feasible in selected cases, but prospective evaluation of bleeding, urinary leakage, renal function, and patient-selection criteria is required before wider adoption.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-67\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 67</p><h3>Laparoscopic Transmesocolic Excision of Left Retroperitoneal Paraganglioma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Siddharth Garg, Dr Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr Gautam Ram Choudhary, Dr Mahendra Singh, Dr Deepak Bhirud, Dr AS Sandhu</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nRetroperitoneal paragangliomas are rare, catecholamine-secreting extra-adrenal tumours arising from chromaffin tissue, and their proximity to major vasculature makes surgical excision challenging. This video aims to demonstrate the technique and feasibility of laparoscopic excision of a left para-aortic paraganglioma, highlighting key steps for safe minimally invasive management of such lesions.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nWe present a case of a 48-year-old female with a two-year history of hypertension with paroxysmal spells of sweating and palpitations with surges in blood pressure, raising suspicion of a catecholamine-secreting tumour. Contrast-enhanced CT and SPECT/CT imaging revealed a heterogeneously enhancing, SSTR-expressing solid-cystic retroperitoneal mass (approximately 5–6 cm) in the left anterior pararenal space at the L1–L3 level, abutting the aorta, left kidney, and psoas muscle, consistent with paraganglioma. After appropriate alpha- and beta-blockade, the patient underwent laparoscopic excision in the right lateral decubitus position via a transmesocolic approach. The tumour was mobilised using sharp and blunt dissection. The mass was carefully separated from the lower pole of the kidney and the aorta.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nA well-circumscribed 6×6×5 cm paraganglioma was completely excised laparoscopically via transmesocolic window without conversion to open surgery and with no intraoperative vascular injury. The postoperative course was uneventful; the drain was removed on postoperative day 2, and the patient was discharged in stable condition by postoperative day 4.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nLaparoscopic transmesocolic excision of retroperitoneal paragangliomas is a safe and effective approach in carefully selected cases, even for tumours in close proximity to major vasculature, provided meticulous dissection and vascular control are employed. This technique offers the established benefits of minimally invasive surgery—reduced morbidity and faster recovery—without compromising oncological or surgical safety, supporting its wider adoption in appropriately worked-up patients.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-68\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 68</p><h3>“Reconstructing the Bladder: A Video Guide to Augmentation Cystoplasty”</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Varsha, Dr.Rahul</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">GMC JAMMU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Methodology: A 22 year old female presented with lower urinary tract symptoms and left flank pain was diagnosed with genitourinary tuberculosis and treated with anti-tubercular therapy for eight weeks. Evaluation revealed a solitary functioning right kidney with hydronephrosis and a non-functioning left kidney, with a contracted thimble bladder. Right PCN was placed. The patient was planned for laparoscopic augmentation cystoplasty. Technical emphasis was placed on strategic port placement to facilitate dissection and deep pelvic suturing, precise bowel segment identification, measurement, and stapling, hitch-stitch stabilization of the bowel segment, and use of barbed sutures (V-Loc/Stratafix) for efficient, secure closure. A suprapubic catheter, per-urethral catheter, and pelvic drain were placed at the end of the procedure. Postoperatively, patients were managed per an Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) protocol, with vigilant monitoring for catheter blockage, gentle flushing as needed, and nutritional optimization, given the frequently compromised baseline health status of these patients.</p><p>Result: Laparoscopic augmentation cystoplasty was successfully performed with meticulous attention to port ergonomics, bowel handling, and suturing technique. A confirmatory MCUG was planned at 3–4 weeks prior to catheter removal. The procedure proved feasible with reduced morbidity, reinforcing that with careful planning, patience, and precise execution, laparoscopic augmentation cystoplasty is a safe and effective option in experienced hands.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-69\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 69</p><h3>Pushing the Boundaries: Robotic Complex Vesicovaginal Fistulas reconstruction</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Niraj Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC and Safdarjung Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: To demonstrate the robotic reconstruction technique for complex vesico-vaginal fistula (VVF)<br/>\nMethods: We demonstrate robotic reconstruction of complex VVF. We reconstructed the complex VVF (previous failed repair/ ureter too close to fistula/ size &gt;2-2.5 cm) with robot assistance. The robotic dexterity and freedom of movement allowed us to repair the complex VVF extravesical or transvaginal with minimal damage to the vagina, bladder and ureters.<br/>\nResults: a total of 14 complex VVF (ureter too close to fistula- 5/previous failed repair and/or large fistula- 9) repair done between 2021-2026. The 1 was converted to open in view of frozen pelvis. The console time: 45 minutes (range 22-87 minutes), hospital stay: 3 days (one case: 8 days), catheter ± DJ stent removal: 10-14 days. None of the patient reported grade 3 or higher complications.<br/>\nConclusion: Robotic assistance allows complex extravesical or transvaginal VVF reconstruction with minimal morbidity.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-70\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 70</p><h3>Robotic ileocystoplasty with ureteric reconstruction</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Niraj Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC and Safdarjung Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims: To demonstrate the robotic reconstruction technique for complex bladder ureter pathology<br/>\nMethods: We demonstrate robotic reconstruction of bladder with ureteric reimplantation in either bladder or ileal tube. In this case we reimplanted ureter in the proximal 10 cm of non-tubularised segment of ileum. The distal 20 cm of ileum was de-tubularised and anastomosed to bi-valved urinary bladder.<br/>\nResults: A total of 5 patients required simultaneous ureteric and bladder reconstruction. Of which, 3 were neurogenic bladder with poor compliance and refluxing megaureter and 2 were tubercular bladder with long segment ureteric stricture. The console time range between 150-205 minutes, blood loss 100-200 ml, drain removal time 2-3 days, hospital stay 5-8 days and catheter + DJ stent removal at 14 days.<br/>\nConclusion: Robotic precise, controlled dissection &amp; fine suturing helps complete the complex reconstruction with minimal morbidity without hurting surgeon’s shoulder.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-71\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 71</p><h3>Robotic VVF - Technical Nuances</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Kapil Garg, Dr. Kapil Dubey, Dr. Sahil Data, Dr. Rajnish Kumar, Dr. Aman Gupta, Dr. Manav Suryavanshi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Amrita institute of Medical Sciences, Faridabad</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nVesicovaginal fistula (VVF) is a challenging complication following hysterectomy. Robotic reconstruction provides enhanced visualization and manoeuvrability in deep pelvic regions. This presentation highlights the critical intraoperative technical nuances required to execute a successful, tension-free robotic transvesical VVF repair.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA 52-year-old female presented with post-hysterectomy continuous urinary incontinence. Preoperative CT urography and cystogram confirmed a small VVF between the posterior bladder dome and vaginal vault. The patient underwent a robotic-assisted transperitoneal transvesical repair incorporating key technical steps:<br/>\nUreteric orifices were cannulated with double-J stents. A Foley catheter was passed vaginally through the fistula tract to assist intraoperative mapping. After adhesiolysis, the bladder was bivalved sagitally down to the fistula (O’Conor technique). The bladder was mobilized adequately circumferentially off the vaginal wall, and scarred tissue was excised. The vaginal wall was closed horizontally, and the bladder wall was closed vertically to avoid overlapping suture lines.<br/>\nAn omental flap was secured between the suture lines.</p><p>Results<br/>\nConsole time was 75 minutes with minimal blood loss. Integrity of the suture line was confirmed intraoperatively with a 200 ml saline leak test. The patient had an uneventful postoperative recovery and was discharged with a urinary catheter. Follow-up evaluation with cystogram demonstrates successful structural repair, complete resolution of incontinence, and maintenance of upper tract function.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nAdherence to the surgical principles technical nuances—such as intraoperative tract cannulation, adequate tissue mobilization, perpendicular multi-layer closures, and omental flap interposition—ensures a reliable, watertight closure and high success rates in robotic VVF repair with minimally invasive benefits.</p><p>No conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-72\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 72</p><h3>ROBOTIC REDO ANASTOMOSIS POST KTP URETERIC STRICTURE</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr R Mongha</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Marengo Asia Hospital, Faridabad</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims</p><p>To describe the management of a post-kidney transplant ureteric stricture causing persistent obstructive uropathy and renal dysfunction, and to demonstrate the feasibility of redo robotic ureteric reconstruction in a previously operated field.</p><p>Methods</p><p>A kidney transplant recipient who underwent transplantation in 2024 presented with a progressive rise in serum creatinine. The patient developed a ureteric stricture and underwent ureteric stenting on three occasions; however, renal function failed to improve satisfactorily. DTPA renography demonstrated delayed and obstructive excretion of radionuclide, confirming significant functional obstruction. In view of persistent obstruction and deteriorating renal function despite repeated endourological interventions, definitive surgical reconstruction was planned. Redo robotic ureteric anastomosis was performed with meticulous dissection of the transplanted ureter, excision of the stenotic segment, and creation of a tension-free mucosa-to-mucosa anastomosis over a DJ stent. The operative technique and technical challenges of robotic reconstruction in a post-transplant setting are demonstrated through surgical video.</p><p>Results</p><p>The procedure was successfully completed robotically with adequate excision of the fibrotic segment and restoration of ureteric continuity. The robotic approach provided enhanced visualization and precise dissection in the reoperative field while facilitating intracorporeal suturing. Postoperatively, urinary drainage was satisfactory, with subsequent improvement in serum creatinine. Follow-up demonstrated satisfactory graft drainage without evidence of recurrent obstruction.</p><p>Conclusion</p><p>Ureteric stricture is an important urological complication following kidney transplantation and may lead to progressive graft dysfunction. While repeated stenting may provide temporary relief, definitive reconstruction should be considered in persistent or recurrent obstruction. Redo robotic ureteric anastomosis is a feasible minimally invasive option in selected transplant recipients, offering precise dissection and reconstruction even in challenging reoperative fields.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-73\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 73</p><h3>THE DIFFICULT RARP: BEYOND THE STANDARD</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Deepak, Dr Aravind, Dr Anup Kumar, Dr Siddharth Yadav, Dr Ruchi Mittal, Dr Abhishek Johari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">VMMC AND SJH, delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nRobot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP) is standardised for an untouched gland in a favourable pelvis. This video demonstrates how the operation is modified when the field is not standard: a prostate operated two years earlier, a resected prostate, locally advanced disease, and a deep narrow pelvis. For each, it shows what was altered at the console, the preparation that permitted it, and the recovery it dictated.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nAn eight-minute video from a single tertiary centre presents four RARPs, each with imaging, clip of the critical steps, and points to watch: (1) 76-year-old man, two years after robot-assisted simple prostatectomy, PSA 29.14 ng/ml, 23 cc residual gland; (2) 61-year-old man, Gleason 4+4 in TURP chips, cT3bN0M0; (3) 60-year-old man, cT3bN1M0, also post-TURP; (4) 79-year-old man, intermediate-risk disease, prominent median lobe, chronic immune thrombocytopenia, deep narrow pelvis.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nThe video demonstrates sharp anterior dissection through an obliterated space of Retzius and definition of a reconstructed prostatovesical junction (1); identification of ureteric orifices drawn toward a cicatrised bladder neck before posterior division, and confirmation of the true urethral lumen at the apex (2); side-specific extrafascial plane selection with complete seminal vesicle excision where tumour abutted the anterior rectum (3); and early posterior access with apical dissection in a restricted envelope, median lobe distorting the trigone (4). Preparation shown alongside includes an interval for inflammation to resolve, three months (1) and twelve weeks (2); MRI pelvimetry with pelvic cavity index and membranous urethral length, and haematological optimisation (4). All were completed robotically without conversion or complication;<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nDifficulty lay in dissection rather than resection. Each modification shown depended on preparation made before docking and determined what had to be anticipated after. RARP is best regarded not as one fixed operation but as a technique adapted to the field in which it is performed.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-74\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 74</p><h3>Robotic Ureteral Tapering and Lich-Gregoir Ureteric Reimplantation in a Child Using the SSI Mantra™ Surgical Robotic System: A Minimalist Three-Port, Drainless Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr R Kuldeep, Dr N Srivastava, Dr A Bajpai ,Dr A Kumar, Dr R Lohani, Dr A Sengar ,Dr S Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Precision urology hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Pediatric megaureter with high-grade vesicoureteric reflux (VUR) requiring surgical correction presents a reconstructive challenge demanding exceptional precision in both ureteral tapering and antireflux reimplantation. The Lich-Gregoir extravesical ureteric reimplantation is a well-established technique offering excellent outcomes with minimal morbidity. Robot-assisted surgery enhances precision through superior magnification, articulated instrumentation, and tremor filtration, particularly valuable in the confined operative field of a pediatric abdomen. We report a novel minimalist robotic approach using the indigenously developed SSI Mantra™ system incorporating unique technical refinements.<br/>\nCase Description and Technique: A 6-year-old male child presenting with right-sided Grade V vesicoureteric reflux and recurrent urinary tract infections underwent robotic ureteral tapering and Lich-Gregoir reimplantation. The procedure was performed using a three-port robotic configuration, with one port serving the dual purpose of robotic instrument access and suction, eliminating an additional assistant port and minimizing abdominal wall trauma — particularly relevant in a small pediatric abdomen. Monopolar hook was exclusively used for meticulous, precise, and bloodless ureteral dissection. Ureteral tapering was performed intracorporeally, followed by tension-free extravesical Lich-Gregoir antireflux reimplantation. No drain was placed at procedure conclusion.<br/>\nOutcome: The procedure was completed entirely robotically without conversion. Operative time was 102 minutes with minimal analgesic requirement (ketorolac 50 mg). Hospital stay was 4 days and urethral catheter was removed at day 6. DJ stent was removed at 3 weeks postoperatively. Micturating cystourethrogram at 2 months revealed a morphologically normal bladder with complete resolution of Grade V VUR, confirming successful reimplantation.<br/>\nConclusion: This case demonstrates the feasibility and safety of SSI Mantra™ robotic ureteral tapering and Lich-Gregoir reimplantation in a pediatric patient with high-grade VUR, using an innovative three-port, drainless, monopolar hook technique, offering a reproducible minimalist framework for complex pediatric ureteric reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-75\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 75</p><h3>Managing a lower-pole crossing vessel in Pediatric Robotic Pyeloplasty: A Renopexy and Vascular Hitch approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Digvijay Singh Tanwar, Dr Shiv Charan Navriya, Dr Chintapali Ravi Chandra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo demonstrate the technique of robot-assisted laparoscopic Anderson-Hynes dismembered pyeloplasty combined with renopexy and a Hellstrom vascular hitch for management of ureteropelvic junction obstruction (UPJO) with a lower-pole crossing vessel in a young child.</p><p>Methods<br/>\nA 3-year-old male, incidentally diagnosed with left UPJO during evaluation for right inguinal hernia repair, was asymptomatic with no history of pain, hematuria, or urinary tract infection. Ultrasonography showed left hydronephrosis (APD 30 mm); NCCT KUB confirmed moderate hydronephrosis without calculus. Diuretic renography (DTPA) revealed a grossly hydronephrotic left kidney with mildly impaired perfusion, preserved differential function (59% left, 41% right), and delayed but significant clearance. He underwent robot-assisted laparoscopic left Anderson-Hynes dismembered pyeloplasty. Intraoperatively, a malrotated kidney with high ureteric insertion and a lower-pole crossing vessel anterior to the PUJ were identified. Renopexy to the lateral abdominal wall was performed to medialize the kidney and create a gravity-dependent, tension-free anastomosis, and a Hellstrom vascular hitch was applied to preserve the crossing vessel before completing the dismembered pyeloplasty over a 3.5Fr DJ stent.</p><p>Results<br/>\nOperative time was 3 hours with blood loss &lt;10 mL. Postoperative course was uneventful apart from transient abdominal distension on POD1, managed conservatively. Oral feeds resumed POD2; urethral catheter removed POD2 with good voiding; abdominal drain removed POD3. Wound was healthy (Southampton Grade 0). The patient was discharged on POD3 with stable vitals and DJ stent in situ.</p><p>Conclusions<br/>\nRobot-assisted laparoscopic pyeloplasty with renopexy and a Hellstrom vascular hitch is safe and technically feasible in young children with a crossing vessel, achieving vessel preservation and a dependent, tension-free anastomosis without vessel transposition or division.</p><p>Conflict of Interest<br/>\nNo conflict of interest to disclose.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-76\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 76</p><h3>Secondary Pelvi-Ureteric Junction Obstruction: Outcome analysis after laparoscopic reconstructive salvage surgery — A 5-Year Institutional Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr R Kuldeep Dr N Srivastava Dr A Bajpai Dr A Kumar Dr R Lohani Dr A Sengar Dr S Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Precision Urology Hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nSecondary pelvi-ureteric junction obstruction (PUJO) represents a surgically challenging entity. Unlike primary PUJO, secondary cases demand individualized reconstructive strategies, often requiring complex procedures beyond standard pyeloplasty. Minimally invasive approaches have emerged as effective salvage options, yet data on laparoscopic and robotic management of secondary PUJO remains limited.<br/>\nObjective:<br/>\nThe primary objective of this video abstract is to illustrate key technical steps and decision-making across this challenging cohort. The secondary objective is to evaluate outcomes of laparoscopic and robotic reconstruction in all cases of secondary PUJO presenting to our institution over a 5-year period.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods:<br/>\nA retrospective review was conducted of 21 consecutive patients with secondary PUJO managed between 2019 and 2024. All cases underwent minimally invasive repair — 19 laparoscopically and 2 robotically. Procedures performed included dismembered Re-do pyeloplasty (n=12),buccal mucosa graft ureteroplasty(n=2) ureterocalycostomy with warm ischaemia (n=6), and ureterocalycostomy with cold ischaemia (n=1). Operative parameters, complications, and functional outcomes were recorded. Success was defined as symptomatic resolution associated with stable or improved renal function and improved washout from the renal pelvis (i.e. T1/2 &lt; 20 min) on renal scan.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nMean age was 32±8.2 years, laterality left: right (14:7), no open conversion, hemoglobin drop of 1.4±0.8 grm%, and hospital stay of 4 ±1.3 days. Overall success rate was 85.7% (18/21). In the pyeloplasty group, two patient (16.6%) had radiological and symptomatic failure requiring re-intervention. In the ureterocalycostomy group, one patient (14.2%) developed recurrent urinary tract infections with deteriorating renal function, ultimately necessitating nephrectomy.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic and robotic reconstruction for secondary PUJO is feasible and effective, with good overall success rates. Ureterocalycostomy under cold or warm ischaemia extends the reconstructive armamentarium for salvage in complex failed cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-77\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 77</p><h3>ROBOTIC PARTIAL NEPHRECTOMY WITH SSI MANTRA</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr R Mongha</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Marengo Asia Hospital, Faridabad</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nRobotic partial nephrectomy (RPN) has emerged as an important nephron-sparing approach for the management of localized renal tumors. The evolution of robotic technology has expanded access to minimally invasive renal surgery, with indigenous platforms such as the SSI Mantra offering an alternative to established robotic systems. We present our experience with robotic partial nephrectomy using the SSI Mantra system and discuss the evolution of robotic renal surgery.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nWe describe the surgical technique and perioperative experience of robotic partial nephrectomy performed using the SSI Mantra robotic surgical system. Key steps including patient positioning, port placement, renal mobilization, tumor localization, hilar control, tumor excision, renorrhaphy, and specimen retrieval are demonstrated through operative video. Particular emphasis is placed on robotic instrument articulation, three-dimensional visualization, precision of dissection, and the learning curve associated with transitioning to an indigenous robotic platform. Perioperative outcomes, including operative time, warm ischemia time, estimated blood loss, complications, and postoperative recovery, are reviewed.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe SSI Mantra platform facilitates precise dissection and suturing during nephron-sparing surgery while providing the advantages of minimally invasive access. Progressive experience has allowed refinement of port placement, hilar dissection, tumor excision, and renorrhaphy, contributing to improved procedural efficiency. The evolution from conventional laparoscopic partial nephrectomy to robotic surgery represents a significant advancement in renal cancer surgery, particularly in managing technically challenging tumors.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRobotic partial nephrectomy with the SSI Mantra demonstrates the expanding capabilities of indigenous robotic technology in complex renal surgery. With increasing experience and refinement of technique, the platform has the potential to make advanced robotic nephron-sparing surgery more accessible while maintaining the principles of oncological control and preservation of renal function.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-78\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 78</p><h3>ROBOT ASSISTED RADICAL PROSTATECTOMY – NERVE SPARING (HOOD TECHNIQUE)</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shivam Upadhayaya</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS RISHIKESH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>### Introduction and Objectives</p><p>Urinary continence after radical prostatectomy depends on preservation of the urethral sphincter complex, supporting musculofascial structures, innervation, urethral length, and bladder neck–urethral configuration. The Hood technique is an anterior approach to robot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP) that preserves the Retzius space contents, including the detrusor apron, arcus tendineus, puboprostatic ligaments, anterior vessels, and detrusor muscle fibers, thereby supporting the urethral sphincter complex.</p><p>### Materials and Methods</p><p>A 66-year-old man with intermediate-risk localized prostate cancer (PSA 11.1 ng/mL) and a 29 × 30 mm left transitional-zone lesion with an intact capsule underwent nerve-sparing RARP using the Hood technique.</p><p>### Results</p><p>The lateral pedicles were controlled athermally, developing a plane between the prostatic capsule and Denonvilliers’ fascia while preserving the neurovascular bundles. An anterior plane was then developed between the detrusor apron and anterior fibromuscular layer up to the apex. The anterior urethra was sharply divided and the prostate removed. Following urethrovesical anastomosis, the arcus tendineus was sutured to the detrusor muscle to reconstruct the hood. The postoperative course was uneventful, and the Foley catheter was removed on day 10. At 2-month follow-up, the patient was voiding well and fully continent. Wagaskar et al. reported continence rates of 21%, 36%, 83%, 88%, 91%, 94%, and 95% at 1, 2, 4, 6, 12, 24, and 48 weeks, respectively.</p><p>### Conclusion</p><p>The Hood technique preserves anterior musculofascial structures supporting the urethral sphincter complex and may facilitate earlier recovery of urinary continence while maintaining sound oncological principles.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-79\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 79</p><h3>Freehand Transperineal Prostate Biopsy: A Simplified PUH Technique</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr R Kuldeep Dr N Srivastava Dr A Bajpai Dr A Kumar Dr R Lohani Dr A Sengar Dr S Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Precision urology hospital</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: Transperineal prostate biopsy is re-emerging as safer alternative to transrectal ultrasonography (TRUS)-guided biopsy, owing to its lower risk of infective complications. Widespread adoption remains limited by increased procedural time, training requirements, financial constraints, the need for high-grade anaesthesia, limited availability of dedicated equipment such as stepper devices and grid templates. We describe a simplified, freehand transperineal biopsy technique performed under local anaesthesia in day-care clinic setting and report our initial experience.<br/>\nMethods: Fifteen patients (mean age 67.7 ± years; mean PSA 11.5 ng/mL; mean prostate volume 46.3 cc) with elevated PSA and/or suspicious imaging findings underwent freehand transperineal prostate biopsy. The procedure was performed under pudendal nerve block, with catheter in situ, using a uniplanar ultrasound probe in sagittal view in dorsal lithotomy position. A single-use sterile needle guide was mounted on the probe at the 12 o'clock position and fixed closely abutting the perineal skin to visualize the urethra. The probe-guide assembly was rotated to either side to visualize the prostate, and standard 12-core biopsies were obtained under direct ultrasound guidance. A compression dressing was applied and removed after 6 hours, followed by observation prior to discharge.<br/>\nResults: All 15 procedures were completed successfully under local anaesthesia alone, with mean procedure time of 15.3 minutes and mean patient-reported pain score of 2.1/10 (VAS). A mean of 12 cores were obtained per patient, with adequate tissue yield in all cases (100%). Malignancy was detected in 9 of 15 patients (60%). No patient developed post-biopsy sepsis or major infective complication; minor self-limiting events (transient haematuria, perineal bruising) occurred in 3 patients (20%).<br/>\nConclusion: This simplified freehand transperineal biopsy technique is safe, reproducible, well-tolerated, cost-effective, achieving good diagnostic yield with zero sepsis, minimal morbidity. It eliminates the need for expensive stepper/grid equipment ,high-grade anaesthesia, supporting its adoption in routine outpatient practice.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-80\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 80</p><h3>Robotic Partial Nephrectomy for an Incidentally Detected Moderate-Complexity Renal Cell Carcinoma Following Acute Necrotizing Pancreatitis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Kapil Dubey , Dr Kapil Garg , Dr Rajneesh Kumar, Dr Sahil Data , Dr Manav suryavanshi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Amrita Institute Of Medical Sciences</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims:<br/>\nThe widespread use of cross-sectional imaging has led to an increase in the incidental detection of small renal masses. Robotic partial nephrectomy (RAPN) is the preferred nephron-sparing treatment for localized renal tumors, offering excellent oncological and functional outcomes. We report the successful management of an incidentally detected moderate-complexity renal mass in a patient recovering from severe acute necrotizing pancreatitis.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nA 52-year-old male undergoing evaluation for severe acute necrotizing pancreatitis was found to have a partially exophytic enhancing right interpolar renal mass measuring 2.2 × 1.7 × 1.6 cm. The lesion had a RENAL nephrometry score of 9p, with preserved bilateral renal function on DTPA scan. After multidisciplinary evaluation and optimization, the patient underwent robot-assisted right partial nephrectomy. Intraoperative ultrasound was utilized for tumour localization and margin delineation. . The pelvicalyceal system was entered during tumour excision and repaired primarily. Tumour excision, pelvicalyceal system repair, and two-layer renorhaphy were performed using standard robotic techniques.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nWarm ischemia time was 16 minutes, and console time was 150 minutes. Abdominal drain was removed on POD 4 and patient discharged. HPR demonstrated clear cell renal cell carcinoma, WHO/ISUP Grade 2, measuring 2.5 cm, confined to the kidney with negative surgical margins. Pathological stage was pT1aNxMx.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRobotic partial nephrectomy was performed in patient after 3 months of pancreatitis episode. Patient behaved well physiologically although intra-abdominal inflammation was present intra-operatively. Hence this surgery should not be a contra-indication in patient with pancreatitis. Careful patient selection, intraoperative ultrasonography, meticulous hilar control, and standardized renorrhaphy contributed to excellent perioperative, functional, and oncological outcomes.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-81\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 81</p><h3>Navigating Vascular Complications During Living Donor Nephrectomy: A Single-Center Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Bhagwati Chandra Verma,Dr Anuj Kumar Yadav,Dr Deepak Rathi,Dr Prasun Ghosh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Medanta The Medicity</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nLiving donor nephrectomy is generally safe; however, intraoperative vascular injury is a potentially life-threatening complication that may result in significant blood loss, donor morbidity. Early recognition, rapid vascular control, and appropriate repair are essential. We present five cases of vascular injury during donor nephrectomy, focusing on management and key learning points.<br/>\nMethods:<br/>\nWe retrospectively reviewed five cases of intraoperative vascular injury encountered during living donor nephrectomy at a renal transplant center. Data regarding donor characteristics, side of nephrectomy, type and site of vascular injury, mechanism, intraoperative management, conversion to open surgery, blood loss, transfusion requirements, postoperative complications, and recipient graft outcomes were evaluated.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nFive vascular injuries were identified during donor nephrectomy, involving the renal hilum or major renal vessels. Injuries occurred during hilar dissection, mobilization, or vascular control. All injuries were recognized intraoperatively, allowing immediate proximal and distal vascular control. Management included direct vascular repair, ligation of injured accessory vessels, and conversion to an open approach when safe minimally invasive control could not be achieved. Prompt team communication facilitated rapid decision-making. Despite significant intraoperative bleeding in some cases, all donors were successfully managed without mortality or major long-term morbidity. All renal grafts were successfully implanted.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nVascular injury during living donor nephrectomy requires immediate and decisive management. Key learning points include meticulous hilar dissection, identification of accessory and variant vessels, controlled application of clips or stapling devices, and a clear operative field. Early recognition and proximal vascular control are critical to prevent uncontrolled hemorrhage. Surgeons should maintain a low threshold for conversion to open surgery. Availability of vascular instruments, preparedness for reconstruction, and close coordination with the transplant team are essential for donor safety. Timely conversion, when required, should be considered a safety measure rather than a failure of minimally invasive surgery.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-82\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 82</p><h3>Right Radical Nephrectomy with IVC Thrombectomy</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Ritesh Mongha</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Marengo Asia Hospital, Faridabad, Haryana</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nTo demonstrate a step-by-step robotic right radical nephrectomy technique in a patient with a large right renal mass with IVC thrombus<br/>\nMethod<br/>\nA 46 year old male presented with an pain abdomen with right renal mass. Contrast-enhanced CT confirmed a right renal mass (cT3bN2M0). The patient underwent a transperitoneal robotic radical nephrectomy using the SSI Mantra 3 robotic system. Key surgical steps highlighted in the video include: (1) colon mobilization and exposure of the retroperitoneum, (3) identification and safe isolation of the renal vein prior to arterial control, and (4) en bloc specimen retrieval with meticulous hemostasis with lymphadenectomy and IVC thrombectomy.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nTotal operative time was 145 minutes, with an estimated blood loss of 100 mL. There were no intraoperative complications. The patient was discharged on postoperative day 3 without events. At follow-up, the patient remains disease-free with stable renal function.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nRobotic radical nephrectomy is a safe, effective, and reproducible approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-83\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 83</p><h3>Robotic Ureterocalycostomy for Complex Recurrent PUJ Stricture After Open Pyelolithotomy: A Challenging Salvage Reconstruction — Video Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR MANJEET KUMAR, DR ARJUN SHARMA, DR GIRISH SHARMA, DR PAWAN KAUNDAL, DR PAMPOSH RAINA, DR KAILASH CHANDER BARWAL</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIMSS, SHIMLA HP INDIA</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Robotic Ureterocalycostomy for Recurrent PUJ Stricture Following Open Pyelolithotomy: A Video Presentation</p><p>Introduction: Recurrent pelvi-ureteric junction (PUJ) obstruction following previous renal surgery can present a challenging reconstructive scenario. Extensive fibrosis and an unfavorable renal pelvis may preclude conventional pyeloplasty. Ureterocalycostomy provides a dependent drainage pathway and is an established salvage option in selected cases.</p><p>Case: We present a video of robotic-assisted left ureterocalycostomy in a 45-year-old female with recurrent PUJ stricture following previous open pyelolithotomy performed at a private hospital.</p><p>Technique: The video demonstrates robotic mobilization of the left kidney and proximal ureter, meticulous dissection through the postoperative fibrotic field, identification of a suitable lower-pole calyx, and preparation of the ureter. The proximal ureter is spatulated and anastomosed to the lower-pole calyx in a tension-free, mucosa-to-mucosa fashion. Intracorporeal suturing and placement of a ureteric stent are demonstrated.</p><p>Outcome: The procedure was completed successfully without major intraoperative complications. The postoperative recovery was uneventful, with satisfactory drainage of the left renal unit and improvement in hydronephrosis on follow-up.</p><p>Conclusion: Robotic ureterocalycostomy is a technically feasible salvage procedure for complex or recurrent PUJ obstruction after previous renal surgery. This video highlights the operative steps, technical considerations, and utility of the robotic platform in reconstructive surgery within a scarred operative field.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-84\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 84</p><h3>Synchronous Robot-assisted Left cortical-sparing adrenalectomy and interaortocaval paraganglioma excision in von Hippel-Lindau disease</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Ujjwal Das, Mahendra Singh, Deepak Prakash Bhirud, Shiv Charan Navriya, Gautam Ram Choudhary, Arjun Singh Sandhu</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS,Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objectives: Pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas (PPGLs) in von Hippel–Lindau (VHL) disease are frequently multifocal. When the contralateral adrenal has already been removed, the remaining gland is the sole source of cortisol, and total adrenalectomy commits the patient to lifelong steroid dependence and adrenal-crisis risk—making cortical-sparing surgery preferable. A coexisting extra-adrenal paraganglioma adds a second catecholamine-secreting target. We present a single-anaesthetic, dual-position robotic strategy addressing both while preserving adrenal function.<br/>\nMethods: A man in his early 20s with genetically confirmed VHL disease and prior childhood contralateral adrenalectomy presented with paroxysmal catecholamine symptoms. ¹⁸F-FDOPA PET/CT showed two functioning nodules in the solitary adrenal (SUVmax 31.3, 25.7) and an interaortocaval paraganglioma at T12–L1 (SUVmax 12.5), with elevated plasma normetanephrine. After alpha-blockade and optimisation, surgery proceeded in two sequential robotic phases under one anaesthetic: cortical-sparing enucleation preserving a vascularised rim, then repositioning and re-docking for interaortocaval paraganglioma excision behind the left renal vein.<br/>\nResults: Completed robotically in 4.5 hours; 50 mL blood loss; no conversion. All lesions were excised with clear margins (two pheochromocytomas, one paraganglioma). Postoperative bradycardia and ventricular ectopy were self-limiting (Clavien–Dindo II). The patient was discharged on day 4 on a weaning steroid regimen, with normal metanephrines and preserved adrenal function at six weeks.<br/>\nConclusions: Synchronous intra-adrenal and extra-adrenal PPGL disease in VHL can be safely managed in a single anaesthetic via a planned intraoperative position change, sparing a second operation and a second high-risk catecholamine anaesthetic while preserving steroid independence</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-85\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 85</p><h3>Laparoscopic Chylo-Lymphatic Disconnection for Chyluria in a Pelvic Solitary Ectopic Kidney: A Surgical Challenge and Video Presentation</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Faizan Ahmad, Nalin Shrivastava, Achint Bajpai, Anurodh Kumar, Ravi Lohan, Aditya PS Sengar, Shashikant Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PRECISION UROLOGY HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Chyluria, caused by abnormal lymphatico-pelvic fistulae, is an uncommon but debilitating condition most frequently attributed to filarial infection. Renal pedicle lymphatic disconnection is a proven nephron-sparing surgical treatment with reported success rates of 95–100% and recurrence rates of 0–3.8%. While silver nitrate sclerotherapy is often attempted first, its outcomes remain unpredictable with variable long-term success. In a solitary kidney, sclerotherapy carries additional concerns of chemical nephritis and renal function impairment, making primary surgical intervention a more prudent and justifiable approach.</p><p>Case Description: We present chyluria in a patient with a pelvic solitary ectopic kidney in whom sclerotherapy was deliberately avoided given its unpredictable outcomes and nephrotoxic risks in a solitary functioning kidney. Preoperative CT angiography delineated the anomalous pelvic renal vascular anatomy and hilar lymphatic channels, facilitating precise surgical planning. Laparoscopic chylo-lymphatic disconnection was performed via a transperitoneal three-port approach (one 10 mm, two 5 mm), involving careful identification of the ectopic renal hilum, precision skeletonization of the renal pedicle under magnification, and selective ligation and clipping of abnormal lymphatico-pelvic fistulae while safeguarding the renal artery, renal vein, and adjacent iliac vasculature.</p><p>Outcome: The procedure was completed without conversion. Mean operative time was 65 minutes with minimal analgesic requirement (tramadol 50 mg) and hospital stay of 3 days. Pelvic drain was removed at POD2. Chyluria resolution was immediate, with urine showing no chyle at POD1 and POD3. Haemoglobin drop was 0.4 g% with preserved renal function at discharge.</p><p>Conclusion: This video demonstrates that CT angiography-guided laparoscopic chylo-lymphatic disconnection is technically feasible, safe, and highly effective in a pelvic solitary ectopic kidney. Primary surgical intervention should be strongly considered as the preferred definitive strategy, superseding sclerotherapy in this anatomically challenging and high-stakes setting.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-86\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 86</p><h3>Contemporary utilization of Laparoscopic stone removal in the era of Endourology: Single institution experience.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Faizan Ahmad, Nalin Shrivastava, Achint Bajpai, Anurodh Kumar, Ravi Lohan, Aditya PS Sengar, Shashikant Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PRECISION UROLOGY HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nLaparoscopic stone removal remains an important surgical option for urolithiasis in anatomically complex and clinically challenging situations. In the contemporary endourological era, selected patients with complex stone disease may require laparoscopy when endourological approaches are infeasible, unsuccessful, or unlikely to achieve satisfactory clearance.</p><p>Objective:<br/>\nTo evaluate the indications, feasibility, safety, and outcomes of laparoscopic stone removal in a heterogeneous cohort of complex urolithiasis. This video highlights technical nuances across seven challenging clinical scenarios and demonstrates the versatility of laparoscopy in individualized stone management.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nPatients undergoing laparoscopic stone removal between April 2020 and April 2026 were retrospectively evaluated. Thirty-two patients underwent laparoscopic stone surgery, including 20 laparoscopic pyelolithotomies (LP) and 12 laparoscopic ureterolithotomies (LU). The LP group included patients undergoing concomitant reconstructive procedures (n=4), pelvic ectopic kidney with calculi (n=3), bilateral stones in anomalous kidneys (n=3), failed previous endourological procedures (n=6), large-burden stones (n=2), and difficult pelvicalyceal anatomy (n=2). The LU group comprised eight patients with ureteric stones &gt;3 cm and four patients requiring simultaneous management of ureteric stricture. All procedures were performed transperitoneally under general anaesthesia. Operative time, blood loss, stone clearance, complications, and hospital stay were assessed. Concomitant procedures included pyeloplasty, Boari flap reconstruction, and ureteric reimplantation.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nAll 32 procedures were successfully completed laparoscopically without conversion to open surgery. Complete stone clearance was achieved in all patients. Mean operative time was 95 ± 18 minutes, with mean estimated blood loss of 85 ± 30 mL. No major intraoperative complications occurred. Hospital stay ranged from 4–6 days, and all patients had an uneventful postoperative recovery without 30-day readmission.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic stone removal is a safe, effective, and versatile approach for selected complex renal and ureteric calculi, particularly when endourological techniques are unsuitable or have failed.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-87\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 87</p><h3>Laparoscopic Ureteric reconstruction for unilateral double ureteric stricture: PUH experience.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Faizan Ahmad, Nalin Shrivastava, Achint Bajpai, Anurodh Kumar, Ravi Lohan, Aditya PS Sengar, Shashikant Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PRECISION UROLOGY HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Case report: Laparoscopic Ureteric reconstruction for unilateral double ureteric stricture: PUH experience.<br/>\nDr Faizan Ahmad, Nalin Shrivastava, Achint Bajpai, Anurodh Kumar, Ravi Lohan, Aditya PS Sengar, Shashikant Mishra<br/>\nBackground: Double ureteric strictures affecting both upper and lower segments of a single ureter represent a rare and complex urological condition requiring sophisticated reconstructive techniques. Such cases challenge conventional surgical approaches and demand comprehensive preoperative planning.<br/>\nMaterial and Methods: A 45-year-old male patient with preserved renal function presented with right-sided double ureteric strictures involving the upper and mid-ureteric segments. Preoperative imaging demonstrated short stenotic segments causing significant hydronephrosis with compromised renal function. The patient had undergone repeated double-J stent placements over two years before definitive surgical reconstruction was planned.<br/>\nSurgical Technique: Laparoscopic transperitoneal reconstruction was performed using five ports in two distinct patient positions. The upper ureteric stricture was managed via ureteroureterostomy in the right lateral position, involving excision of the stenotic segment followed by tension-free anastomosis of healthy ureteric ends. The mid-ureteric stricture was carefully dissected while preserving medial vascular supply. Patient positioning was changed to steep Trendelenburg for Boari flap creation. The bladder flap was anastomosed to the lower ureteric end in the right lateral position, effectively bridging the defective lower segment.<br/>\nResults: The procedure was completed laparoscopically in 150 minutes. Postoperative recovery was uneventful with minimal analgesic requirements (150mg diclofenac), drain removal on postoperative day three, and hospital discharge on day five. Urethral catheter removal occurred on day seven, with double-J stent removal at three weeks. Three-month follow-up CT intravenous pyelography confirmed successful drainage, improved hydronephrosis, and preserved renal function.<br/>\nConclusion: This case demonstrates the feasibility of combining ureteroureterostomy with Boari flap reconstruction for ipsilateral double ureteric strictures, offering excellent functional outcomes while avoiding urinary diversion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-88\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 88</p><h3>Robot-assisted radical nephroureterectomy with bladder cuff excision for upper moiety urothelial carcinoma in a left duplex collecting system</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Abhishek Jain, Dr Shivam Priyadarshi, Dr Govind Sharma, Dr Somendra Bansal, Dr Mohit Sethi</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">SMS Superspeciality Hospital Jaipur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nUpper tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC) arising in a duplex collecting system is exceedingly rare, and no consensus exists on the optimal surgical approach. We demonstrate the feasibility and technique of robot-assisted radical nephroureterectomy (RANU) with bladder cuff excision for upper-moiety UTUC in a left duplex kidney.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 76-year-old chronic smoker presented with five months of painless gross haematuria. Serum creatinine was 1.5 mg/dL and urine cytology showed high-grade atypical urothelial cells. CT urography revealed a left duplex system with double ureters, gross upper-moiety hydroureteronephrosis with cortical thinning and non-excretion, and irregular polypoidal enhancing soft tissue along the upper-moiety pelvicalyceal system and ureter; metastatic work-up was negative. Cystoscopy confirmed two left orifices obeying the Weigert-Meyer rule; upper-moiety retrograde pyelography showed a non-negotiable obstruction at the mid-ureter, and a lower-moiety double-J stent was placed as an intraoperative landmark. RANU was performed the following day in right lateral decubitus using Da Vinci Xi. Steps demonstrated: Colonic medialisation, hilar dissection revealing a single left renal artery bifurcating early to supply both moieties, control of the common pedicle, En bloc mobilisation of both ureters to the bladder, extravesical bladder cuff excision, lymphadenectomy, and Endobag retrieval.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nOperative time was 130 minutes; estimated blood loss 100 mL. No intraoperative or postoperative complications occurred. The drain was removed on postoperative day 3 and the patient discharged on day 4. Histopathology confirmed high-grade urothelial carcinoma of the upper moiety, pT3NX, with negative margins.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nRANU with bladder cuff excision is safe and reproducible in a duplex kidney. A shared early-bifurcating pedicle and dual ureters make moiety-sparing surgery unsafe in high-risk disease; preoperative CT angiography and lower-moiety stenting are valuable adjuncts, and robotic wristed instrumentation facilitates en bloc distal ureterectomy with single bladder cuff excision.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-89\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 89</p><h3>Standardizing Intracorporeal Bricker Ileal Conduit During Robotic Radical Cystectomy: Technical Nuances for a Reproducible Technique</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Manav Suryavanshi, Sahil Data, Rajnish Kumar, Kapil Garg, Kapil Dubey</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Amrita Institute of Medical Sciences and Research Centre, Faridabad, Haryana, India</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nIntracorporeal ileal conduit reconstruction remains one of the most technically demanding steps of robotic radical cystectomy. We present a standardized technique highlighting practical nuances that facilitate reproducibility and simplify intracorporeal Bricker diversion.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nFollowing robotic radical cystectomy after macroscopically complete TURBT resection of a large friable necrotic bladder tumour with keratinising squamous cell carcinoma, and standard-template pelvic lymphadenectomy, intracorporeal Bricker diversion was performed using a minimal instrument strategy comprising Hot Shears, Cadiere forceps, Vessel Sealer, Tip-Up fenestrated grasper, and a single needle holder. A 20-cm ileal segment approximately 25 cm proximal to the ileocecal junction was isolated. The left ureter was transposed behind the sigmoid mesocolon to the right side to facilitate a tension-free diversion.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nBowel continuity was restored using stapled ileo-ileal anastomosis. Bilateral uretero-ileal anastomoses were fashioned using the Bricker technique with continuous 4-0 PDS sutures over 6 Fr × 28 cm double-J stents. Frozen sections from ureteric margins were negative. Stoma construction was standardized by ensuring adequate aperture using three-finger calibration. Four-point fascial fixation was performed using sheath-to-Lambert sutures, followed by stoma nipple maturation using 2-0 Vicryl sutures. No conduit Foley catheter was utilized, and only a single pelvic drain was placed. Estimated blood loss was &lt;300 mL, console time was 5 hours 40 minutes, and postoperative recovery was uneventful with discharge on postoperative day 5. Final histopathology: Squamous cell carcinoma, pT3b N0 Mx . Lymph node status: 0/14<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nIntracorporeal Bricker diversion can be distilled into a sequence of reproducible steps using a minimal instrument approach. Emphasis on ureteral orientation, standardized stoma construction, and simplified drainage strategies facilitates safe execution and may improve the reproducibility of robotic urinary diversion.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-90\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 90</p><h3>Out-Dyed: Emphasizing the importance of Intraoperative Dye test in Urethrovaginal Fistula</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Nikhil Khattar, Dr Gautam Banga</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">The Perineo Urology Clinic</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim: to demonstrate the importance of intraoperative dye test in a urethrovaginal fistula surgery where generally one would omit performing it<br/>\nMethods: A 37 year female had an instrumental delivery more than 15 years ago and since then continued of leakage which was missed in her many gynecological exams, one CT urography and two cystoscopies. Scopy however revealed a single urethrovaginal fistula which was closed and intraoperative dye test was performed which revealed another hidden fistula. Surgery was completed after closure of this missed fistula and providing second layer cover with Martius flap.<br/>\nResults: Catheter was removed three weeks later. At three months the patient is voiding well with mild stress incontinence<br/>\nConclusion: Intraoperative reassessment with dye test can save from a missed fistula even in urethrovaginal fistula repair</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-91\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 91</p><h3>Robotic Radical Cystectomy in a Multiply Operated Female Pelvis: Technical Strategies for Safe Completion</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Manav Suryavanshi, Rajnish Kumar, Sahil Data, Kapil Dubey, Kapil Garg</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Amrita Institute of Medical Sciences and Research Centre, Faridabad, Haryana, India</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nRobotic radical cystectomy (RRC) in females with multiple prior pelvic surgeries is technically challenging because of dense adhesions, distorted anatomy, and obliterated tissue planes. We demonstrate strategies enabling safe robotic completion of radical cystectomy in a hostile female pelvis while maintaining oncological principles.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 50-year-old female presented with recurrent gross haematuria. Imaging demonstrated a 5.2 × 3.9 cm right lateral wall bladder mass with adjacent perivesical fat stranding suggestive of locally advanced disease. Initial TURBT achieved macroscopically complete resection of a large friable necrotic bladder tumour, and histopathology revealed keratinising squamous cell carcinoma. The patient had undergone previous suprapubic cystolithotomy, two lower segment caesarean sections, and hysterectomy. Robotic radical cystectomy with extended pelvic lymphadenectomy was planned. Initial laparoscopic access and port placement were challenging because of dense adhesions, necessitating meticulous adhesiolysis before docking.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nDense omental adhesions, a severely plastered bladder, and loss of normal pelvic planes were encountered. Bilateral ovaries were excised en bloc with the specimen, and the dissection plane was deliberately developed inferior to the ovaries to facilitate distal ureteric dissection up to the vesicoureteric junction. Distal ureteric frozen sections were negative. Extended pelvic lymphadenectomy was performed according to the standard radical cystectomy template with preservation of both obturator nerves. The specimen was retrieved transvaginally, followed by vaginal vault repair. Estimated blood loss was &lt;300 mL, console time was 5 hours 40 minutes, and no conversion was required. The postoperative course was uneventful, with discharge on postoperative day 5. Final histopathology: Squamous cell carcinoma, pT3b N0 Mx . Lymph node status:0/14<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nA hostile female pelvis should not be considered a contraindication to robotic radical cystectomy. Careful access, systematic adhesiolysis, strategic development of alternative tissue planes, and adherence to oncological principles permit safe completion of robotic surgery in selected complex cases.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-92\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 92</p><h3>Catecholamine Storm to Robotic Precision: Managing Bilateral Large Pheochromocytomas with Large Paraaortic Paraganglioma in VHL Syndrome</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Manish Gupta</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">MGMCH,</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Background:<br/>\nVon Hippel–Lindau (VHL) syndrome is an inherited multisystem tumor syndrome associated with pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma (PPGL). Synchronous bilateral adrenal pheochromocytomas with an extra-adrenal para-aortic paraganglioma represent a rare and technically challenging presentation, particularly when minimally invasive surgery is contemplated.</p><p>Case Presentation:</p><p>32yr old female patient presented with a 2-month history of recurrent palpitations, excessive perspiration, and dyspnea on exertion during evaluation for hypertension. Ultrasonography revealed bilateral adrenal masses. Triple-phase CECT abdomen with adrenal protocol demonstrated a left adrenal mass measuring 6.4 × 6.5 × 6.1 cm, a right adrenal mass measuring 4.0 × 2.5 cm, and a left para-aortic paraganglioma measuring 3.0 × 3.7 × 3.9 cm. Biochemical evaluation showed elevated serum free metanephrine (113.16 pg/mL) and markedly elevated serum free normetanephrine (&gt;2000 pg/mL), confirming a functioning PPGL.<br/>\nIn view of the multifocal disease, comprehensive genetic and systemic evaluation was performed and was suggestive of VHL syndrome. Following appropriate preoperative alpha-adrenergic blockade and multidisciplinary optimization, the patient underwent robotic excision of the bilateral adrenal tumors with robotic resection of the left para-aortic paraganglioma.</p><p>Surgical Technique:</p><p>The video demonstrates the key steps of the robotic procedure, including patient positioning and port placement, meticulous identification and control of the adrenal and para-aortic vascular anatomy, early vascular control, careful tumor dissection, preservation of critical surrounding structures. Particular emphasis is placed on the technical challenges of managing bilateral adrenal pheochromocytomas and an extra-adrenal para-aortic paraganglioma in a single minimally invasive surgical setting.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe robotic procedure was successfully completed without intraoperative complications. All three lesions were excised with satisfactory hemostasis and preservation of adjacent vital structures. The postoperative course was uneventful, with appropriate blood pressure stabilization and no major morbidity.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nhis video highlights the technical feasibility and surgical nuances of robotic management of multifocal VHL-associated pheochromocytoma–paraganglioma.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-93\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 93</p><h3>Beyond the Robot: Feasibility of Totally Laparoscopic Anterior Pelvic Exenteration with Orthotopic Neobladder Using Modified Studer Pouch in Carcinoma Urinary Bladder</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rahul Anand</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, JODHPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo evaluate the technical feasibility, safety, and early functional outcomes of totally<br/>\nlaparoscopic anterior pelvic exenteration with orthotopic neobladder reconstruction using a<br/>\nmodified Studer pouch in a young female with muscle-invasive bladder cancer, as a<br/>\nminimally invasive alternative to open or robotic approaches.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 35-year-old female with muscle-invasive carcinoma of the urinary bladder underwent<br/>\ntotally laparoscopic anterior pelvic exenteration with orthotopic neobladder reconstruction. All<br/>\nsteps — bowel isolation, pouch configuration, urethroileal anastomosis, and ureteroileal<br/>\nimplantation — were performed entirely intracorporeally using a modified Studer technique,<br/>\nwithout conversion to open or robotic assistance. Perioperative parameters recorded<br/>\nincluded operative time, estimated blood loss, drain output, time to ambulation and oral<br/>\nintake, hospital stay, and early postoperative complications.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nTotal operative time was 8 hours, with estimated blood loss of 200 ml. The patient was<br/>\nambulated by postoperative day 2 and resumed oral intake by day 5. Drain output remained<br/>\nelevated for the first 5 days before tapering. No major intraoperative or postoperative<br/>\ncomplications occurred, and the patient was discharged on postoperative day 9. The entirely<br/>\nlaparoscopic approach allowed completion of a technically demanding reconstruction without<br/>\nthe need for an assisting robotic platform.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nTotally laparoscopic anterior pelvic exenteration with orthotopic neobladder using a modified<br/>\nStuder pouch is technically feasible in selected patients who are young and can be avoided<br/>\nwith ureterostomy. Despite the extended operative time, it offers the benefits of minimally<br/>\ninvasive surgery, including reduced blood loss, early ambulation, reduced postoperative<br/>\npain, and satisfactory functional recovery and outcomes. This case supports the growing role<br/>\nof advanced laparoscopy in complex urological oncologic reconstructions particularly<br/>\nrelevant in settings where robotic platforms are inaccessible.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-94\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 94</p><h3>From Donor to Recipient: A Step-by-Step Video of Robot-Assisted Living Donor Kidney Transplantation with Regional Hypothermia Centre's Initial Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rahul Anand</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS JODHPUR</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nTo demonstrate the standardized operative technique and report the initial institutional outcomes of<br/>\nrobot-assisted kidney transplantation (RAKT) with regional hypothermia in a living-donor setting,<br/>\nevaluating its feasibility as a minimally invasive alternative to open transplantation.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA 38-year-old male with end-stage renal disease secondary to chronic glomerulonephritis, on<br/>\nmaintenance hemodialysis for 2 years, with a functioning left radio-cephalic AV fistula and prior<br/>\nbilateral femoral catheterization, underwent RAKT using a kidney from his 61-year-old mother<br/>\n(living-related donor). The donor kidney was procured via laparoscopic donor nephrectomy.<br/>\nStandardized ex-vivo bench preparation — arterial spatulation, venous trimming, and ureteric<br/>\nhandling — preceded robotic implantation. Vascular anastomoses were performed to the right external<br/>\niliac vessels under regional hypothermia. Outcome measures included operative time, ischemia<br/>\ntimes, graft function (serum creatinine), and post-operative recovery milestones.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nTotal operative time was 200 minutes, with warm ischemia time of 3 minutes and cold ischemia<br/>\ntime of 118 minutes, both within institutionally acceptable limits. The post-operative course was<br/>\nuneventful. Drain was removed on POD 8 after confirming low drain-fluid creatinine, urethral<br/>\ncatheter and skin sutures on POD 10, and DJ stent on POD 14. Serum creatinine at POD 10 was 1.30<br/>\nmg/dL, indicating excellent early graft function without vascular, urological, or wound-related<br/>\ncomplications.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nThis initial institutional experience confirms that RAKT with regional hypothermia is a safe,<br/>\nreproducible technique in a carefully selected recipient, achieving excellent early graft function while<br/>\npreserving the graft and wound-related advantages of minimally invasive surgery. This video<br/>\nprovides a stepwise, reproducible operative roadmap — from bench preparation to vascular and<br/>\nureteric anastomosis — for centers initiating robotic renal transplant programmes, and supports wider adoption of RAKT as a viable evolution in transplant urology.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-95\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 95</p><h3>Laparoscopic-Assisted Ureteroplasty Using Monti Ileal Replacement for long segment Upper Ureteric Stricture: A Modified Approach to Reduce Morbidity.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr M Amir, Dr N Srivastava, Dr A Bajpai, Dr A Kumar, Dr R Lohani, Dr A Sengar, Dr S Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PRECISION UROLOGY HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nLong ureteral defects exceeding the reach of bladder flaps necessitate tissue replacement using reconfigured intestinal segments. The Monti ileal ureter replacement represents an effective reconstructive option with reduced complication rates in selected patients.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nWe performed a modified laparoscopic-assisted approach in a 46-year-old male with iatrogenic long segment left upper ureteric stricture. The procedure utilized transperitoneal laparoscopy with three ports and a 5 cm Pfannenstiel incision for extracorporeal Monti preparation. Following adhesiolysis and excision of the strictured segment in lateral position, an 8 cm ureteral defect was identified. A 2-3 cm ileal segment was harvested 15-20 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve with mesentery preservation. The segment was detubularized along its antimesenteric border and retubularized transversely over a catheter according to Monti principles, creating a narrow-Caliber tube of increased length. Intracorporeal anastomosis to proximal and distal ureter was performed using 4-0 Vicryl sutures.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe 8 cm defect reconstruction was completed in 160 minutes. Postoperative recovery included oral intake on day 4, minimal analgesia requirement (250 mg diclofenac), 6-day hospital stay, and drain removal on day 6. Double-J stent remained for 4 weeks. Diagnostic ureteroscopy confirmed ureteric patency, with imaging at 3 months demonstrating successful drainage. Total scar length measured 6 cm.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic-assisted Monti ureteroplasty is technically feasible, offering reduced postoperative morbidity, improved cosmesis, faster recovery, and excellent functional outcomes for selected upper ureteric strictures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-96\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 96</p><h3>Laparoscopic Pyelo-vesicostomy as salvage procedure for Pelvi-ureteric Junction Obstruction in Pelvic Kidney: PUH Experience</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr M Amir, Dr N Srivastava, Dr A Bajpai, Dr A Kumar, Dr R Lohani, Dr A Sengar, Dr S Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PRECISION UROLOGY HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nPelvic kidney, a congenital renal ectopia resulting from arrested embryological ascent, presents unique surgical challenges when complicated by pelvi-ureteric junction obstruction (PUJO). Aberrant vasculature, distorted pelvicalyceal anatomy, and restricted pelvic working space frequently preclude conventional pyeloplasty.</p><p>Methods:<br/>\nWe present two cases of symptomatic secondary PUJO in pelvic kidneys managed via laparoscopic pyelo-vesicostomy. Both patients (aged 29 and 44 years) demonstrated flank pain, palpable lower abdominal masses, and recurrent urinary tract infections. Preoperative imaging revealed severe hydronephrosis secondary to PUJO, confirmed by obstructed drainage curves on diuretic renography. The surgical technique involved direct anastomosis of the renal pelvis to the bladder dome, bypassing the obstructed pelvi-ureteric segment. Following mobilization of the dilated renal pelvis and excision of the pathological PUJ segment, tension-free mucosa-to-mucosa anastomosis was performed between the dependent pelvic portion and bladder dome. Internal drainage was maintained with double-J stenting.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nBoth procedures utilized three-port laparoscopy (one 10-mm camera port, two 5-mm working ports). Operative times were 110 and 130 minutes, with hemoglobin decreases of 0.9 and 0.5 g/dL. Hospital stays were 3 and 4 days, with oral intake resumed at 48 and 72 hours postoperatively. Stents were removed at 3 weeks. Postoperative courses were uncomplicated, with complete symptom resolution. Follow-up imaging demonstrated effective decompression of the collecting system, improved drainage patterns, and preserved differential renal function.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic pyelo-vesicostomy constitutes a definitive reconstructive alternative for PUJO in pelvic kidneys when conventional pyeloplasty is contraindicated by anatomical constraints, offering excellent functional outcomes without complex ureteric reconstruction.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-97\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 97</p><h3>Prone Position Modified Split-Leg Endoscopic Combined Intrarenal Surgery (ECIRS) for Multiple Calyceal Calculi in a Solitary Kidney with Chronic Kidney Disease: A Representative Case Report from PUH.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr M Amir, Dr N Srivastava, Dr A Bajpai, Dr A Kumar, Dr R Lohani, Dr A Sengar, Dr S Mishra</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">PRECISION UROLOGY HOSPITAL</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim:<br/>\nTo report a representative case of Prone Endoscopic combined intrarenal surgery(ECIRS) performed in the modified prone split-leg position for a patient with a solitary kidney, Chronic kidney disease(CKD), and multiple calyceal calculi, and to review it against our ongoing prone ECIRS experience and the supporting literature.</p><p>Material and Methods:<br/>\nA 35-year-old woman (BMI 28 kg/m²) with a left solitary functioning kidney (right atrophic kidney) presented with fever and reduced appetite. She had prior PCNL elsewhere with residual stones and an indwelling DJ stent. Serum creatinine was 3.5 mg/dL (eGFR 30 mL/min), and NCCT KUB showed three residual calyceal stones with total stone bulk of 3.5 cm. After two weeks of stent drainage for optimization, she underwent ECIRS in the modified prone split-leg position via a single 20-Fr percutaneous tract, with a flexible ureteroscope passed retrogradely over a 9/11-Fr access sheath.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nAll three stones were cleared, including laser fragmentation of a large middle calyceal calculus. Operative time was 150 minutes, hemoglobin drop was 1 g/dL, and no intraoperative complications occurred. The nephrostomy was removed on day 2, and the patient was discharged on day 3. The DJ stent was removed at three weeks; NCCT at one month confirmed complete stone clearance, with renal function improved to a creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL (eGFR 48 mL/min).</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nSingle-tract prone ECIRS in the modified prone split-leg position achieved complete clearance with minimal blood loss, short hospitalization, and measurable renal functional recovery in a solitary, CKD-affected kidney, without multiple tracts or an auxiliary procedure. This case supports prone ECIRS as a feasible, low-morbidity option for complex calyceal stones in functionally vulnerable kidneys.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-98\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 98</p><h3>A Rare Guest in the Adrenal Neighbourhood: Robotic Management of a Retroperitoneal Mature Cystic Teratoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Akash, Dr Nipun, Dr Waheedu</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max super speciality hospital, Shalimar Bhag</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>A 33-year-old woman, presented with complaints of persistent left-sided abdominal pain. A contrast-<br/>\nenhanced CT scan of the whole abdomen demonstrated a large, well-circumscribed suprarenal mass<br/>\non the left side, measuring approximately 8.5 × 9.5 × 7.1 cm. The lesion showed heterogeneous<br/>\nattenuation with fat density, soft-tissue components, and internal calcifications, features highly<br/>\nsuggestive of a retroperitoneal teratoma. The mass was displacing the adrenal gland superiorly and<br/>\nwas closely abutting the left kidney, renal vessels, pancreas, and spleen, although major fat planes<br/>\nwere maintained. Her initial laboratory evaluation revealed a raised LDH and a mildly elevated CA-<br/>\n125, while AFP and β-hCG levels were within normal limits, helping narrow the differential diagnosis<br/>\naway from germ-cell malignancies.<br/>\nGiven the size of the lesion and its proximity to vital structures, she was planned for robot-assisted<br/>\nexcision of the retroperitoneal suprarenal mass. On<br/>\n27th November 2025, she underwent the procedure. Intraoperatively, a large 9.5 × 8.5 cm mass was<br/>\nseen above the left kidney within the gerota’s fascia, with the left adrenal gland extremely thinned<br/>\nout and draped over the superior surface. A single large tumor feeding vein was identified, clipped,<br/>\ntransfixed, and divided. The mass was found to be densely adherent to the spleen and left renal<br/>\nvessels, accompanied by significant neovascularization. Decompression of the mass was performed,<br/>\nand aspiration yielded cystic fluid containing hair and calcified material, further confirming the<br/>\ndiagnosis of teratoma. Careful hilar and superior-pole dissection allowed complete separation of the<br/>\ntumor from surrounding structures, and the specimen was removed intact in a retrieval bag.<br/>\nHemostasis was secured and a drain was placed.<br/>\nHer post-operative course was smooth and uneventful, with no complications. She recovered well<br/>\nand was discharged in a stable condition.<br/>\nHistopathology confirmed a mature teratoma, correlating with the pre-operative imaging and intraoperative findings.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-99\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 99</p><h3>Robot-Assisted Bilateral Modified Leadbetter-Politano Ureteric Reimplantation: Step by step surgical technique</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Siddharth Garg, Dr Gautam Ram Choudhary</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Jodhpur</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Title: Robot-Assisted Bilateral Modified Leadbetter-Politano Ureteric Reimplantation: Step by step surgical technique<br/>\nObjective: Modified Leadbetter-Politano Ureteric Reimplantation is a well-established surgical technique for the management of Vesicoureteral Reflux (VUR). Its adoption declined with the advent of laparoscopic surgery because of complexity of the procedure. With the increasingly availability of robotic surgical system at high volume centres, there has been renewed interest in performing complex reconstruction in a minimally invasive way. In this context, we aim to present the stepwise technique of this procedure.<br/>\nCases Details: 1. Twenty-one-year-old male with bilateral HDUN with Large capacity bladder with dysfunctional voiding who underwent robot assisted tapering of ureters with Modified Leadbetter Politano bilateral Ureteric Re-implantation with Reduction Cystoplasty.<br/>\n2. Seven-year-old male child with CKD and bilateral Grade 5 VUR with history of prior failed Laparoscopic Bilateral Ureteric Reimplantation by Modified Lich-Gregoir technique, underwent bilateral robot assisted Modified Leadbetter-Politano Ureteric Re-implantation.<br/>\nSurgical Technique: Under general anesthesia, the patients were positioned in Trendelenburg position and transperitoneal robotic approach was used. For adult patient, first bladder was reduced by excising dome of the bladder, then bilateral ureters were transacted as low as possible and tapered till just above the iliac vessels. In child ureters were entering in anterior part of the bladder and were transacted as low as possible and bladder was opened transversely. In both cases Bilateral Modified Lead Better Politano Ureteric Re-implantation done, submucosal tunnel was created by injecting adrenaline saline.<br/>\nResults &amp; Conclusion: This video revisits the steps of this complex reconstructive procedure using da Vinci Xi robotic system. The technique is feasible and reproducible, with surgical outcomes comparable to those achieved with the conventional open approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-100\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 100</p><h3>Robotic Surgery in a 1.5-Year-Old: Simultaneous Right Ureteric Reimplantation and Left Nephrectomy — Technical Challenges and Solutions</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">DR SAURABH JOSHI</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AAKASH HOSPITAL, DWARKA ,DELHI</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>To evaluate the feasibility of simultaneous robotic right ureteric reimplantation and left nephrectomy in a 1.5-year-old child, focusing on port placement, robotic ergonomics, instrument clashing, and intracorporeal suturing in a very small abdominal cavity.</p><p>Methods</p><p>A 1.5-year-old child requiring left nephrectomy and right ureteric reimplantation underwent simultaneous robotic surgery. Preoperative planning included individualized port placement according to the child’s abdominal dimensions and the location of both operative targets. The robotic ports were positioned to achieve adequate triangulation while minimizing external and internal arm collisions. The surgical video demonstrates key steps of renal dissection, ureteric mobilization, ureteric reimplantation, nephrectomy, and intracorporeal suturing. Particular attention was given to technical modifications required because of the patient’s young age and limited working space.</p><p>Results</p><p>Both procedures were successfully completed using a single robotic setup. The major challenge was establishing optimal port geometry in a very small abdomen while maintaining adequate working distance and instrument range. Careful port placement allowed satisfactory exposure of both renal and pelvic operative fields and minimized robotic arm interference. Precise robotic movements and advanced intracorporeal suturing skills were essential for ureteric reimplantation. The case demonstrates that complex bilateral procedures can be performed without compromising visualization or surgical precision when the robotic configuration is appropriately adapted to the patient.</p><p>Conclusions</p><p>Simultaneous robotic right ureteric reimplantation with left nephrectomy is technically feasible in a 1.5-year-old child in experienced hands. Individualized port placement is the key technical consideration in pediatric robotic surgery, particularly in infants and toddlers with restricted abdominal space. Proper robotic ergonomics, careful dissection, precise tissue handling, and intracorporeal suturing skills are essential for successful completion of complex procedures. This video presentation highlights practical strategies for overcoming port-placement challenges and instrument clashing and demonstrates the potential of robotic surgery for advanced minimally invasive pediatric urological procedures.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-101\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 101</p><h3>Simultaneous Bilateral Video Endoscopic Inguinal Lymphadenectomy (VEIL) for Carcinoma Penis</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Vanji Nathan Subramani, Saurabh Meshram, Ankit Singh, Toshib G A, Kevin Arulraj, Manoj Kumar, Amlesh Seth</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>**Introduction:**<br/>\nVideo endoscopic inguinal lymphadenectomy (VEIL) is a minimally invasive alternative to open inguinal lymphadenectomy for penile cancer, with the potential to reduce wound morbidity while maintaining oncological efficacy. Near-infrared (NIR) fluorescence imaging with indocyanine green (ICG) enables real-time lymphatic mapping and sentinel node identification. This study evaluates the feasibility, safety, and perioperative outcomes of simultaneous bilateral VEIL (sB-VEIL) under ICG-guided NIR fluorescence.</p><p>**Materials and Methods:**<br/>\nFour patients requiring bilateral inguinal lymphadenectomy underwent sB-VEIL. Approximately 2 mL of ICG was injected into the prepuce, peritumoral region, or amputated penile base before lymphadenectomy. Bilateral thigh working spaces were created using CO₂ insufflation through three trocars on each side. Modified inguinal lymphadenectomy was performed according to standard anatomical landmarks, with NIR fluorescence used for real-time identification of lymphatic channels and lymph nodes.</p><p>**Results:**<br/>\nEight VEIL procedures were successfully completed in four patients without conversion to open surgery or intraoperative complications. The oncological dissection template was maintained in all cases. A mean of five lymph nodes per side were retrieved, with histologically positive nodes identified in two patients. No postoperative wound necrosis, lymphocele, or seroma was observed. All patients had uneventful recovery and short hospital stays. ICG-NIR guidance facilitated identification of lymphatic channels and sentinel nodes. Simultaneous bilateral surgery enabled treatment of both groins under a single anaesthetic, potentially reducing operative time and costs compared with staged or robotic approaches. At 3-month follow-up, all patients remained disease-free without local or regional recurrence.</p><p>**Conclusion:**<br/>\nSimultaneous bilateral VEIL under ICG-guided NIR fluorescence is a feasible, safe, and efficient approach for bilateral inguinal lymphadenectomy in penile cancer. It combines the advantages of minimally invasive surgery with enhanced lymphatic visualisation and may represent a cost-effective alternative to robotic approaches.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-102\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 102</p><h3>ICG Guided Simultaneous Bilateral Laparoscopic Video Endoscopic Inguinal Lymphadenectomy (VIEL) for Penile Cancer</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Toshib G A, Dr Saurabh Meshram, Dr Nitish Aggarwal, Dr Vaanji Nathan, Dr Manoj Kumar</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims<br/>\nThis study evaluates the feasibility, safety, and outcomes of simultaneous bilateral VEIL (sB‑VEIL) performed under ICG‑guided NIR fluorescence imaging, highlighting its efficiency and potential as a cost‑effective alternative to robotic approaches. The integration of near‑infrared (NIR) fluorescence imaging with Indocyanine Green (ICG) enhances intraoperative visualization through real‑time lymphatic mapping and sentinel node identification.</p><p>Materials and Methods<br/>\nFour patients with indications for bilateral inguinal lymphadenectomy underwent sB‑VEIL. About 2 ml of ICG dye was injected into the prepuce, peritumoral region, or amputated penile base before the start of the lymphadenectomy to aid fluorescence mapping. The procedure followed anatomical landmarks: adductor longus medially, sartorius laterally, and the inguinal ligament superiorly. Bilateral thigh pockets were developed simultaneously, and modified bilateral inguinal lymphadenectomy was completed using NIR fluorescence for real‑time lymphatic mapping and node identification.</p><p>Results<br/>\nEight VEIL procedures were successfully completed in four patients without conversion to open surgery. In all cases, oncological templates were maintained, and no intraoperative complications occurred. The mean number of nodes retrieved was five per side, with two patients demonstrating histologically positive nodes. No postoperative wound necrosis, lymphocele, or seroma formation was observed, and recovery was uneventful with short hospital stays. NIR guidance improved intraoperative visualization, enabling more precise identification of sentinel nodes and lymphatic channels. The approach allowed both groins to be operated on during the same anesthesia, reducing operative time and anesthetic exposure compared to staged or robotic procedures. At a three‑month follow‑up, all patients remained disease‑free with no local or regional recurrences.</p><p>Conclusion<br/>\nSimultaneous bilateral VEIL under ICG‑NIR guidance is safe, feasible, and time‑efficient. It combines the benefits of minimally invasive surgery with improved lymphatic visualisation while reducing operative time and cost compared to robotic approaches.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-103\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 103</p><h3>Spontanous Perinephric Hematoma</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sonu Sharma,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Fortis Hospital, Shalimar Bagh, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Perinephric hematomas are defined by haemorrhage originating from the kidney parenchyma into the subcapsular and perirenal spaces. Spontaneous perinephric hematomas are rare and usually occur due to an underlying renal mass, vascular abnormality, coagulation/inflammatory disorder. Classic presentation for spontaneous perinephric hematoma includes acute flank pain, haemorrhagic shock, and detection of a renal mass (Lenk’s triad).<br/>\nCase: 46 year female, complaint of acute left flank pain for 3 days, vomiting and loose stools. She had raised blood pressure and excruciating pain which was managed with NTG infusion and fentanyl infusion, blood transfusion. CT revealed a large subcapsular collection around kidney with entire cortex showing suboptimal enhancement with multiple non enhancing areas. No significant excretion seen on delayed phase. She underwent Laparoscopic left nephrectomy. Post operative period was uneventful.<br/>\nConclusion: Ultrasound is useful for quick identification, but CT is more sensitive and can give information about the underlying aetiology. Perinephric or subcapsular hematoma management involves haemorrhagic shock resuscitation with blood transfusions, vasoactive medications, and cardiopulmonary monitoring. Stable patients can be monitored with conservative treatment. Anticoagulants should be stopped during acute bleeding. Unstable patients in haemorrhagic shock require definitive treatment by intervention radiologist angioembolisation. If patients also have a renal mass or no intervenable target for IR, then surgical intervention with a partial or completer nephrectomy may be necessary. In our case we found no cause for hematoma hence this was an unusual case of perinephric hematoma.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-104\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 104</p><h3>Urethral diverticulum with calculi in an adult: Sequale of hypospiadias repair</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sonu Sharma,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">FORTIS HOSPITAL, SHALIMAR BAGH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: A urethral diverticulum (UD) represents a rare pathological condition within the lower urinary tract, often characterized by elevated voiding pressure, fibrosis and necrosis, which can result from stenosis in the urethra or urethral meatus. Infections and injuries arising from medical procedures are usually the predisposing factors. Surgical treatment of hypospadias represents a complex challenge in pediatric urology. Well-documented long-term complications include urethrocutaneous fistula, stricture formation, development of urethral diverticula, urethral hair growth occurring at a rate ranging between 5 and 15 %. Hypospadias surgery is the most common cause of an acquired diverticulum in children but not in adult males.<br/>\nCase: 34 year old male, came with discharge from scrotum with swelling near testis since. L/E: hard swelling in left scrotum, discharge from scrotal skin above the hard swelling. Both testis palpable, micropenis.<br/>\nH/O urethroplasty with chordee release with urethral tube made from dorsal skin of penis in 1991.<br/>\nReceived testosterone for a short duration, Urethral calibration in childhood. We did scrotal exploration, removal of calculi and urethroplasty. Post operative period was uneventful and catheter was removed after 3 weeks with patient voiding well.<br/>\nConclusion: Urethral diverticula can result in stone formation, incomplete voiding, infection, postvoid dribbling, haematuria and other complications. Lateral or oblique views obtained during a retrograde urethrogram can effectively reveal the abnormality, with cystoscopy helping as the confirmatory diagnostic procedure. The management of male urethral diverticulum varies depending on factors such as its size, anatomical location, and presenting symptoms. Open surgery is recommended for cases involving large diverticula, significant stone burdens, or complex surgical histories. If there are no other associated complications, a straightforward partial excision followed by a multilayer closure is typically satisfactory.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-105\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 105</p><h3>Robotic Partial Nephrectomy for Hilar Tumour</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Sonu Sharma,</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">FORTIS HOSPITAL, SHALIMAR BAGH</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction: Renal hilar tumours pose significant challenges for partial nephrectomy. Being closely related to the hilar vasculature and perinephric fat increases the complexity of the resection and likelihood of complications. Surgical planning begins with high quality pre-operative imaging to determine the optimal access, exact location and depth of the tumour, and document the vascular anatomy. Robotic-assisted partial nephrectomy has become an established approach for nephron-sparing surgery.<br/>\nCase: 52/M, came with incidentally detected renal mass. No co morbidities<br/>\nUSG: 28*28mm well defined hetero-echoic lesion in upper pole ? Renal origin? Supra renal origin. Cortical cyst 25*20mm at upper pole. PET CT: Mildly FDG avid at least two well circumscribed, partially exophytic cortical lesion in upper pole cortex of right kidney, larger lesion appears to be suspicious of primary neoplastic renal lesion, smaller cyst appears equivocal. We did a Robotic Partial Nephrectomy.<br/>\nConclusion: Robotic-assisted partial nephrectomy has superseded the laparoscopic approach in recent years with increased access. Robotic assistance offers enhanced visualization and dexterity to tackle the complexities of these cases which translates to improved perioperative outcomes compared to a standard laparoscopic approach.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-106\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 106</p><h3>Interim results of OUTCOMES OF VENTRAL INLAY BUCCAL MUCOSAL GRAFT URETHROPLASTY VS DORSAL ONLAY BUCCAL MUCOSAL GRAFT URETHROPLASTY IN FEMALE URETHRAL STRICTURE DISEASE: A RANDOMISED CONTROL TRIAL</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr. Kawaljit Singh Kaura, Dr. Suresh Kumar Goyal, Dr. Abhay S Gaur, Dr. Tejas Chaudhari</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">AIIMS, Bathinda</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction and Objectives<br/>\nFemale urethral stricture disease (USD) is a rare condition that significantly impacts urinary function and quality of life. We aim to present the results of a randomized controlled trial comparing two Buccal Mucosal Graft Urethroplasty techniques—Ventral Inlay (VI) and Dorsal-Onlay (DO)—to evaluate their efficacy and safety.<br/>\nMethods<br/>\nA total of 26 female patients with USD between January 2025 and January 2026 were enrolled. Participants were randomly assigned to undergo either VI-BMGU or DO-BMGU. Preoperative evaluation included American Urological Association (AUA) symptom scores, urine maximum flow rate (Qmax), post-void residual urine volume (PVR) assessed ultrasonographically. USD was confirmed using a 12 Fr cystoscope. Postoperative assessments at three months included AUA symptom scores, uroflowmetry, and PVR measurements.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nThe mean age of the patients was 46.4 years with mean stricture of 2.5 cm. Pre-operative median Q-max was 5 ml/sec, median AUA score of 15, median PVR at 121 cc. At the three-month follow-up, the median Qmax was 20 ml/sec in VIBMGU group and 21 ml/sec in DOBMGU group, median AUA score of 3, median PVR of 25 cc equal for both groups(p&lt;0.05). No patients experienced recurrence during the follow-up. Compared to DO-BMGU, VI-BMGU demonstrated additional advantages, including shorter operative time, reduced (p&lt;0.05)blood loss, and decreased postoperative pain, vaginal sparing.<br/>\nConclusions<br/>\nBoth VI-BMGU and DO-BMGU are effective surgical techniques for female USD, yielding comparable improvements in urinary function and symptom relief. However, VI-BMGU offers the added benefits of a simpler surgical approach, reduced operative time, lesser blood loss, and less postoperative discomfort while preserving vaginal integrity</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-107\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 107</p><h3>Video (Robotic) RARP</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Satyendra Singh</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Apollo hospitals , Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>RARP</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-108\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 108</p><h3>Transperitoneal Laparoscopic Nephrectomy for pyonephrotic /XGPN kidneys – Surgical technique and nuances in navigating inflammatory adhesions and anatomical distortion – Video presentation.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Rahul Gupta , Dr Ajay Anand</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">GMC Jammu</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nLaparoscopic nephrectomy for a pyonephrotic non-functioning kidney is a technically challenging but feasible option. It offers benefits like less pain and faster recovery. Still, it needs experienced laparoscopic surgeons to perform the procedure due to severe inflammation and scar tissue, which results in ill-defined planes and distorted anatomy, thus making dissection difficult.<br/>\nWe demonstrate the technique and nuances in cases of pyonephrotic non-functioning kidneys managed laparoscopically.</p><p>Material and methods:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis was done after IRB clearance for all laparoscopic simple nephrectomies at GMC Jammu. The study period ranged from 2014 -2024. A total of 1000 nephrectomies were recorded, and the data were analysed. Of these 425 (44.5%) renal units were removed for calculus pyonephrosis. In 400 renal units (95%), preplaced PCN was in situ, and the remaining were diagnosed intraoperatively. The data recoded included the demographic profile, any history of previous surgery, intraoperative parameters (operative time in minutes, injury to adjoining organs, need to remove adjoining organs like spleen, need for colostomy, and others). All complications were recorded using Clavein dindo score.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nOf the 425 patients' data included in the study, the majority were males, accounting for 70% (300). The left side accounted for 80% (340). 65 patients (15%) needed conversion to open in view of either non-progression or intraoperative complication (bleeding).150 (35%) patients gave a history of previous renal intervention, either endoscopy (85) or open surgery (65) in the past. Blood transfusion was needed in 225 (60%) patients. The average hospital stay was 5 days, with postoperative wound infection being the commonest complication. There was no mortality recorded.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic nephrectomy for calculus pyonephorosis is feasible in the hands of an experienced laparoscopic surgeon with favourable outcomes. One should be prepared for complications and keep a low threshold for conversion in case of non-progression intraoperatively.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-109\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 109</p><h3>“From Challenge to Precision: 3D Laparoscopic Resection of a Giant Suprarenal Teratoma”</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">RAHUL GUPTA</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">GMC JAMMU</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>AIM:<br/>\nExtra-gonadal teratomas account for approximately 15% of all teratomas, with retroperitoneal lesions being rare and adrenal teratomas exceedingly uncommon. Due to their asymptomatic nature and large size at presentation, they are traditionally managed with open surgery. We aim to demonstrate the feasibility and technical nuances of 3D laparoscopic resection of a large right suprarenal teratoma.</p><p>Materials and Methods:<br/>\nA 45-year-old female was incidentally found to have a 10 × 8 cm cystic, calcified right suprarenal mass during evaluation for dyspepsia and cholelithiasis. She underwent laparoscopic cholecystectomy and presented to us after 6 weeks for definitive management. She was hypertensive, with no evidence of functional adrenal pathology. A transperitoneal 3D laparoscopic approach was planned. The video demonstrates patient positioning, port placement, adhesiolysis at the porta hepatis, identification and safeguarding of the inferior vena cava (IVC), and meticulous retroperitoneal dissection. Special emphasis is placed on tumor mobilization from the right renal hilum and IVC using the roll-over technique, and careful separation from the liver undersurface.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nThe procedure was completed successfully with a total ORT of 160minutes, using a 3D laparoscopic approach without intraoperative complications. Despite the tumour size and proximity to vital structures, complete excision was achieved with minimal blood loss (70cc) and no requirement for transfusion. The postoperative course was uneventful, and the patient recovered well ON 3RD Post operative day.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\n3D laparoscopic resection of large suprarenal teratomas is feasible and safe in experienced hands. Careful preoperative planning, advanced laparoscopic skills, and precise retroperitoneal dissection are essential to achieve optimal outcomes with minimal morbidity, even in complex anatomical scenarios.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-110\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 110</p><h3>Transperitoneal Laparoscopic Simple Nephrectomy for pyonephrotic /XGPN kidneys – Surgical technique and nuances in navigating inflammatory adhesions and anatomical distortion – Video presentation.</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">RAHUL GUPTA ,HASEEB,AAJAY ANAND</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Introduction:<br/>\nLaparoscopic nephrectomy for a pyonephrotic non-functioning kidney is a technically challenging but feasible option. It offers benefits like less pain and faster recovery. Still, it needs experienced laparoscopic surgeons to perform the procedure due to severe inflammation and scar tissue, which results in ill-defined planes and distorted anatomy, thus making dissection difficult.<br/>\nWe demonstrate the technique and nuances in cases of pyonephrotic non-functioning kidneys managed laparoscopically.</p><p>Material and methods:<br/>\nA retrospective analysis was done after IRB clearance for all laparoscopic simple nephrectomies at GMC Jammu. The study period ranged from 2014 -2024. A total of 1000 nephrectomies were recorded, and the data were analysed. Of these 425 (44.5%) renal units were removed for calculus pyonephrosis. In 400 renal units (95%), preplaced PCN was in situ, and the remaining were diagnosed intraoperatively. The data recoded included the demographic profile, any history of previous surgery, intraoperative parameters (operative time in minutes, injury to adjoining organs, need to remove adjoining organs like spleen, need for colostomy, and others). All complications were recorded using Clavein dindo score.<br/>\nResults:<br/>\nOf the 425 patients' data included in the study, the majority were males, accounting for 70% (300). The left side accounted for 80% (340). 65 patients (15%) needed conversion to open in view of either non-progression or intraoperative complication (bleeding).150 (35%) patients gave a history of previous renal intervention, either endoscopy (85) or open surgery (65) in the past. Blood transfusion was needed in 225 (60%) patients. The average hospital stay was 5 days, with postoperative wound infection being the commonest complication. There was no mortality recorded.<br/>\nConclusion:<br/>\nLaparoscopic nephrectomy for calculus pyonephorosis is feasible in the hands of an experienced laparoscopic surgeon with favourable outcomes. One should be prepared for complications and keep a low threshold for conversion in case of non-progression intraoperatively.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-111\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 111</p><h3>na</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">na</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Not provided</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>na</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-112\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 112</p><h3>Avoiding and Managing Ureteric Injury During Robot-Assisted Radical Prostatectomy: A Video-Based Case Report</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shailesh c sahay, Dr Pawan Kesarwani, Dr Shivam Chaudhary, Dr Prashant Sevach</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim and Objective<br/>\nTo present a rare case of ureteric injury during robot-assisted radical prostatectomy (RARP), highlight the factors leading to the injury, and demonstrate its successful robotic management.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods<br/>\nA 65-year-old gentleman presented with lower urinary tract symptoms and an elevated serum PSA of 15.4 ng/mL. Prostate biopsy revealed adenocarcinoma with a Gleason score of 4+3. Multiparametric MRI and PSMA PET imaging confirmed localized prostate cancer.<br/>\nStandard robotic port placement was performed, and posterior dissection was initiated. The procedure was started by a newly trained surgeon. During left vas deferens and seminal vesicle dissection, bleeding obscured the operative field. The procedure was subsequently taken over by a senior consultant, who reassessed the anatomy. It was identified that the left ureter had been mistakenly identified as the vas deferens/seminal vesicle and had been transacted with electrocautery approximately 5–6 cm proximal to the ureterovesical junction.<br/>\nThe left seminal vesicle was then dissected correctly. Both proximal and distal ureteric ends were mobilized, and a tension-free end-to-end ureteroureterostomy was performed robotically over a 6 Fr double-J (DJ) stent. The robot-assisted radical prostatectomy was subsequently completed in the standard fashion.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nThe postoperative recovery was uneventful. The urethral catheter was removed after 3 weeks, and the DJ stent was removed at 6 weeks. Retrograde pyelography demonstrated a patent ureter with a well-healed anastomotic site and no evidence of leakage or stricture.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nUreteric injury during robot-assisted radical prostatectomy is an exceptionally rare complication. Prompt recognition of the injury, accurate identification of the anatomy, and immediate robotic reconstruction are essential for achieving excellent functional outcomes while preventing long-term morbidity. This case highlights the importance of meticulous anatomical dissection, especially during the learning curve of robotic pelvic surgery.<br/>\nYou tube link-<br/>\nhttps://youtu.be/Ej1eYUeeoN4</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-113\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 113</p><h3>Role of hemostatic patch (Veriset) in Robotic assisted Partial Nephrectomy- Can it obviate the need for renorraphy?</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shailesh C Sahay, Dr Pawan Kesarwani, Dr Shivam Chaudhary, Dr Prashant Sevach</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Patparganj, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Title- Role of hemostatic patch (Veriset) in Robotic assisted Partial Nephrectomy- Can it obviate the need for renorraphy?<br/>\nAim and objective<br/>\nHemostatic patch (Veriset) serves as an adjunct during partial nephrectomy to achieve sutureless closure of the renal parenchyma. It facilitates an \"off-clamp\" or minimal ischemia approach, reducing both warm ischemia time (WIT) and surgical complications. Here, we are demonstrating a case of Robotic assisted partial nephrectomy where veriset was used for tumor bed hemostasis without any need for renorraphy.<br/>\nMaterial and methods<br/>\nThis patient a 54 years old gentleman presented with an asymptomatic mass of 3.5 cm in midpole of left kidney. Tumor was located anteriorly just near the bifurcation of renal vein. Patient was positioned in left flank position and standard robotic port placements were done. Left renal artery and veins were dissected and clamped with bulldog clamps. Tumor was enucleated and one small venous branch ligated with suture. Veriset was placed over the tumor bed and clamps removed. There was no bleeding seen afterwards so no need for renorraphy was observed. Postoperative period was uneventful.<br/>\nResult<br/>\nVeriset application obviated the need for renorraphy in this case which reduced the warm ischemia time to 12 minutes and also saved the renal nephrons loss due to suturing. Patient recovered well without any significant bleeding.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nThe sutureless technique with veriset application is best reserved for tumors of low-to-moderate complexity. Careful case selection is the key for successful outcome.</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-114\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 114</p><h3>The \"Mickey Mouse\" Bladder: Robotic-Assisted Diverticulectomy for a Rare Case of Congenital Multiple Urinary Bladder Diverticula</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shailesh C Sahay, Dr Pawan Kesarwani, Dr Shivam Chaudhary, Dr Prashant Sevach</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Patparganj, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aim<br/>\nUrinary bladder (UB) diverticula are commonly associated with bladder outlet obstruction, whereas congenital bladder diverticula are rare. We present the surgical management of a rare case of congenital multiple urinary bladder diverticula using a robotic-assisted approach, accompanied by a surgical video.<br/>\nMaterials and Methods<br/>\nAn 18-year-old male presented with obstructive lower urinary tract symptoms. Ultrasonography of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder (KUB) revealed normal kidneys and prostate, with a post-void residual urine volume of 350 mL. Two large diverticula, measuring approximately 5 cm and 6 cm, were identified on the posterosuperior aspect of the urinary bladder. Micturating cystourethrogram (MCU) confirmed the presence of these large diverticula, producing a characteristic appearance resembling the \"Mickey Mouse\" face. The patient underwent robotic-assisted excision of both bladder diverticula, followed by primary bladder reconstruction.<br/>\nResults<br/>\nThe postoperative course was uneventful. The Foley catheter was removed three weeks after surgery following a cystogram, which demonstrated a watertight repair with no evidence of urinary leakage. The reconstructed bladder showed a smooth, spherical contour, and the patient experienced complete resolution of voiding symptoms.<br/>\nConclusion<br/>\nRobotic-assisted bladder diverticulectomy is a safe, feasible, and effective minimally invasive approach for the management of large congenital bladder diverticula. The enhanced dexterity, superior visualization, and ergonomic advantages of the robotic platform facilitate meticulous dissection and precise bladder reconstruction, leading to excellent functional outcomes.<br/>\nSurgical Video: https://youtu.be/1Am71gmi8mg</p></div></details></article><article class=\"sp-abstract\" id=\"video-115\"><p class=\"sp-abs-no\">Video Abstract 115</p><h3>Robotic Transmesocolic Pyeloplasty for Pediatric Pelviureteric Junction Obstruction: A Feasible and Time-Saving Approach</h3><p class=\"sp-authors\">Dr Shailesh chandra sahay, Dr Pawan Kesarwani, Dr Shivam Chaudhary</p><p class=\"sp-inst\">Max Superspeciality Hospital, Patparganj, Delhi</p><details><summary>Read abstract</summary><div class=\"sp-abs-body\"><p>Aims and objectives:<br/>\nConventional laparoscopic and robotic pyeloplasty usually require mobilization of the descending colon to expose the pelviureteric junction (PUJ). We demonstrate the technique and feasibility of robotic-assisted transmesocolic pyeloplasty in a pediatric patient.</p><p>Materials and methods</p><p>A 7-year-old boy presented with intermittent left flank pain for two years. Non-contrast CT of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder (NCCT KUB) demonstrated left PUJ obstruction. A diuretic renogram (DTPA) showed a right renal GFR of 43 mL/min with unobstructed drainage and a left renal GFR of 35 mL/min with obstructed drainage. Cystoscopy, retrograde pyelography, and left double-J stent placement were performed during the same sitting, confirming the diagnosis. The patient was positioned in the left flank position, and robotic port placement was performed in the standard fashion. The procedure was completed using the da Vinci Xi robotic system via a transmesocolic approach.</p><p>Results:<br/>\nThe transmesocolic approach provided direct access to the PUJ without the need for colonic mobilization. In pediatric patients, the relatively thin mesentery facilitates easier identification and dissection of the PUJ, reducing operative time while maintaining excellent surgical exposure and reconstruction.</p><p>Conclusion:<br/>\nRobotic-assisted transmesocolic pyeloplasty is a safe, feasible, and effective alternative to the conventional colon-reflecting approach in selected pediatric patients with PUJ obstruction. The technique offers excellent exposure, minimizes tissue dissection, and has the potential to reduce operative time</p></div></details></article>"

